window.GATE_BANK=window.GATE_BANK||{};window.GATE_BANK["compiler-design"]=[{"id": "CD-U1-Q01", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Separating lexical analysis from syntax analysis is standard compiler practice. Which of the following is NOT one of the standard reasons for the separation?", "options": ["It simplifies the overall design of the compiler.", "It allows specialised, efficient techniques for buffering and scanning the source text.", "It improves portability, because the peculiarities of the input alphabet are isolated in one place.", "It removes all ambiguity from the grammar before the parser sees it."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three classical reasons for a separate lexical phase are simplicity of design, compiler efficiency (specialised buffering and scanning) and compiler portability (the input-alphabet peculiarities are confined to the lexer).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Options (A), (B) and (C) are exactly those three reasons.</li><li>Ambiguity is a property of the <i>grammar</i>, decided by the parser; no scanner can change the set of parse trees a grammar admits.</li><li>A lexer can reject an ill-formed lexeme, but it cannot decide whether the grammar is ambiguous.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D) — removing ambiguity is not a reason for separating lexical analysis.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that the lexer \"resolves\" grammar-level conflicts. The lexer classifies characters into tokens; ambiguity of the grammar survives it untouched.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q02", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A scanner uses the maximal-munch (longest-match) rule and has token classes for identifiers, ++, + and =. For the input a+++b, which token sequence does it return?", "options": ["a  ++  +  b", "a  +  ++  b", "a  +  +  +  b", "a  +++  b"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Maximal munch: at every position the scanner takes the <i>longest</i> prefix that is a valid token; only when several rules match the same longest prefix does rule order break the tie.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Position 1: <code class=\"mm\">a</code> starts an identifier; an identifier cannot contain <code class=\"mm\">+</code>, so the token is <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</li><li>Position 2: the characters are <code class=\"mm\">+++</code>. The longest valid token here is <code class=\"mm\">++</code> (two characters), so it is returned.</li><li>Position 4: the single <code class=\"mm\">+</code> remains and is returned as <code class=\"mm\">+</code>.</li><li>Position 5: <code class=\"mm\">b</code> is an identifier.</li><li>Result: <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">++</code>, <code class=\"mm\">+</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A): <code class=\"mm\">a ++ + b</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Splitting greedily as <code class=\"mm\">+ + +</code> (option C) ignores maximal munch, and treating <code class=\"mm\">+++</code> as one token (option D) invents a token class that does not exist.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q03", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "For the source line int sum = 0; a conventional lexer inserts how many new entries into the symbol table while scanning it?", "options": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The symbol table holds information about identifiers. Keywords are recognised by the lexer as fixed token classes and are not entered as identifiers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">int</code> — a keyword: returned as the token class INT, no symbol-table entry.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">sum</code> — an identifier: one entry is created (its lexeme, type, later its scope and address).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">=</code> and <code class=\"mm\">;</code> — punctuation tokens, no entry.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">0</code> — a constant; constants are usually kept in a separate literal/constant table, not the symbol table.</li><li>Total new symbol-table entries: 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — exactly one entry, for <code class=\"mm\">sum</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the keyword <code class=\"mm\">int</code> (giving 2) or the constant <code class=\"mm\">0</code> (giving 3). Keywords are not identifiers, and constants belong to a separate table.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q04", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following decisions cannot be made by the lexical analyser alone?", "options": ["Whether a sequence of characters is a well-formed identifier of the language.", "Whether an identifier used in an expression has been declared earlier in the program.", "Whether a sequence of characters forms a valid numeric literal of the language.", "Whether a comment opened near the end of the file is still unterminated."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A lexer classifies characters into tokens using regular expressions. Anything that needs information gathered across the whole program — such as which identifiers are declared, and in which scope — is outside the regular-power of a scanner and belongs to semantic analysis with a symbol table.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Well-formedness of an identifier (A) is exactly a regular-expression test, so the lexer decides it.</li><li>A valid numeric literal (C) is again a regular language; the lexer decides it.</li><li>An unterminated comment (D) is detected while matching the comment pattern at end of file — a lexical error.</li><li>Declaration-before-use (B) needs a symbol table filled in program order and a notion of scope; a scanner cannot answer it, and a one-token lookahead certainly cannot.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — declaration checking is a semantic (parser/semantic-analyser) task, not a lexical one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that because the lexer fills a symbol table it can also check declarations. The lexer records identifiers; it does not reason about their declarations.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q05", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which regular expression denotes exactly the set of identifiers that begin with a letter and continue with letters or digits (one or more characters in all)?", "options": ["[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*", "[A-Za-z]*[A-Za-z0-9]*", "[A-Za-z0-9]+", "[A-Za-z][A-Za-z]*"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The first character must come from the letter class and the remaining characters from the letter-or-digit class, with zero or more remaining characters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) forces one letter, then any number of letters or digits — exactly the required language.</li><li>(B) lets the first part match the empty string, so the digit part can supply a leading digit (<code class=\"mm\">9x</code> is matched); it also matches the empty string.</li><li>(C) allows an identifier to start with a digit (<code class=\"mm\">1abc</code>).</li><li>(D) forbids digits anywhere after the first character.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (B) is the classic trap: writing two stars in sequence does not impose a constraint on the first character, because the first star may match nothing.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q06", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "How many states does the minimal DFA accepting the language denoted by (a|b)*abb have?", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "8"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DFA for \"ends with <code class=\"mm\">abb</code>\" only has to remember how much of the pattern <code class=\"mm\">abb</code> is currently matched as a suffix of the input read so far.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>State 0: nothing matched (no suffix of <code class=\"mm\">abb</code>).</li><li>State 1: the input ends in <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</li><li>State 2: the input ends in <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>.</li><li>State 3 (accepting): the input ends in <code class=\"mm\">abb</code>.</li><li>Transitions: on <code class=\"mm\">b</code> state 0 stays in 0; on <code class=\"mm\">a</code> state 0 goes to 1; state 2 on <code class=\"mm\">b</code> goes to 3, on <code class=\"mm\">a</code> back to 1; state 3 on <code class=\"mm\">b</code> stays in 3, on <code class=\"mm\">a</code> goes to 1.</li><li>All four states are reachable and pairwise distinguishable, so the minimal DFA has 4 states.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — 4 states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 8 by applying the <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span> subset-construction bound. That bound is a worst case for the <i>NFA</i> of the expression; the minimal DFA for this language needs only 4 states.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q07", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following regular expressions does not denote the set of all strings over {a, b}?", "options": ["(a + b)*", "(a* b*)*", "(b* a*)*", "(a b*)*"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two expressions denote the same language only if each can generate every string of the other. Test a string that starts with the \"wrong\" symbol.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span> by definition.</li><li>(B) <code class=\"mm\">(a*b*)*</code>: any string can be cut into maximal blocks of a's and b's, so it generates all of <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span>.</li><li>(C) <code class=\"mm\">(b*a*)*</code>: similarly, cut the string into maximal blocks of b's followed by a's.</li><li>(D) <code class=\"mm\">(ab*)*</code>: every iteration starts with an <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, so every non-empty generated string begins with <code class=\"mm\">a</code>. The string <code class=\"mm\">ba</code> is not generated.</li><li>Hence (D) is not <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that any star applied to a concatenation equals <span class=\"mm\">Σ*</span>. Always test a short string that begins with the symbol the first factor does not allow.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q08", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "lex-input-structure", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A Lex/Flex specification has three sections separated by %% lines. In which section are the pattern–action rules written?", "options": ["The first section (definitions)", "The second section (rules)", "The third section (user subroutines)", "Any of the three; order does not matter"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A Lex program is <i>declarations %% translation rules %% auxiliary procedures</i>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Section 1 (definitions): optional C declarations in <code class=\"mm\">%{ %}</code>, plus named regular definitions such as <code class=\"mm\">DIGIT [0-9]</code>.</li><li>Section 2 (rules): one line per rule, <i>pattern</i> followed by the <i>action</i> to run when the pattern matches.</li><li>Section 3 (user subroutines): auxiliary C code, typically <code class=\"mm\">main()</code> and error routines.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — the second section holds the rules.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Placing helper functions in the rules section. Only pattern&ndash;action pairs belong there; any other C code must go in the third section (or in <code class=\"mm\">%{ %}</code> for declarations).</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q09", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "lex-input-structure", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A Lex/Flex scanner contains the rule [0-9]+. While scanning the line total = 100; that rule fires. What are yytext and yyleng for this match?", "options": ["yytext = \"100\", yyleng = 3", "yytext = \"100\", yyleng = 2", "yytext = \" 100\", yyleng = 4", "yytext = \"total = 100\", yyleng = 11"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">yytext</code> points at the characters of the lexeme that the rule actually matched; <code class=\"mm\">yyleng</code> is the number of characters in that lexeme.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The rule <code class=\"mm\">[0-9]+</code> matches the maximal run of digits, which is <code class=\"mm\">100</code>.</li><li>The match contains exactly three characters: <code class=\"mm\">1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">0</code>, <code class=\"mm\">0</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">yytext</code> = <code class=\"mm\">\"100\"</code> and <code class=\"mm\">yyleng</code> = 3.</li><li>The leading space is not part of the match; it would have been consumed by a separate whitespace rule.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Two errors are common: counting the length from zero (giving 2) and including the preceding blank in the lexeme (giving 4). <code class=\"mm\">yytext</code> never contains characters the rule did not match.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q10", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "lex-input-structure", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which statement about a DFA-based scanner produced by Lex/Flex is true?", "options": ["The automaton is built when the scanner is generated, so scanning n characters takes time proportional to n, with at most one character of lookahead past the current token.", "A fresh automaton is built for each token while the scanner runs, which is why scanning is slow.", "The scanner backtracks over the input to find the longest match, so the worst case is quadratic.", "The scanner needs a pushdown stack, because regular expressions can nest."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The patterns of all rules are compiled into one automaton (regular expressions &rarr; NFA &rarr; DFA) at scanner-generation time. At run time the scanner just simulates that DFA on the input.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Simulation performs one transition per input character, so the work is <span class=\"mm\">O(n)</span> for <span class=\"mm\">n</span> characters.</li><li>The only lookahead is the single character that fails to extend the current token; it is pushed back and starts the next token.</li><li>There is no backtracking: a DFA is deterministic, so the last accepting state seen is the end of the token.</li><li>Nesting is beyond regular languages; a scanner has no stack — that is exactly why balanced constructs are left to the parser.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the NFA (which may branch) with the DFA that is actually simulated, and therefore expecting backtracking or exponential behaviour.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q11", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "input-buffering-lookahead", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In the two-buffer input scheme, a special sentinel character is placed at the end of each half-buffer. Its purpose is to", "options": ["mark the end of the token currently being scanned.", "remove the need to test for the end of the buffer on every character, so that only one test per character is needed.", "allow the scanner to match tokens of unbounded length.", "terminate the program once the input file is exhausted."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Without a sentinel the inner loop must check two conditions per character: \"is the buffer exhausted?\" and \"which character is this?\". A sentinel — a character that cannot occur in the source, such as end-of-file — merges the two tests into one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Each half-buffer ends with the sentinel.</li><li>The loop advances while the current character is not the sentinel; a single comparison suffices.</li><li>On reaching the sentinel, the scanner knows the half is exhausted and refills it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the sentinel marks the end of a token (it marks the end of a <i>buffer</i>) or that it lifts the bound on lexeme length (it does not — a lexeme must still fit in the buffer).</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q12", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "input-buffering-lookahead", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A DFA-based lexer is scanning a token and, after reading character c, the automaton enters a non-accepting state from which no accepting state is reachable. Assuming it had reached an accepting state earlier, what does the lexer do?", "options": ["It reports a lexical error immediately, because c cannot extend any token.", "It returns the last token it accepted and treats c as the first character of the next token.", "It restarts the automaton at the initial state and rescans the whole buffer from the beginning.", "It discards the accepted token and reports an error."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The lexer must return the longest prefix of the input that is a token. It therefore remembers the position of the last accepting state, together with the input position where that state was reached.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The automaton accepted a prefix of length <span class=\"mm\">k</span> and then read further characters that lead to a dead state.</li><li>The token is the accepted prefix; the extra characters read after position <span class=\"mm\">k</span> are returned to the input (only one character here, <code class=\"mm\">c</code>).</li><li>Scanning restarts from the initial state at that pushed-back position.</li><li>An error is reported only if <i>no</i> accepting state was reached at all.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Declaring an error as soon as the automaton leaves the accepting set. A token has already been found; the dead state only says the token cannot be extended.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q13", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A scanner lists its rules in this order: (1) [a-z]+ → return ID; (2) if → return IF. In a Lex/Flex-style scanner, what happens on the input if?", "options": ["IF is returned, because keyword rules automatically outrank identifier rules.", "ID is returned, because both rules match the same two-character lexeme and the earlier rule wins the tie, so rule (2) never fires.", "A lexical error is reported, because two rules match the same text.", "ID is returned for if but IF for a longer word such as iffy."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rule selection is two-stage: (i) choose the longest match among all rules; (ii) if several rules match that same longest lexeme, the rule written earliest wins.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Both rules match exactly two characters of <code class=\"mm\">if</code> — neither match is longer.</li><li>The tie is therefore broken by rule order, and rule (1) is earlier.</li><li>So the scanner returns ID and rule (2) is dead code: no input can ever make it fire.</li><li>The correct specification lists the keyword rule <i>before</i> the identifier rule.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that keyword recognition is automatic, or that a \"more specific\" pattern outranks a more general one. Lex has no specificity rule — only longest match, then earliest rule. Option (D) is also wrong because <code class=\"mm\">iffy</code> matches only <code class=\"mm\">[a-z]+</code> (longer than <code class=\"mm\">if</code>).</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q14", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "input-buffering-lookahead", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In a two-buffer scheme the two halves hold N characters each and both halves are filled by one read operation. A lexeme begins near the end of the first half and continues into the second. Which statement is true?", "options": ["The scanner reports an error, because a lexeme may not cross a buffer boundary.", "Scanning simply continues into the second half; before the first half is next refilled, the part of the lexeme that lies in it must be preserved (moved to the start of the buffer).", "The scanner must restart the lexeme from the beginning of the file.", "The buffer must be doubled for every lexeme that crosses the boundary."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The two halves are contiguous, so a lexeme that crosses the midpoint needs no special action <i>while</i> it is being read. The danger is the later refill, which would overwrite the lexeme's first characters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Characters <span class=\"mm\">1 … N</span> live in the first half and <span class=\"mm\">N+1 … 2N</span> in the second; the scan pointer moves through them as one buffer.</li><li>A lexeme starting at <span class=\"mm\">N−3</span> and ending at <span class=\"mm\">N+4</span> is read normally.</li><li>When the scan pointer reaches the end of the second half, the first half is refilled — which would destroy the lexeme's head.</li><li>The standard fix is to move the lexeme's characters to the front of the buffer (or, equivalently, to enlarge the buffer) before refilling.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a lexeme cannot cross the boundary. It can; what cannot happen is refilling a half that still holds part of the current lexeme.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q15", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A scanner has the token classes INT = [0-9]+, FLOAT = [0-9]+\\.[0-9]+ and DOT = \\.. On the input 3.14 the scanner returns", "options": ["INT 3, then DOT ., then INT 14", "FLOAT 3.14", "FLOAT 3.1, then INT 4", "a lexical error, because 3.14 matches two rules"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Rule priority is decided by match length first. INT can match only the first character; FLOAT can match all four; the longest match therefore wins, and rule order is irrelevant here.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>At position 1, INT matches <code class=\"mm\">3</code> (length 1).</li><li>At the same position FLOAT matches <code class=\"mm\">3.14</code> (length 4).</li><li>Longest match wins: FLOAT <code class=\"mm\">3.14</code> is returned as one token.</li><li>The DOT rule is never consulted, because a longer match already exists.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — a single FLOAT token.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the first matching rule wins (option A). Order only breaks ties between matches of equal length; here the lengths differ, so longest match decides.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q16", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about lexemes and tokens are true?", "options": ["Two different lexemes can belong to the same token class.", "A token is a pair: a token name (an abstract class) and an optional attribute value; a lexeme is the concrete string matched.", "Every token returned for a keyword must carry an attribute value.", "A correct scanner assigns each lexeme to exactly one token class, because longest-match and rule-priority resolve any overlap."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A token class is a set of lexemes; the lexer returns a token name plus, when needed, an attribute that distinguishes the members of that class.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: <code class=\"mm\">count1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">total</code> are different lexemes of the same class <i>id</i>.</li><li>(B) True: that is the standard definition — e.g. &lt;id, pointer to symbol-table entry&gt;.</li><li>(C) False: keywords and punctuation need no attribute; the token name alone identifies them.</li><li>(D) True: the two conventions (longest match, then earliest rule) make the scanner deterministic, so a lexeme such as <code class=\"mm\">if</code> cannot be both a keyword and an identifier.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (C) is the trap: attributes exist to distinguish members of a class (which identifier? which constant?). A keyword has no members to distinguish.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q17", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following languages can be recognised by a DFA, and hence by a Lex/Flex-generated lexer?", "options": ["The set of strings over {a, b} that end in ab.", "The set of strings over {a, b} that contain an equal number of a's and b's.", "The set of strings over {a, b} of the form aⁿbⁿ, n ≥ 0.", "The set of valid C integer literals (decimal, octal and hexadecimal)."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A scanner can recognise exactly the regular languages. A language is regular iff some finite automaton accepts it; languages needing unbounded counting are not regular (pumping lemma).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) Regular: a three-state automaton remembers whether the last two symbols were <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>.</li><li>(B) Not regular: it needs to count the difference between the number of a's and b's, which is unbounded.</li><li>(C) Not regular: it needs to count the a's and compare with the b's.</li><li>(D) Regular: the set of syntactically valid literals is described by a regular expression such as <code class=\"mm\">0[xX][0-9a-fA-F]+ | 0[0-7]* | [1-9][0-9]*</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a larger buffer or a bigger automaton can count. No finite automaton can compare two unbounded counts — that is exactly what the pumping lemma rules out.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q18", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "lex-input-structure", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about Lex/Flex are true?", "options": ["When several rules match, the longest match wins; among rules matching the same longest lexeme, the rule written first wins.", "The finite automaton is built when the scanner is generated, so scanning n characters takes O(n) time.", "In the rules section, a rule whose action is the single character | uses the action of the next rule.", "The definitions section must be the last section of a Lex specification."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Lex fixes both the rule-selection policy and the three-section layout; the generated scanner is a table-driven DFA.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: longest match first, then earliest rule — the same two-stage rule as in any maximal-munch scanner.</li><li>(B) True: patterns are compiled to one DFA at generation time, and simulation costs one transition per character.</li><li>(C) True: <code class=\"mm\">|</code> as an action means \"same action as the following rule\".</li><li>(D) False: the order is definitions, rules, user subroutines — definitions come first.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (D) is the trap, and its mirror image — putting declarations after the rules — is the most common practical mistake when writing a Lex file.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q19", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "input-buffering-lookahead", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about the two-buffer input scheme and its sentinels are true?", "options": ["The sentinel must be a character that cannot occur in the source text, so it can never be mistaken for real input.", "Even with the sentinel in place, the scanner still needs two tests per character: one for the end of the buffer and one for the character class.", "If a lexeme crosses from one half-buffer into the other, the part of the lexeme already read must be preserved before that half is refilled.", "Without enlarging the buffer or moving the lexeme, a single lexeme cannot be longer than the buffer."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The sentinel exists precisely to halve the per-character work, and the scheme keeps lexemes intact by moving them before a refill.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: end-of-file is the usual sentinel; if it could appear in the text, a real occurrence would be read as the buffer end.</li><li>(B) False: the sentinel removes the end-of-buffer test, so one test per character remains — that is the entire point of the trick.</li><li>(C) True: otherwise the refill overwrites the lexeme's head.</li><li>(D) True: a lexeme must fit in the buffer; longer lexemes force a bigger buffer or a move-to-front copy.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (C) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (B) is the trap — the sentinel is not cosmetic. If two tests per character were still needed, the scheme would buy nothing.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q20", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about the lexer–parser interface are true?", "options": ["A lexer must produce the complete token stream for a file before the parser can begin.", "A token may carry an attribute value, for example a pointer to the symbol-table entry of an identifier.", "Deciding whether an identifier has been declared before its use is part of lexical analysis.", "In a single-pass design the parser may keep one token of lookahead, which amounts to pushing a token back to the lexer."], "answer_display": "Options (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The parser normally pulls tokens on demand through a procedure such as <code class=\"mm\">getNextToken()</code>, and the two phases communicate through tokens with attributes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) False: production compilers are demand-driven; buffering the whole file as tokens wastes memory and delays error reporting.</li><li>(B) True: <code class=\"mm\">&lt;id, p&gt;</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">p</code> points at the identifier's symbol-table entry, is the standard example.</li><li>(C) False: declaration checking needs the symbol table built across statements — a semantic task.</li><li>(D) True: a one-token pushback is a common way to give the parser the lookahead it needs.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (B) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Statement (A) is the trap: it describes a batch design nobody uses, and it would make single-pass compilation impossible.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q21", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "How many tokens does a C lexer return for the input line total = count1 + 42;? Count keywords, identifiers, operators, constants and punctuation as one token each, and ignore whitespace.", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every lexeme that is not whitespace or a comment becomes exactly one token; whitespace between tokens is discarded by the scanner.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">total</code> — identifier (1)</li><li><code class=\"mm\">=</code> — assignment operator (2)</li><li><code class=\"mm\">count1</code> — identifier (3)</li><li><code class=\"mm\">+</code> — additive operator (4)</li><li><code class=\"mm\">42</code> — integer constant (5)</li><li><code class=\"mm\">;</code> — punctuation (6)</li><li>Whitespace contributes no token.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 6 tokens.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the four spaces as tokens, or merging <code class=\"mm\">count1</code> into one token with <code class=\"mm\">+</code>. Whitespace is skipped, and each maximal lexeme is a separate token.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q22", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "The minimal DFA accepting the language denoted by (a|b)*abb has how many states? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The automaton only needs to remember the longest prefix of <code class=\"mm\">abb</code> that is a suffix of the input read so far.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>State 0 — no suffix of the pattern (start state).</li><li>State 1 — input ends in <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</li><li>State 2 — input ends in <code class=\"mm\">ab</code>.</li><li>State 3 — input ends in <code class=\"mm\">abb</code> (accepting).</li><li>Transitions: 0 -a-&gt; 1, 0 -b-&gt; 0, 1 -a-&gt; 1, 1 -b-&gt; 2, 2 -a-&gt; 1, 2 -b-&gt; 3, 3 -a-&gt; 1, 3 -b-&gt; 3. All four states are reachable and pairwise distinguishable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 4 states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 8 from the <span class=\"mm\">2ⁿ</span> subset-construction bound, or 3 by forgetting that the start state is distinct from the \"ends in <code class=\"mm\">a</code>\" state.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q23", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "input-buffering-lookahead", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A scanner uses the two-buffer scheme with halves of 4096 characters each, and each read operation fills both halves. A source file of 10,000 characters is scanned. How many read operations are performed? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> One read fills the whole buffer, i.e. both halves: 2 × 4096 = 8192 characters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Read 1 supplies characters 1 … 8192.</li><li>Characters 8193 … 10,000 (1808 characters) remain, so read 2 supplies the rest and hits end of file.</li><li>No third read is needed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 2 read operations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dividing 10,000 by 4096 and rounding up (giving 3). A read fills both halves, so the capacity per read is 8192, not 4096.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q24", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "lex-input-structure", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A complete Lex/Flex specification has three sections. How many %% separator lines are needed to delimit them? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three sections in a linear file need two delimiters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Section 1 (definitions) &nbsp; <code class=\"mm\">%%</code> &nbsp; Section 2 (rules) &nbsp; <code class=\"mm\">%%</code> &nbsp; Section 3 (user subroutines).</li><li>Separators required: 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 2 separator lines.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing three separators (which produces an empty fourth section) or none at all (in which case the whole file is treated as the rules section).</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q25", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "How many states does the minimal DFA for the language of all strings over {a, b} whose third symbol from the end is a have — that is, for the language of (a|b)*a(a|b)(a|b)? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> To decide a property of the last three symbols the automaton must remember those three symbols, and nothing older. There are <span class=\"mm\">2³ = 8</span> possible three-symbol suffixes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Let the states be the 8 strings of length ≤ 3 recording the last three symbols read (with shorter strings for inputs of length below 3).</li><li>On reading a symbol, drop the oldest symbol and append the new one — a sliding window of width 3.</li><li>A state is accepting exactly when its recorded suffix has <code class=\"mm\">a</code> in the third position from the end, i.e. suffixes <code class=\"mm\">a??</code>.</li><li>All 8 states are reachable, and any two distinct suffixes are distinguished by appending the length-3 string that makes one accepting and the other not, so the DFA is minimal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 8 states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 (remembering only the last symbol) or 16 (remembering four). The window must be exactly three symbols wide: fewer cannot decide the property, more is redundant.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q26", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which one of the following is not passed from the lexical analyser to the syntax analyser as a token?", "options": ["A keyword such as while", "An identifier such as total", "A comment such as /* note */", "A relational operator such as <="], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A token is the pair (token name, attribute value). White space and comments are recognised by the scanner only so that they can be discarded; they never reach the parser.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The scanner reads characters and groups them into lexemes.</li><li>Each lexeme that matches a token pattern is returned as a token name with an optional attribute value.</li><li>Comments and blank space have patterns too, but their only purpose is to be skipped.</li><li>So a comment is consumed by the lexer and is not a token.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C) — a comment is not a token.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> A comment is made of characters and is matched by a regular expression, so it feels like a token. Being recognised by the lexer and being handed to the parser are different things.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q27", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Three ideas are involved in scanning: a token, a pattern and a lexeme. A lexeme is", "options": ["a regular expression that describes every string of a token class", "the actual sequence of characters in the source text that matches the pattern of a token", "the internal integer code that a compiler assigns to a token class", "the list of attributes kept for an identifier in the symbol table"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Pattern, lexeme and token form a three-level hierarchy: the pattern is the description, the lexeme is the concrete string, and the token is the abstract class handed to the parser.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Pattern: the form that the lexemes of a token may take, written as a regular expression.</li><li>Lexeme: a sequence of characters in the source program that matches a pattern.</li><li>Token: the abstract class returned to the parser.</li><li>Hence a lexeme is the actual character sequence.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Option (A) is the pattern and option (C) is the token code. Collapsing the three levels is the usual mistake.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q28", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "The scanner returns a pair (token name, attribute value). For an identifier token the attribute value is normally", "options": ["the token name repeated, so that the parser can compare names", "a pointer to the entry for that lexeme in the symbol table", "the line number on which the identifier occurs", "empty, because identifiers carry no information"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Attribute values distinguish different lexemes that share one token name. Every identifier maps to the token name <code class=\"mm\">id</code>, so the attribute must identify which identifier it is.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All identifiers share the token name <code class=\"mm\">id</code>.</li><li>Without an attribute, <code class=\"mm\">count</code> and <code class=\"mm\">total</code> would be indistinguishable to the parser.</li><li>The scanner therefore returns a pointer to the symbol-table entry holding the lexeme and its attributes.</li><li>The line number is used for diagnostics and is not the attribute of the identifier.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the attribute is the character string itself. Implementations may store the string in the symbol table and pass a pointer; the attribute is the symbol-table entry, not the token name.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q29", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "The C statement x = -1; is scanned with maximal munch. How many tokens does the scanner produce? Treat -= as a valid C operator, and ignore white space.", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Maximal munch takes the longest prefix that matches <i>some</i> pattern. A compound operator exists only if it really is a token of the language, so <code class=\"mm\">=-</code> cannot be formed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>At <code class=\"mm\">x</code> the identifier pattern matches just <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, giving token 1.</li><li>At <code class=\"mm\">=</code> the scanner tries the two-character candidate <code class=\"mm\">=-</code>; no such operator exists, so the one-character <code class=\"mm\">=</code> is taken. Token 2.</li><li>The <code class=\"mm\">-</code> is then the unary minus operator. Token 3.</li><li>The constant <code class=\"mm\">1</code> is token 4 and the <code class=\"mm\">;</code> is token 5.</li><li>Total: <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, <code class=\"mm\">=</code>, <code class=\"mm\">-</code>, <code class=\"mm\">1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">;</code> — five tokens.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C) — 5 tokens.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by treating <code class=\"mm\">=-</code> as one operator, or 3 by assuming the unary minus belongs to the constant. The minus sign is a token in its own right.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q30", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following are tokens of the C language? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["int", "42", "/* note */", "&&", "a newline character"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The token classes of a language are keywords, identifiers, constants, string literals, operators and punctuators. White space and comments are not token classes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">int</code> is a keyword, hence a token.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">42</code> is an integer constant, hence a token.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">/* note */</code> is a comment: recognised only so that it can be discarded.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">&amp;&amp;</code> is a logical operator, hence a token.</li><li>A newline is white space: skipped, not a token.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting comments or white space as tokens, which inflates every token-count answer by one or two.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q31", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following tasks belong to the lexical analyser? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Stripping comments and white space before the tokens reach the parser.", "Entering each new identifier into the symbol table.", "Deciding whether the grammar of the language is ambiguous.", "Recording the line number so that later phases can report positions.", "Performing type checking on expressions."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The scanner does character-level work: it groups characters into lexemes, discards noise, records positions and maintains the symbol table. Grammar-level and type-level decisions belong to later phases.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Stripping comments and white space: a scanner task, since only the scanner sees raw characters.</li><li>Inserting identifiers into the symbol table: done while scanning, so each identifier is entered once.</li><li>Deciding whether the grammar is ambiguous: a property of the grammar, decided by the parser designer — no scanner can affect it.</li><li>Recording line numbers: the scanner tracks <code class=\"mm\">yylineno</code> for error messages.</li><li>Type checking: a semantic-analysis task, performed on the syntax tree.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the scanner 'resolves' ambiguity. Ambiguity is a property of the grammar; separating phases cannot remove it.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q32", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "tokens-lexemes-patterns", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "How many tokens does a C lexer return for the statement a[i] = b + 1;? Count keywords, identifiers, operators, constants and punctuation as one token each, and ignore white space. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "9", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Token counting: every keyword, identifier, constant, operator and punctuator counts once; white space and comments count zero.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Scan left to right under maximal munch.</li><li>Tokens: <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">[</code>, <code class=\"mm\">i</code>, <code class=\"mm\">]</code>, <code class=\"mm\">=</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, <code class=\"mm\">+</code>, <code class=\"mm\">1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">;</code>.</li><li>No two adjacent characters here combine into a longer token.</li><li>Count: 9.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 9</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Merging the brackets into a single 'bracket' token, or counting the spaces. Each punctuator is its own token.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q33", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which regular expression denotes exactly the C unsigned decimal integer constants that have no leading zeros?", "options": ["[0-9]+", "[1-9][0-9]*", "0|[1-9][0-9]*", "0?[1-9][0-9]*"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A no-leading-zero literal is either the single digit 0, or a non-zero digit followed by any number of digits. The two cases must be unioned.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">[0-9]+</code> allows leading zeros such as 007.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">[1-9][0-9]*</code> excludes the single digit 0.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">0|[1-9][0-9]*</code> is exactly the union of the two valid shapes.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">0?[1-9][0-9]*</code> still generates 05, because the optional 0 may precede a non-zero digit.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that 0 itself is legal. The 'first digit non-zero' rule has to be written as a union, not as an optional prefix.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q34", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "The pattern [0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?([eE][+-]?[0-9]+)? describes", "options": ["integer constants only", "decimal floating-point constants with an optional fractional part and an optional exponent", "identifiers that begin with a digit", "hexadecimal constants"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each bracketed group is optional, so the pattern is the concatenation of a mandatory digit run with an optional fraction and an optional exponent.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">[0-9]+</code> is the mandatory integer part.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(\\.[0-9]+)?</code> adds an optional fraction, so 3.25 is accepted.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">([eE][+-]?[0-9]+)?</code> adds an optional exponent with an optional sign, so 1e-9 is accepted.</li><li>Together they describe decimal floating-point constants; hex forms need 0x and hex digits.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the <code class=\"mm\">?</code> as 'one or more'. It means zero or one occurrence, so both groups may be absent and plain integers are accepted too.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q35", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A scanner has exactly two patterns: P1 = a+ returning token A, and P2 = ab returning token B. Using maximal munch, what does it produce for the input aab?", "options": ["A(aa), then a lexical error at the b", "A(a), then A(ab)", "A(a), then A(a), then a lexical error at the b", "B(ab), preceded by a lexical error at the first a"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Maximal munch compares, at the current position, the longest prefix that each pattern can match, and commits to the longest one. There is no backtracking into a shorter match to suit a different pattern.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>At position 1 the remaining input is <code class=\"mm\">aab</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a+</code> matches <code class=\"mm\">aa</code> — two characters.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">ab</code> matches nothing at position 1, because the second character is a, not b.</li><li>The longer match wins: token A with lexeme <code class=\"mm\">aa</code>.</li><li>At position 3 the input is <code class=\"mm\">b</code>: neither pattern matches, so a lexical error is reported.</li><li>The scanner never re-splits <code class=\"mm\">aa</code> into a single a in order to make <code class=\"mm\">ab</code> possible.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting the scanner to back up and return B(ab). A DFA-based scanner commits to the longest match found and does not search for a globally consistent tokenisation.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q36", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which one of the following strings does not belong to the language denoted by (ab|ba)*?", "options": ["abab", "abba", "baab", "aabb"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every string of <code class=\"mm\">(ab|ba)*</code> is a concatenation of two-character blocks, each of which is ab or ba. So the length is even and, inside each block, the two characters differ.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">abab</code> = ab · ab, so it is in the language.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">abba</code> = ab · ba, so it is in the language.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">baab</code> = ba · ab, so it is in the language.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">aabb</code> would have to split as aa · bb or a · abb …, and neither aa nor bb is ab or ba. Not in the language.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D) — <code class=\"mm\">aabb</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only the length or only the counts of a and b. Both <code class=\"mm\">aabb</code> and <code class=\"mm\">abba</code> have two a's and two b's, yet only the second is in the language.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q37", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Over the alphabet {a, b}, which regular expression denotes exactly the strings containing an even number of a's?", "options": ["(b*ab*a)*b*", "(a|b)*a(a|b)*", "a*b*", "(ab|ba)*"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Write the string as a sequence of b-runs separated by single a's. The number of a's is even exactly when the a's can be grouped in pairs, each pair being <code class=\"mm\">a</code> with any number of b's before and after it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A string with an even number of a's can be split as <code class=\"mm\">b*</code>, then repeated blocks <code class=\"mm\">a b* a</code>, then a final <code class=\"mm\">b*</code>.</li><li>That is exactly <code class=\"mm\">(b*ab*a)*b*</code>.</li><li>Check the distractors: <code class=\"mm\">(a|b)*a(a|b)*</code> admits aba (two a's, fine) but also ab, which has one a.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a*b*</code> has at most one run of a's and admits a (odd).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">(ab|ba)*</code> admits only even-length alternating strings, and misses bbaabb.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reaching for <code class=\"mm\">(b*ab*)*</code>, which is also correct, or for <code class=\"mm\">(aa)*</code>, which forbids b's. The correct form must allow b's anywhere.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q38", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following token classes are specified by regular expressions and can therefore be handled by a DFA-based scanner? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["C identifiers", "Integer constants with an optional decimal exponent", "Balanced parentheses", "Comments delimited by /* and */, without nesting", "String literals with escaped quotes"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D) and (E)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A token class is lexer-friendly exactly when the set of its strings is regular, that is, recognisable by a finite automaton with no auxiliary storage.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Identifiers: a start set followed by a starred set — regular.</li><li>Integer constants with an optional exponent: a finite union of concatenations — regular.</li><li>Balanced parentheses: needs an unbounded count of openers, so not regular.</li><li>Non-nesting comments: <code class=\"mm\">\\/\\*([^*]|\\*+[^*/])*\\*+\\/</code> — regular.</li><li>Escaped string literals: <code class=\"mm\">\"([^\"\\\\]|\\\\.)*\"</code> — regular, since an escape is a fixed two-character unit.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B), (D) and (E).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rejecting escaped string literals because they look recursive. A backslash escape is not a nested construct, so a star over two alternatives suffices.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q39", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A scanner uses the three patterns below over the alphabet of digits, . and +, and applies maximal munch.\n[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+ &rarr; FLOAT, [0-9]+ &rarr; NUM, \\+ &rarr; PLUS.\nHow many tokens does it produce for the input 12.5+3? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Maximal munch may run across the boundary between two candidate patterns; the longer match wins even when the shorter pattern is listed first.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>At position 1 the candidates are <code class=\"mm\">12</code> (NUM, two characters) and <code class=\"mm\">12.5</code> (FLOAT, four characters).</li><li>The longer match wins: FLOAT with lexeme <code class=\"mm\">12.5</code>.</li><li>At position 5 the input is <code class=\"mm\">+3</code>; <code class=\"mm\">+</code> matches PLUS.</li><li>At position 6, <code class=\"mm\">3</code> matches NUM.</li><li>Total: FLOAT, PLUS, NUM — three tokens.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 3</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by splitting <code class=\"mm\">12.5</code> into 12, . and 5, or 5 by also counting a separate dot token.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q40", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "regular-expressions-to-lexers", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "How many states does the minimal DFA for the language of all strings over {a, b} whose second symbol from the right is a have? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DFA that must inspect a fixed suffix of length k remembers the last k symbols, so it needs k + 1 states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>State q0: fewer than two symbols have been read, or the last two symbols are not yet decided.</li><li>State q1: the last symbol read is a.</li><li>State q2: the last two symbols read are a followed by anything — accepting.</li><li>On reading a new symbol x, the window slides: q0 goes to q1 if x = a else stays q0; q1 goes to q2 if the new symbol arrives after an a; q2 goes to q1 or q0 depending on x.</li><li>All three states are reachable and pairwise distinguishable, so 3 is minimal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 3</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by confusing this with 'third symbol from the right is a', which needs 8 states, or with 'ends in ab', which needs 3 states but a different acceptance condition.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q41", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "lex-input-structure", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In a Lex/Flex specification, the text between %{ and %} is", "options": ["the pattern–action rules", "C declarations that are copied verbatim into the generated scanner", "the user's main function, which is mandatory", "a comment block that Lex ignores"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The first section of a Lex file holds definitions. C code inside <code class=\"mm\">%{</code> <code class=\"mm\">%}</code> is copied into the generated scanner, while macro definitions outside those delimiters are local shorthands.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">%{</code> <code class=\"mm\">%}</code> encloses raw C: <code class=\"mm\">#include</code> lines, global variables, typedefs.</li><li>That text is emitted verbatim at the top of the generated scanner.</li><li>Pattern–action rules belong to the second section.</li><li>The third section holds auxiliary functions, which may include <code class=\"mm\">main</code>, but <code class=\"mm\">main</code> is not mandatory when a library driver is linked.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the two kinds of content in the first section: C code inside <code class=\"mm\">%{</code> <code class=\"mm\">%}</code>, and regex macros such as <code class=\"mm\">DIGIT [0-9]</code> outside them.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q42", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "lex-input-structure", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Inside a Lex action, the variable yylineno holds", "options": ["the number of the input line currently being scanned", "the length of the matched lexeme", "the text of the matched lexeme", "the number of rules that have fired so far"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Lex exposes several auxiliaries to the action code: <code class=\"mm\">yytext</code> (the lexeme), <code class=\"mm\">yyleng</code> (its length) and <code class=\"mm\">yylineno</code> (the current line number).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">yytext</code> is the matched character string.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">yyleng</code> is the number of matched characters.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">yylineno</code> counts newlines consumed so far, so it is the current input line.</li><li>It is normally used to prefix error messages with a line number.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Mixing up <code class=\"mm\">yylineno</code> and <code class=\"mm\">yyleng</code>. The 'len' in the name is the only thing that makes it a length.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q43", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "lex-input-structure", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which statement about yywrap() in a Lex/Flex scanner is correct?", "options": ["It is called after every lexeme to validate the token.", "It is called when the scanner reaches end of input; returning 1 tells the scanner to stop.", "It must return 0 for the scanner to terminate.", "It replaces yylex() when the input comes from a file."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When <code class=\"mm\">yylex()</code> exhausts its input it calls <code class=\"mm\">yywrap()</code>. A non-zero return value signals that there is no further input, so scanning ends; returning 0 tells the scanner that a new input file has been set up in <code class=\"mm\">yyin</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The scanner hits the end of <code class=\"mm\">yyin</code> and calls <code class=\"mm\">yywrap()</code>.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">yywrap()</code> returns 1, <code class=\"mm\">yylex()</code> returns 0 and the scan is finished.</li><li>If it returns 0, the scanner continues reading from whatever <code class=\"mm\">yyin</code> now points at — this is how multiple input files are chained.</li><li>It is not called per lexeme, and it never replaces <code class=\"mm\">yylex()</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reversing the convention: 1 means 'we are done'. Without <code class=\"mm\">%option noyywrap</code>, you must supply your own <code class=\"mm\">yywrap()</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q44", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "lex-input-structure", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following names are standard Lex/Flex auxiliary entities usable inside a rule action? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["yytext", "yyleng", "yylineno", "yytoken", "yylex"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C) and (E)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Flex exposes <code class=\"mm\">yytext</code>, <code class=\"mm\">yyleng</code>, <code class=\"mm\">yylineno</code>, <code class=\"mm\">yyin</code>, <code class=\"mm\">yyout</code> and the entry point <code class=\"mm\">yylex</code>. <code class=\"mm\">yytoken</code> is not part of the interface.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">yytext</code> — the matched lexeme: valid.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">yyleng</code> — its length: valid.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">yylineno</code> — the current line: valid.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">yytoken</code> — does not exist in Lex or Flex.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">yylex</code> — the scanner function itself, callable recursively: valid.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B), (C) and (E).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every identifier beginning with 'yy' is real. <code class=\"mm\">yytoken</code>, <code class=\"mm\">yypos</code> and <code class=\"mm\">yytextlen</code> are common inventions.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q45", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "lex-input-structure", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A Lex rule [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]* fires on the input line temp1 = 7;. What is the value of yyleng when the rule's action runs? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The scanner matches the longest prefix that the rule accepts, and <code class=\"mm\">yyleng</code> is the length of that matched lexeme.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The rule starts matching at the first character, t.</li><li>It accepts letters and digits, so it continues through e, m, p and 1.</li><li>The next character is a space, which is not in the character class, so matching stops.</li><li>The lexeme is <code class=\"mm\">temp1</code>, five characters, so <code class=\"mm\">yyleng</code> is 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 5</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the following space (6) or the whole statement. <code class=\"mm\">yyleng</code> covers the matched lexeme only.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q46", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "input-buffering-lookahead", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Why does a lexical analyser read its input into two buffers instead of one?", "options": ["So that a lexeme crossing a buffer boundary can be handled without copying it to another place.", "So that two tokens can be scanned at the same time.", "So that the number of disk reads becomes exactly zero.", "So that the symbol table can be kept in the second buffer."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In the two-buffer scheme the halves are adjacent in memory, so a lexeme that runs off the end of the first half continues directly into the second half.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Fill the first half; when the scanner reaches its end, fill the second half.</li><li>Because the halves are contiguous, the tail in the first half and the head in the second form one continuous string.</li><li>No copying or shifting of the lexeme is needed.</li><li>Scanning is sequential and single-threaded, so parallel scanning plays no part.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the two-buffer scheme with double buffering for I/O overlap. Its purpose is contiguous storage across a boundary.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q47", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "input-buffering-lookahead", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A DFA-based scanner has read up to position p in a non-accepting state, but it passed through an accepting state at position q, where q < p. It must fall back to q + 1. In the two-buffer scheme this fall-back costs", "options": ["re-reading the characters from q + 1 to p from secondary storage", "a constant-time pointer assignment, because those characters are still in the buffer", "copying the lexeme into a separate array and restarting the DFA", "restarting the DFA from the beginning of the file"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The scanner keeps a pointer to the character after the last accepting state. Retraction is a pointer move; the characters have not been discarded.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The DFA records the furthest position at which some pattern accepted, say q.</li><li>Scanning continues to p looking for a longer match.</li><li>If no longer match appears, the lexeme is q characters wide and scanning resumes at q + 1.</li><li>All characters between q and p are still in the buffer, so the cost is one assignment.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming retraction means backtracking over the input. Only the pointer moves; the characters are neither re-read nor re-examined.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q48", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "input-buffering-lookahead", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "In the two-buffer scheme each half holds N characters and the two halves are adjacent in memory. If the scanner never moves a lexeme, the longest lexeme it can hold is", "options": ["N characters", "2N characters", "unbounded", "N/2 characters"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Both halves together form one contiguous region of 2N characters. A lexeme may begin anywhere in the first half and run on into the second, so its maximum length is the size of the whole region.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The two halves are adjacent, so the buffer is one array of 2N characters.</li><li>A lexeme may start at the first character of the first half.</li><li>It may then run to the last character of the second half.</li><li>Maximum length without moving anything: 2N characters.</li><li>A longer lexeme forces the scanner to move the part already read, or to enlarge the buffer.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — 2N characters.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering N because each half is N long. The halves are contiguous, which is exactly why the scheme exists.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q49", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "input-buffering-lookahead", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "Which of the following statements about lookahead and retraction in a DFA-based scanner are true? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The scanner remembers the furthest position at which some pattern was accepted.", "Retraction means the characters are read again from the file.", "One character of lookahead past the end of a token may be needed to decide that the token has ended.", "Retraction is a pointer move; the characters stay in the buffer.", "A DFA-based scanner never needs any lookahead."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A deterministic scanner advances while the DFA keeps accepting, and keeps a record of the last accepting position so that it can retreat cheaply when the automaton dies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The last accepting position is stored: true, and it is what makes retraction cheap.</li><li>Re-reading from the file: false; the characters are already in the buffer.</li><li>One character of lookahead may be needed: true — for example, <code class=\"mm\">[0-9]+</code> cannot know the number has ended until it sees a non-digit.</li><li>Retraction is a pointer move: true.</li><li>No lookahead ever: false; the scanner must see the character that terminates the token.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (C) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking lookahead means unbounded backtracking. For a DFA scanner the lookahead is bounded by the longest token and retraction is a single pointer assignment.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U1-Q50", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 1, "unit_slug": "lexical-analysis", "subtopic": "input-buffering-lookahead", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-26", "text": "A DFA-based scanner has consumed 6 characters of the input. The last accepting state it passed through was reached after 4 characters, and from the current state no accepting state is reachable. How many characters must it retract before the next token starts? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Retraction distance = (characters consumed) &minus; (position of the last accepting state). The scanner gives back everything past the accepted lexeme.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Characters consumed: 6.</li><li>Position of the last accepting state: 4, so the token is 4 characters wide.</li><li>Characters read past the token: 6 &minus; 4 = 2.</li><li>The scanner resets its pointer to position 5, so it retracts 2 characters.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 2</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 6 or 4. The question asks for the number of characters handed back, not the length of the token or of the read-ahead.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q01", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Consider the grammar S &rarr; a S b | &epsilon; over the alphabet {a, b}. Which one of the following is FIRST(S)?", "options": ["{a}", "{a, &epsilon;}", "{a, b}", "{a, b, &epsilon;}"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(&alpha;)</span> is the set of terminals that can begin a string derived from <span class=\"mm\">&alpha;</span>, plus <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> when <span class=\"mm\">&alpha;</span> derives the empty string. It is computed production by production: a terminal contributes itself, and a nonterminal contributes the FIRST sets of its own alternatives.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The alternative <span class=\"mm\">a S b</span> begins with the terminal <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">a &isin; FIRST(S)</span>.</li><li>The alternative <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> derives the empty string, so <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon; &isin; FIRST(S)</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">b</span> is never the first symbol of any right side, so it is not in <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(S)</span> — it only appears after <span class=\"mm\">S</span> has already produced something.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(S) = {a, &epsilon;}</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: {a, &epsilon;} — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding <span class=\"mm\">b</span> because it occurs in the grammar. <span class=\"mm\">b</span> follows <span class=\"mm\">S</span>, which makes it a member of <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S)</span>, not of <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(S)</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q02", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar E &rarr; E + T | T, T &rarr; T * F | F, F &rarr; ( E ) | id, immediate left recursion is removed from E alone. The productions for E and for the new nonterminal E&prime; are then", "options": ["E &rarr; T E&prime;, E&prime; &rarr; + T E&prime; | &epsilon;", "E &rarr; E&prime; T, E&prime; &rarr; T + E&prime; | &epsilon;", "E &rarr; T E&prime;, E&prime; &rarr; T + E&prime; | &epsilon;", "E &rarr; T + E&prime; | T, E&prime; &rarr; E&prime; + T | &epsilon;"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A rule of the form <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; A &alpha; | &beta;</span> generates the strings <span class=\"mm\">&beta; &alpha;*</span>. Replacing it by <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &beta; A&prime;</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A&prime; &rarr; &alpha; A&prime; | &epsilon;</span> produces exactly the same language without the left recursion.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Here <span class=\"mm\">A = E</span>, <span class=\"mm\">&alpha; = + T</span> and <span class=\"mm\">&beta; = T</span>.</li><li>The recursive alternative <span class=\"mm\">E &rarr; E + T</span> is rewritten so that the recursion moves to the right: <span class=\"mm\">E&prime; &rarr; + T E&prime;</span>.</li><li>The base alternative becomes <span class=\"mm\">E &rarr; T E&prime;</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">E&prime; &rarr; &epsilon;</span> terminates the repetition.</li><li>Option C reverses the order inside <span class=\"mm\">E&prime;</span> to <span class=\"mm\">T + E&prime;</span>, which generates <span class=\"mm\">T T + &hellip;</span> — a different language. Option B moves <span class=\"mm\">E&prime;</span> to the wrong side. Option D leaves the recursion in place.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: E &rarr; T E&prime;, E&prime; &rarr; + T E&prime; | &epsilon; — option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the order of the symbols while \"moving\" the recursion. The recursive tail must stay exactly as it was — <span class=\"mm\">+ T</span>, in that order — and only its head changes from <span class=\"mm\">E</span> to <span class=\"mm\">E&prime;</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q03", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The grammar A &rarr; A a | A b | c is transformed by removing immediate left recursion, which yields A &rarr; c A&prime; together with the productions of A&prime;. How many productions does the transformed grammar contain in total — the productions for A plus all the productions for A&prime;? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; A &alpha;<sub>1</sub> | &hellip; | A &alpha;<sub>m</sub> | &beta;<sub>1</sub> | &hellip; | &beta;<sub>n</sub></span> the elimination gives <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &beta;<sub>1</sub> A&prime; | &hellip; | &beta;<sub>n</sub> A&prime;</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A&prime; &rarr; &alpha;<sub>1</sub> A&prime; | &hellip; | &alpha;<sub>m</sub> A&prime; | &epsilon;</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Two recursive alternatives, so <span class=\"mm\">m = 2</span>: <span class=\"mm\">a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li>One base alternative, so <span class=\"mm\">n = 1</span>: <span class=\"mm\">c</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">A</span> keeps one production: <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; c A&prime;</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">A&prime;</span> gets <span class=\"mm\">A&prime; &rarr; a A&prime;</span>, <span class=\"mm\">A&prime; &rarr; b A&prime;</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A&prime; &rarr; &epsilon;</span> — three productions.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">1 + 3 = 4</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 4 productions.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-production of <span class=\"mm\">A&prime;</span>, or merging the two recursive alternatives into a single <span class=\"mm\">A&prime; &rarr; a | b A&prime;</span> line. Each alternative of the original rule stays a separate production of <span class=\"mm\">A&prime;</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> is one more.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q04", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S &rarr; A B, A &rarr; a | &epsilon;, B &rarr; b, the set FOLLOW(A) is", "options": ["{a}", "{b}", "{b, $}", "{$}"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</span> is the set of terminals that can appear immediately to the right of <span class=\"mm\">A</span> in some sentential form, plus <span class=\"mm\">$</span> if <span class=\"mm\">A</span> can end a sentential form. It is read off from where <span class=\"mm\">A</span> occurs on right sides.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">A</span> occurs in <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; A B</span>, followed by <span class=\"mm\">B</span>.</li><li>So everything in <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(B)</span> belongs to <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</span>. <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(B) = {b}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">B</span> is not nullable, so the symbols that follow <span class=\"mm\">S</span> do not flow into <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</span>; <span class=\"mm\">$</span> stays out.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A) = {b}</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: {b} — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding <span class=\"mm\">$</span> to every FOLLOW set. <span class=\"mm\">$</span> reaches <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</span> only when the rest of the right side after <span class=\"mm\">A</span> can vanish entirely — here it cannot.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q05", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about LL(1) grammars are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["No left-recursive grammar is LL(1).", "For any nonterminal, the FIRST sets of two distinct alternatives are disjoint, and where one alternative is nullable its FOLLOW set is disjoint from the FIRST sets of the other alternatives.", "Every LL(1) grammar is unambiguous.", "Every unambiguous grammar is LL(1).", "An LL(1) parser sometimes has to backtrack over tokens it has already read."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A grammar is LL(1) when one token of lookahead always determines which alternative to expand. That forces the FIRST sets of the alternatives of a nonterminal to be disjoint, and, when an alternative can vanish, forces its FOLLOW set to be disjoint from the FIRST sets of the others.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: in <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; A &alpha; | &beta;</span> both alternatives can begin with the same string (namely <span class=\"mm\">&beta; &alpha;</span>&hellip;), so the FIRST sets collide and no finite lookahead separates them. Every left-recursive grammar has such a nonterminal somewhere on a cycle.</li><li>(B) true: this is exactly the LL(1) condition; it is what makes each table cell hold at most one entry.</li><li>(C) true: if the grammar were ambiguous, two distinct leftmost derivations would share a prefix, so the two productions applied at the divergence point would have to be told apart by the same lookahead — a table conflict.</li><li>(D) false: the classic dangling-else grammar is unambiguous but is not LL(1). Unambiguity alone does not give one-token decisions.</li><li>(E) false: an LL(1) parser never backtracks; the table decides every step.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating \"unambiguous\" and \"LL(1)\" as synonyms. LL(1) is a property of the grammar plus a one-token decision procedure; unambiguous grammars that need two tokens of lookahead, or that are left-recursive, fall outside it.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q06", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Consider the dangling-else grammar S &rarr; i E t S S&prime; | a, S&prime; &rarr; e S | &epsilon;, E &rarr; b, where i, t and e stand for if, then and else. In its LL(1) parsing table, which cell receives two entries?", "options": ["M[S, i]", "M[S&prime;, e]", "M[E, b]", "None — the grammar is LL(1)"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A cell <span class=\"mm\">M[A, a]</span> is filled by the alternative <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &alpha;</span> when <span class=\"mm\">a &isin; FIRST(&alpha;)</span>, and by <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &epsilon;</span> for every <span class=\"mm\">a &isin; FOLLOW(A)</span>. Two entries in one cell mean the grammar is not LL(1).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">S&prime;</span> occurs at the very end of <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; i E t S S&prime;</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S&prime;) &supe; FOLLOW(S) = {e, $}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">FIRST(e S) = {e}</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">M[S&prime;, e]</span> receives <span class=\"mm\">S&prime; &rarr; e S</span>.</li><li>Because <span class=\"mm\">S&prime;</span> is nullable, <span class=\"mm\">S&prime; &rarr; &epsilon;</span> is entered in every cell of <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S&prime;)</span> — and <span class=\"mm\">e</span> is one of them.</li><li>So <span class=\"mm\">M[S&prime;, e]</span> holds both <span class=\"mm\">S&prime; &rarr; e S</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S&prime; &rarr; &epsilon;</span>: the parser cannot tell, from the token <span class=\"mm\">else</span>, whether it belongs to the inner or the outer <span class=\"mm\">if</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: M[S&prime;, e] — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting the conflict at <span class=\"mm\">M[S, i]</span> because the grammar is \"about\" the two <span class=\"mm\">if</span> rules. The clash is on the token <span class=\"mm\">else</span>: it is in <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(e S)</span> and also in <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S&prime;)</span>, which is what an &epsilon;-production needs.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q07", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S &rarr; A B, A &rarr; a A | &epsilon;, B &rarr; b B | &epsilon;, how many cells of the LL(1) parsing table are non-empty? Count a cell once however many entries it holds. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Fill <span class=\"mm\">M[A, a]</span> from <span class=\"mm\">FIRST</span> for every alternative that begins with a terminal, and place <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &epsilon;</span> in every cell <span class=\"mm\">M[A, b]</span> with <span class=\"mm\">b &isin; FOLLOW(A)</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Sets: <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A) = {a, &epsilon;}</span>, <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(B) = {b, &epsilon;}</span>, <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(S) = {a, b, &epsilon;}</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S) = {$}</span>; <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A) = FIRST(B) &minus; {&epsilon;} &cup; FOLLOW(S) = {b, $}</span>; <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(B) = FOLLOW(S) = {$}</span>.</li><li>Row <span class=\"mm\">S</span>: <span class=\"mm\">M[S, a] = S &rarr; A B</span> (from <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A)</span>), <span class=\"mm\">M[S, b] = S &rarr; A B</span> (A nullable, <span class=\"mm\">b &isin; FIRST(B)</span>), <span class=\"mm\">M[S, $] = S &rarr; A B</span> (both nullable). Three cells.</li><li>Row <span class=\"mm\">A</span>: <span class=\"mm\">M[A, a] = A &rarr; a A</span>, <span class=\"mm\">M[A, b] = A &rarr; &epsilon;</span>, <span class=\"mm\">M[A, $] = A &rarr; &epsilon;</span>. Three cells.</li><li>Row <span class=\"mm\">B</span>: <span class=\"mm\">M[B, b] = B &rarr; b B</span>, <span class=\"mm\">M[B, $] = B &rarr; &epsilon;</span>. Two cells.</li><li>Total <span class=\"mm\">3 + 3 + 2 = 8</span>. No cell holds two entries, so the grammar is LL(1).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8 non-empty cells.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &epsilon;</span> in a single cell labelled <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>. The &epsilon;-production is duplicated into every terminal of the FOLLOW set — that duplication is exactly what can create a conflict.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q08", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S &rarr; A B, A &rarr; a | &epsilon;, B &rarr; b | &epsilon;, the set FIRST(S) is", "options": ["{a, b}", "{a, b, &epsilon;}", "{a, &epsilon;}", "{a, b, $}"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> For a right side <span class=\"mm\">X<sub>1</sub> X<sub>2</sub> &hellip; X<sub>n</sub></span>, add <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(X<sub>1</sub>) &minus; {&epsilon;}</span>; add <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(X<sub>2</sub>) &minus; {&epsilon;}</span> as well only if <span class=\"mm\">X<sub>1</sub></span> is nullable; and add <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> only if every <span class=\"mm\">X<sub>i</sub></span> is nullable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A) = {a, &epsilon;}</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">a &isin; FIRST(S)</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">A</span> is nullable, so <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(B) &minus; {&epsilon;} = {b}</span> also enters <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(S)</span>.</li><li>Both <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> are nullable, so <span class=\"mm\">A B</span> derives <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> and <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon; &isin; FIRST(S)</span>.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(S) = {a, b, &epsilon;}</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: {a, b, &epsilon;} — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping at the first symbol that has a non-empty FIRST set. The second symbol contributes only because the first is nullable — but it does contribute.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q09", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A recursive-descent parser for an LL(1) grammar must choose one alternative of A when the next input token is x. It chooses the alternative &alpha; such that", "options": ["x &isin; FIRST(&alpha;); if no alternative satisfies this and A is nullable, it takes A &rarr; &epsilon; whenever x &isin; FOLLOW(A).", "x &isin; FOLLOW(&alpha;) for every alternative &alpha; of A.", "x &isin; FIRST(A), taking the first alternative listed whenever several qualify.", "it tries every alternative in turn and backtracks when one fails."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In predictive (LL(1)) parsing the lookahead token must identify the alternative uniquely: the token is either one that the alternative can start with, or — when the alternative is <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> — one that may legitimately follow the nonterminal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If <span class=\"mm\">x &isin; FIRST(&alpha;)</span> the alternative can begin with <span class=\"mm\">x</span>, so expanding it is correct.</li><li>If no alternative can begin with <span class=\"mm\">x</span> but <span class=\"mm\">x &isin; FOLLOW(A)</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">A</span> must produce nothing here and the parser takes <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &epsilon;</span>.</li><li>LL(1) guarantees these two tests never both apply and never leave the choice open, so no backtracking is needed (option D is the definition of a non-predictive parser).</li><li><span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(&alpha;)</span> (option B) is not defined — FOLLOW is a property of a nonterminal, not of a right side.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: the FIRST test, with the FOLLOW test for the nullable case — option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A)</span> instead of <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(&alpha;)</span>. <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A)</span> is the union over all alternatives, so it cannot distinguish them — which is precisely the information the parser needs.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q10", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The grammar A &rarr; a b c | a b d | a e is fully left factored. The result is", "options": ["A &rarr; a A&prime;, A&prime; &rarr; b c | b d | e", "A &rarr; a A&prime;, A&prime; &rarr; b A&Prime; | e, A&Prime; &rarr; c | d", "A &rarr; a A&prime;, A&prime; &rarr; b A&Prime; | e A&Prime;&prime;, A&Prime; &rarr; c | d", "A &rarr; A&prime; a, A&prime; &rarr; b c | b d | e"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Left factoring pushes the longest common prefix of two alternatives into a new nonterminal, so that one token of lookahead decides between the remainders. It is applied repeatedly until no two alternatives of the same nonterminal share a first symbol.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All three alternatives begin with <span class=\"mm\">a</span>, so factor it once: <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; a A&prime;</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A&prime; &rarr; b c | b d | e</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">A&prime;</span> still has two alternatives beginning with <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, so factor again: <span class=\"mm\">A&prime; &rarr; b A&Prime; | e</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A&Prime; &rarr; c | d</span>.</li><li>Now every nonterminal has alternatives with distinct FIRST sets: <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(c) = {c}</span>, <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(d) = {d}</span>, <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(b A&Prime;) = {b}</span>, <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(e) = {e}</span>. Factoring is complete.</li><li>Option A stops after one step and leaves a FIRST/FIRST conflict on <span class=\"mm\">b</span>; option C introduces a needless nonterminal for <span class=\"mm\">e</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: A &rarr; a A&prime;, A&prime; &rarr; b A&Prime; | e, A&Prime; &rarr; c | d — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Factoring only the outer common symbol. A single pass leaves the inner pair <span class=\"mm\">b c</span> and <span class=\"mm\">b d</span> still ambiguous on <span class=\"mm\">b</span>; factoring is applied until no FIRST conflict remains.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q11", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following transformations leave the language generated by a grammar unchanged? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Replacing A &rarr; A &alpha; | &beta;, where &beta; does not begin with A, by A &rarr; &beta; A&prime;, A&prime; &rarr; &alpha; A&prime; | &epsilon;.", "Left factoring two alternatives that share a common prefix.", "Deleting every &epsilon;-production of the grammar.", "Deleting useless symbols, that is, non-generating and unreachable symbols.", "Reordering the alternatives of a non-LL(1) grammar until it becomes LL(1)."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A transformation is language-preserving when the set of terminal strings derivable from the start symbol is unchanged. Left-recursion elimination and left factoring are such transformations; deleting &epsilon;-productions and reordering alternatives are not, in general.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; A &alpha; | &beta;</span> generates <span class=\"mm\">&beta; &alpha;<sup>k</sup></span> for <span class=\"mm\">k &ge; 0</span>, and so does <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &beta; A&prime;</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A&prime; &rarr; &alpha; A&prime; | &epsilon;</span>.</li><li>(B) true: factoring only regroups alternatives, it derives exactly the same strings.</li><li>(C) false: dropping every &epsilon;-production removes <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> from the language and breaks every derivation that relied on an optional part; a correct elimination must add compensating productions instead of merely deleting.</li><li>(D) true: a symbol that generates no terminal string, or that can never be reached from the start symbol, contributes nothing to the language.</li><li>(E) false: reordering alternatives cannot remove a FIRST/FIRST or FIRST/FOLLOW conflict; the ambiguity stays in the grammar. Only rewriting the rules helps.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing every \"clean-up\" step is safe. &epsilon;-elimination is language-preserving only when it is done properly — replicating the nullable symbols into the other right sides — and even then <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> is lost unless the start symbol is nullable.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q12", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A predictive parser uses the grammar E &rarr; T E&prime;, E&prime; &rarr; + T E&prime; | &epsilon;, T &rarr; F T&prime;, T&prime; &rarr; * F T&prime; | &epsilon;, F &rarr; ( E ) | id. For the input id + id it applies a sequence of productions. How many productions are applied in total, counting each &epsilon;-production that is applied? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "9", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A predictive parser expands nonterminals left to right, and each expansion is the application of exactly one production. The count is therefore the number of nonterminal expansions in the leftmost derivation of the input, including the expansions of the &prime;-nonterminals to <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">E &rarr; T E&prime;</span> — 1.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">T &rarr; F T&prime;</span> — 2.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">F &rarr; id</span>, matching the first <span class=\"mm\">id</span> — 3.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">T&prime; &rarr; &epsilon;</span>, since the lookahead is <span class=\"mm\">+</span> and <span class=\"mm\">+ &isin; FOLLOW(T&prime;)</span> — 4.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">E&prime; &rarr; + T E&prime;</span>, consuming the <span class=\"mm\">+</span> — 5.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">T &rarr; F T&prime;</span> — 6.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">F &rarr; id</span>, matching the second <span class=\"mm\">id</span> — 7.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">T&prime; &rarr; &epsilon;</span>, lookahead <span class=\"mm\">$</span> — 8.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">E&prime; &rarr; &epsilon;</span>, lookahead <span class=\"mm\">$</span> — 9.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 9 productions.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting only the \"visible\" productions and dropping the two <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-expansions. Every nonterminal in the sentential form must be expanded, and a nonterminal that disappears by <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> still costs one production.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q13", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A grammar is LL(1) if and only if, for every pair of distinct alternatives &alpha; and &beta; of a nonterminal A,", "options": ["FIRST(&alpha;) &cap; FIRST(&beta;) = &empty;, and if &alpha; &rArr;* &epsilon; then FIRST(&beta;) &cap; FOLLOW(A) = &empty; (and symmetrically for &beta;).", "FIRST(&alpha;) &cap; FOLLOW(A) = &empty; for both alternatives.", "FIRST(&alpha;) &cap; FIRST(&beta;) = &empty; and FOLLOW(A) = &empty;.", "the grammar contains no &epsilon;-production."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The LL(1) condition is exactly the statement that no cell of the parsing table receives two entries. Two ways of colliding are possible: two alternatives can start with the same terminal, or a nullable alternative can vanish on a terminal that another alternative can start with.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">FIRST(&alpha;) &cap; FIRST(&beta;) = &empty;</span> rules out the first kind of collision: no terminal selects two alternatives by starting them.</li><li>If <span class=\"mm\">&alpha; &rArr;* &epsilon;</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; &alpha;</span> is entered in every cell <span class=\"mm\">M[A, b]</span> with <span class=\"mm\">b &isin; FOLLOW(A)</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(&beta;)</span> must avoid <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</span>.</li><li>Options B and C are too strong: <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</span> may be non-empty, it just must not overlap the FIRST sets of the other alternatives.</li><li>Option D is false: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; a S b | &epsilon;</span> contains an <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-production and is LL(1).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking LL(1) forbids &epsilon;-productions. It only requires that the &epsilon;-alternative be distinguishable from the others by the lookahead, which is what the FOLLOW test checks.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q14", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S &rarr; A B, A &rarr; &epsilon; | a, B &rarr; b | &epsilon;, the set FIRST(B) is", "options": ["{b}", "{b, &epsilon;}", "{&epsilon;}", "{a, b, &epsilon;}"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">FIRST</span> of a nonterminal is the union of the FIRST sets of its alternatives, taken one production at a time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; b</span> contributes <span class=\"mm\">b</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; &epsilon;</span> contributes <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>.</li><li>No other production has <span class=\"mm\">B</span> on the left.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(B) = {b, &epsilon;}</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: {b, &epsilon;} — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the alternatives of <span class=\"mm\">A</span> into <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(B)</span>, or writing <span class=\"mm\">{&epsilon;}</span> alone because <span class=\"mm\">B</span> is nullable. Nullability adds <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>; it does not remove the terminals.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q15", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about recursive-descent parsing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Without memoisation, a backtracking recursive-descent parser can take time exponential in the input length on some grammars.", "For an LL(1) grammar one token of lookahead always selects the alternative, so no backtracking is needed.", "Recursive descent handles a left-recursive grammar directly, because the recursion in the parser mirrors the recursion in the grammar.", "Left recursion must be removed before a recursive-descent parser is written.", "A recursive-descent parser builds the parse tree bottom-up, from the leaves to the root."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A recursive-descent parser has one procedure per nonterminal, and a procedure calls the procedures of the symbols on the right side of the alternative it chooses. It is therefore a top-down, leftmost-derivation parser, and its control flow copies the shape of the grammar.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: with backtracking, each failed alternative may re-parse the same suffix, and the number of partial parses can grow exponentially — the classical reason backtracking descent is avoided in production compilers.</li><li>(B) true: LL(1) means the parsing table has at most one entry per cell, and a recursive-descent parser implements that table directly.</li><li>(C) false: for <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; A &alpha; | &beta;</span> the procedure for <span class=\"mm\">A</span> calls itself before consuming any input, so it recurses forever without making progress.</li><li>(D) true: that is why the elimination of Unit-2 left-recursion removal precedes the parser construction.</li><li>(E) false: bottom-up parsing (shift-reduce) builds the tree from the leaves; recursive descent builds it from the root downwards.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the parser's recursion can \"handle\" the grammar's recursion. The parser mirrors the grammar, so a left-recursive rule becomes an infinite loop rather than a left-associative parse.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q16", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In the grammar F &rarr; ( E ) | id, how many terminals belong to FIRST(F)? (Enter an integer. Do not count &epsilon;, since F is not nullable.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(F)</span> is the set of terminals that can begin a string derived from <span class=\"mm\">F</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">F &rarr; ( E )</span> begins with the terminal <span class=\"mm\">(</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">F &rarr; id</span> begins with the terminal <span class=\"mm\">id</span>.</li><li>Neither alternative is nullable, so <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon; &notin; FIRST(F)</span>.</li><li>Hence <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(F) = {(, id}</span> — two terminals.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2 terminals.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <span class=\"mm\">)</span> or <span class=\"mm\">E</span>. <span class=\"mm\">)</span> comes after <span class=\"mm\">E</span> and never begins a string of <span class=\"mm\">F</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">E</span> is a nonterminal, not a terminal.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q17", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S &rarr; A a | b, A &rarr; S d | c, indirect left recursion is removed by substituting the S-productions into A and then removing the immediate left recursion. The resulting productions for A are", "options": ["A &rarr; A a d | b d | c, with no further step needed", "A &rarr; b d A&prime; | c A&prime;, A&prime; &rarr; a d A&prime; | &epsilon;", "A &rarr; b d | c, A&prime; &rarr; a d A&prime;", "A &rarr; S d | c, unchanged, because the recursion is in S"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Indirect (mutual) left recursion is removed in two stages: substitute the earlier nonterminal's alternatives into the later one, which turns the indirect recursion into an immediate one, and then apply the standard immediate-elimination rule.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take the order <span class=\"mm\">S</span> then <span class=\"mm\">A</span>. In <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; S d</span>, replace <span class=\"mm\">S</span> by its alternatives: <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; A a d | b d</span>, together with the existing <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; c</span>.</li><li>Now <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is immediately left recursive with <span class=\"mm\">&alpha; = a d</span> and <span class=\"mm\">&beta; &isin; {b d, c}</span>.</li><li>Apply the rule: <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; b d A&prime; | c A&prime;</span> and <span class=\"mm\">A&prime; &rarr; a d A&prime; | &epsilon;</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">S</span> itself is not left recursive and stays as it is.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: A &rarr; b d A&prime; | c A&prime;, A&prime; &rarr; a d A&prime; | &epsilon; — option B.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping after the substitution (option A) — the grammar is then still left recursive, only immediately so — or forgetting that the substituted alternative <span class=\"mm\">b d</span> also needs the <span class=\"mm\">A&prime;</span> suffix.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q18", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following statements about FIRST and FOLLOW sets is FALSE?", "options": ["FOLLOW(A) can contain $ only if A can occur as the rightmost symbol of a sentential form.", "If A is nullable and occurs at the end of a production, then FOLLOW(A) contains the FOLLOW of the head of that production.", "FIRST(A) contains &epsilon; exactly when A is nullable.", "FOLLOW(A) contains &epsilon; whenever A is nullable."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <span class=\"mm\">FIRST</span> describes what a string can begin with, and <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> is a legitimate \"beginning\" of a nullable string. <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW</span> describes what can come <i>after</i> a nonterminal in a sentential form; its symbols are always terminals or the end marker <span class=\"mm\">$</span>, never <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: <span class=\"mm\">$</span> enters <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</span> only through the propagation rule for a symbol at the right end of a production, which ultimately traces back to <span class=\"mm\">S</span>.</li><li>(B) true: this is the standard propagation rule, including the chain through nullable symbols.</li><li>(C) true: it is the definition of nullability expressed through FIRST.</li><li>(D) false: <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</span> is a set of terminals and <span class=\"mm\">$</span>. Nullability puts <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> in <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A)</span> and is handled in the table by copying the <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>-production across <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</span>, not by putting <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> inside it.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option D is false.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Symmetry. FIRST and FOLLOW look similar but are asymmetric: FIRST may hold <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>, FOLLOW never does; FOLLOW holds <span class=\"mm\">$</span>, FIRST never does.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q19", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following grammars are LL(1)? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["S &rarr; a S b | &epsilon;", "S &rarr; A a | b, A &rarr; b | &epsilon;", "S &rarr; a A | b B, A &rarr; c | &epsilon;, B &rarr; c | &epsilon;", "E &rarr; E + T | T", "S &rarr; i E t S S&prime; | a, S&prime; &rarr; e S | &epsilon;, E &rarr; b"], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Test each nonterminal: the FIRST sets of its alternatives must be disjoint, and where an alternative is nullable its FOLLOW set must not overlap the FIRST sets of the others. A grammar is LL(1) only if every nonterminal passes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; a S b | &epsilon;</span>: <span class=\"mm\">FIRST</span> sets are <span class=\"mm\">{a}</span> and <span class=\"mm\">{&epsilon;}</span>; <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S) = {b, $}</span> does not contain <span class=\"mm\">a</span>. LL(1).</li><li><span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; A a | b</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; b | &epsilon;</span>: <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A a) = {b, a}</span> because <span class=\"mm\">A</span> can be <span class=\"mm\">b</span> or <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(b) = {b}</span>. Both contain <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">M[S, b]</span> gets two entries. Not LL(1).</li><li><span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; a A | b B</span>, <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; c | &epsilon;</span>, <span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; c | &epsilon;</span>: <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A) = FOLLOW(B) = {$}</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">M[A, c] = A &rarr; c</span>, <span class=\"mm\">M[A, $] = A &rarr; &epsilon;</span> and likewise for <span class=\"mm\">B</span>. The two nonterminals never share a row, so no conflict. LL(1).</li><li><span class=\"mm\">E &rarr; E + T | T</span> is left recursive: the two alternatives of <span class=\"mm\">E</span> have the same FIRST set. Not LL(1).</li><li>The dangling-else grammar has two entries in <span class=\"mm\">M[S&prime;, e]</span>. Not LL(1).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Judging by the look of the rules. Option C looks suspicious because <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> have identical right sides, yet it is LL(1): conflicts are per row of the table, and <span class=\"mm\">A</span> and <span class=\"mm\">B</span> occupy different rows.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q20", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S &rarr; A B C, A &rarr; a | &epsilon;, B &rarr; b | &epsilon;, C &rarr; c | &epsilon;, how many symbols does FOLLOW(A) contain? Count $ as a symbol. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is followed by a string of nullable symbols, every terminal that can begin one of them follows <span class=\"mm\">A</span>, and if all of them can vanish, the FOLLOW of the head propagates as well.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">A</span> occurs in <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; A B C</span>, followed by <span class=\"mm\">B C</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">FIRST(B) &minus; {&epsilon;} = {b}</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">b &isin; FOLLOW(A)</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">B</span> is nullable, so <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(C) &minus; {&epsilon;} = {c}</span> also enters: <span class=\"mm\">c &isin; FOLLOW(A)</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">C</span> is nullable too, so the rest of the right side can vanish completely and <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S) = {$}</span> propagates: <span class=\"mm\">$ &isin; FOLLOW(A)</span>.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A) = {b, c, $}</span>, three symbols.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 3 symbols.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2 by stopping at <span class=\"mm\">B</span>, or answering 4 by counting <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span>. Nullability is what carries the FOLLOW of the head into <span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</span>, and <span class=\"mm\">&epsilon;</span> is never a member of a FOLLOW set.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q21", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a table-driven predictive parser the symbol on top of the stack is the nonterminal A, the current input symbol is a, and M[A, a] = A &rarr; X Y Z. The parser", "options": ["pops A and pushes Z, then Y, then X, so that X is on top.", "pops a from the input and pushes A.", "shifts a onto the stack.", "reduces the handle X Y Z to A."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A predictive parser keeps a stack of grammar symbols with the start symbol at the bottom. When the top is a nonterminal it expands it using the table; when the top is a terminal it must match the current input symbol and is popped.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The stack is <span class=\"mm\">&hellip; A</span> with <span class=\"mm\">A</span> on top, so <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is popped.</li><li>The right side <span class=\"mm\">X Y Z</span> is pushed in reverse order — <span class=\"mm\">Z</span> first, then <span class=\"mm\">Y</span>, then <span class=\"mm\">X</span> — so that <span class=\"mm\">X</span>, the leftmost symbol, ends up on top and is matched next.</li><li>Nothing is consumed from the input at this step; the input pointer moves only when a terminal on top matches.</li><li>Options B, C and D describe shift-reduce (bottom-up) behaviour, not predictive parsing.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Pushing the right side left to right. That would put <span class=\"mm\">Z</span> on top and the parser would try to match the rightmost symbol first — a rightmost, not leftmost, derivation.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q22", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A backtracking recursive-descent parser is used on a grammar with no LL(1) guarantee. In the worst case, for an input of length n, the work it may do grows", "options": ["linearly in n", "as n log n", "exponentially in n", "as n&sup2;"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A predictive (LL(1)) parser is linear because the table fixes every decision. A backtracking parser instead explores alternatives, and when several alternatives can match the same prefix the number of partial parses can double with every token.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>On failure the parser restores the input position and tries the next alternative, re-parsing the same suffix.</li><li>If the grammar allows <span class=\"mm\">k</span> alternatives at each of <span class=\"mm\">n</span> levels, the search tree has on the order of <span class=\"mm\">k<sup>n</sup></span> nodes.</li><li>Classic examples, such as the ambiguous expression grammar, show this exponential blow-up in practice.</li><li>Memoisation (packrat parsing) removes the repetition and restores linear time at the cost of memory — but that is no longer plain backtracking.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: exponentially in n — option C.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any parser is linear in the input. Linearity is a property of the deterministic table-driven method, not of top-down parsing in general.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q23", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about left recursion and left factoring are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Left factoring can turn a grammar that is not LL(1) into an LL(1) grammar without changing the language.", "Removing left recursion always yields an LL(1) grammar.", "Indirect left recursion must be removed by substituting in a fixed order of nonterminals before the immediate left recursion is removed.", "A recursive-descent parser that meets a left-recursive alternative recurses forever.", "Left factoring introduces no new nonterminals."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Left recursion and FIRST/FIRST conflicts are the two obstacles to predictive parsing. Left-recursion elimination removes the first; left factoring removes the second. Neither is a complete LL(1) transformation by itself.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: a grammar whose only defect is a shared prefix becomes LL(1) after factoring, and factoring preserves the language.</li><li>(B) false: <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; a A | b B</span>, <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; c | &epsilon;</span>, <span class=\"mm\">B &rarr; c | &epsilon;</span> is free of left recursion and is LL(1) — but <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; A a | b</span>, <span class=\"mm\">A &rarr; b | &epsilon;</span> is also free of left recursion and is <i>not</i> LL(1), because of a FIRST/FIRST conflict.</li><li>(C) true: the substitution step turns mutual recursion into immediate recursion, and it needs a fixed order (for example <span class=\"mm\">A<sub>1</sub>, &hellip;, A<sub>n</sub></span>) or the process does not terminate.</li><li>(D) true: the procedure for <span class=\"mm\">A</span> calls itself before consuming input.</li><li>(E) false: factoring creates a new nonterminal (here <span class=\"mm\">A&prime;</span>) for every common prefix it extracts.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: options (A), (C) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing \"no left recursion\" implies \"LL(1)\". FIRST/FIRST and FIRST/FOLLOW conflicts survive left-recursion removal untouched.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q24", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S &rarr; A a | b, A &rarr; b | &epsilon;, which statement about its LL(1) parsing table is TRUE?", "options": ["Exactly one cell, M[S, b], holds two entries, so the grammar is not LL(1).", "The grammar is LL(1), because the two alternatives of A begin differently.", "M[A, a] holds A &rarr; b.", "M[A, $] holds A &rarr; &epsilon;."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The FIRST set of a right side is computed left to right, and a nullable first symbol lets the following symbols contribute. It is <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A a)</span>, not <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A)</span>, that decides the row of <span class=\"mm\">S</span>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A) = {b, &epsilon;}</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">FIRST(A a) = {b} &cup; {a} = {b, a}</span>: <span class=\"mm\">A</span> may be <span class=\"mm\">b</span>, or it may vanish and let <span class=\"mm\">a</span> begin the string.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">FIRST(b) = {b}</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">M[S, b]</span> receives both <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; A a</span> and <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; b</span>. That is the conflict.</li><li><span class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A) = {a}</span>, so <span class=\"mm\">M[A, b] = A &rarr; b</span> and <span class=\"mm\">M[A, a] = A &rarr; &epsilon;</span>; <span class=\"mm\">M[A, $]</span> stays empty.</li><li>So options C and D name cells that hold the wrong entry, and option B ignores the conflict in row <span class=\"mm\">S</span>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: M[S, b] holds two entries — option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Checking only the alternatives of the \"suspicious\" nonterminal <span class=\"mm\">A</span>. Here <span class=\"mm\">A</span> is perfectly well behaved; the conflict is created one level up, by the nullability of <span class=\"mm\">A</span> inside <span class=\"mm\">S &rarr; A a</span>.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q25", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following is true of every top-down parser?", "options": ["It builds a leftmost derivation of the input string.", "It builds a rightmost derivation in reverse.", "It reduces handles at the top of the stack.", "It can parse every context-free grammar."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A top-down parser starts at the start symbol and expands nonterminals until the frontier matches the input. Expanding the leftmost nonterminal at every step produces a leftmost derivation, so the parse tree grows from the root towards the leaves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Recursive descent and LL(1) table-driven parsing both expand the leftmost nonterminal, giving a leftmost derivation — statement A.</li><li>Statement B describes bottom-up (shift-reduce) parsing, which reconstructs a rightmost derivation in reverse.</li><li>Statement C is the handle-reduction step of LR parsing, again bottom-up.</li><li>Statement D is false: a top-down parser needs a grammar free of left recursion and FIRST/FIRST conflicts; many context-free grammars, including all ambiguous ones, are outside its reach.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: option A.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing \"top-down\" with \"backtracking\" or with \"handles\". Top-down means root-to-leaves and leftmost expansion; handles and rightmost derivations belong to the bottom-up family.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q26", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Consider the grammar S → A b, A → c | ε. Which one of the following is FIRST(S)?", "options": ["{c}", "{c, b}", "{c, b, ε}", "{b}"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">FIRST</code> of a string is read left to right: the first symbol contributes its own FIRST set without ε, and only when that symbol is nullable does the next symbol contribute.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">FIRST(A) = {c, ε}</code>, because the alternative <code class=\"mm\">c</code> gives the terminal <code class=\"mm\">c</code> and the alternative <code class=\"mm\">ε</code> makes A nullable.</li><li>In <code class=\"mm\">S → A b</code>, A comes first, so <code class=\"mm\">c ∈ FIRST(S)</code>; ε itself never enters <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(S)</code>.</li><li>Because A is nullable, the scan moves on to <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">b ∈ FIRST(S)</code>.</li><li>S itself derives no empty string, so ε is not in <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(S)</code>. Hence <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(S) = {c, b}</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: {c, b} — option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing <code class=\"mm\">{c, b, ε}</code> because A is nullable. ε belongs to <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(A)</code>, not to <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(S)</code>; a nullable symbol passes the scan to the next symbol, it does not pass ε along.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q27", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a table-driven predictive parser the symbol on top of the stack is a terminal that equals the current input symbol. The parser must", "options": ["replace the stack top by the right side of a production.", "pop the terminal from the stack and advance the input to the next symbol.", "report a syntax error, because a terminal can never reach the top of the stack.", "push the current input symbol onto the stack and continue."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The parser has three actions only: expand a nonterminal using the table, match a terminal against the input, and report an error. A match consumes one symbol from each side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The stack top is a terminal and the input symbol equals it, so the two agree on the next symbol of the sentential form.</li><li>The parser pops that terminal from the stack and advances the input pointer past the matching symbol.</li><li>No production is applied: expansions are only triggered when the stack top is a nonterminal.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Pop the stack top and advance the input — option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing to expand a production here. The table <code class=\"mm\">M[A, a]</code> is consulted only when the stack top A is a nonterminal; a terminal on top is matched, never expanded.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q28", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → a A | B c, A → b | ε, B → d, how many terminals belong to FIRST(S)? Do not count ε, since S is not nullable. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">FIRST</code> of a nonterminal is the union of the FIRST sets of its alternatives, and a nullable symbol that ends an alternative adds nothing to that alternative's FIRST set.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">FIRST(A) = {b, ε}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(B) = {d}</code>.</li><li>Alternative <code class=\"mm\">a A</code>: it begins with the terminal <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is in <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(S)</code>; A is nullable but it is the last symbol, so the scan ends there and contributes no further terminal.</li><li>Alternative <code class=\"mm\">B c</code>: B contributes <code class=\"mm\">d</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">d ∈ FIRST(S)</code>.</li><li>S is not nullable, so ε is excluded: <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(S) = {a, d}</code>, two terminals.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting three terminals, by imagining that the nullable A at the end of <code class=\"mm\">a A</code> lets the scan reach some further symbol. There is no symbol after A, so the scan stops.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q29", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The grammar A → x y z | x y w | x v is left factored as far as possible. Which one of the following is the result?", "options": ["A → x A′; A′ → y z | y w | v", "A → x A′; A′ → y A″ | v; A″ → z | w", "A → x A′; A′ → y A″ | v A‴; A″ → z | w", "A → A′ x; A′ → y z | y w | v"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Left factoring removes the longest common prefix of two or more alternatives and repeats on the remainder until no two alternatives of a nonterminal share a first symbol.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>All three alternatives begin with <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A → x A′</code> with <code class=\"mm\">A′ → y z | y w | v</code>.</li><li>Two alternatives of <code class=\"mm\">A′</code> still share the prefix <code class=\"mm\">y</code>, so factor again: <code class=\"mm\">A′ → y A″ | v</code>.</li><li>The remainder <code class=\"mm\">z | w</code> has no common prefix: <code class=\"mm\">A″ → z | w</code>.</li><li>Factoring stops here because every nonterminal now has alternatives with distinct first symbols.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — <code class=\"mm\">A → x A′; A′ → y A″ | v; A″ → z | w</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping after one factoring step, which leaves <code class=\"mm\">A′ → y z | y w</code> with the shared prefix <code class=\"mm\">y</code> still in place, so <code class=\"mm\">FIRST</code> sets of two alternatives still overlap.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q30", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about an LL(1) parsing table are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A cell of the table may hold more than one production when the grammar is not LL(1).", "If a nonterminal A is nullable, the FOLLOW set of A decides which columns of row A receive A → ε.", "A nullable nonterminal contributes entries only in the columns of its own FIRST set.", "In the table of an LL(1) grammar no cell can hold two different productions.", "Deleting every ε-production from a grammar leaves its parsing table unchanged."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The table is filled by two rules: <code class=\"mm\">A → α</code> goes into <code class=\"mm\">M[A, a]</code> for each <code class=\"mm\">a ∈ FIRST(α)</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">A → ε</code> goes into <code class=\"mm\">M[A, b]</code> for each <code class=\"mm\">b ∈ FOLLOW(A)</code>. A grammar is LL(1) exactly when no cell receives two entries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: when two rules place different productions in one cell, that cell holds both, and the grammar is not LL(1).</li><li>(B) True: <code class=\"mm\">A → ε</code> can be applied only when the lookahead can follow A, which is precisely <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</code>.</li><li>(C) False: <code class=\"mm\">A → ε</code> is placed using FOLLOW, not FIRST, so a nullable nonterminal also writes into columns outside its FIRST set.</li><li>(D) True: that is the definition of LL(1) — a single entry per cell gives the parser a unique move for every lookahead.</li><li>(E) False: ε-productions create the FOLLOW-driven entries, so deleting them removes cells and changes the language.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a nullable nonterminal writes only into its FIRST columns. The ε-alternative is entered through FOLLOW, which is how a predictive parser knows the nonterminal may vanish here.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q31", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → a A | b, A → c | ε, which cell of the LL(1) parsing table holds the production A → ε?", "options": ["M[A, c]", "M[A, b]", "M[A, $]", "M[S, $]"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A production <code class=\"mm\">A → ε</code> is entered in the columns given by <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</code>: the parser may drop A exactly when the next input symbol is one that can legitimately follow A.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A) = FOLLOW(S) = {$}</code>, because A appears only at the end of <code class=\"mm\">S → a A</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">A → ε</code> goes into <code class=\"mm\">M[A, $]</code>.</li><li>The alternative <code class=\"mm\">A → c</code> occupies <code class=\"mm\">M[A, c]</code> instead, so no cell receives two entries and the grammar is LL(1).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">M[A, $]</code> — option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Entering <code class=\"mm\">A → ε</code> in <code class=\"mm\">M[A, c]</code>. The column is the lookahead token that triggers the move, and ε is applied when the lookahead follows A, not when it starts A.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q32", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Immediate left recursion is removed from every nonterminal of the grammar E → E + T | T, T → T * F | F, F → ( E ) | id. How many productions does the resulting grammar contain in total? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The rewrite for <code class=\"mm\">A → A α₁ | … | A αₘ | β₁ | … | βₙ</code> is <code class=\"mm\">A → β₁ A′ | … | βₙ A′</code> together with <code class=\"mm\">A′ → α₁ A′ | … | αₘ A′ | ε</code>: n productions for A, m + 1 for the new nonterminal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>E has one β (namely T) and one α (<code class=\"mm\">+ T</code>): <code class=\"mm\">E → T E′</code> and <code class=\"mm\">E′ → + T E′ | ε</code> — 3 productions.</li><li>T has one β (F) and one α (<code class=\"mm\">* F</code>): <code class=\"mm\">T → F T′</code> and <code class=\"mm\">T′ → * F T′ | ε</code> — 3 productions.</li><li>F has no left recursion: <code class=\"mm\">F → ( E ) | id</code> stays at 2 productions.</li><li>Total 3 + 3 + 2 = 8 productions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 8</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 6 by forgetting the new nonterminals, or 12 by counting each α-alternative as an extra production of A rather than of A′.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q33", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A recursive-descent procedure is written for the alternative A → a B c. It has matched a, called the procedure for B, and that call has returned successfully. What must it do next?", "options": ["match the token c and advance the input past it.", "call the procedure for A again, since A appears on the left side.", "push B onto an explicit stack.", "reduce a B c to A and return without reading further input."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A procedure for A walks the right side of its chosen production from left to right: a terminal is matched against the input, a nonterminal becomes a recursive call.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The right side is <code class=\"mm\">a B c</code>. The first symbol <code class=\"mm\">a</code> was matched, and B was handled by its own procedure.</li><li>The next symbol is the terminal <code class=\"mm\">c</code>, so the procedure matches <code class=\"mm\">c</code> against the current input symbol and advances.</li><li>After the whole right side is consumed the procedure returns, and its caller continues with the symbol after A.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Match the terminal <code class=\"mm\">c</code> and advance — option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting a recursive call for <code class=\"mm\">c</code>. Only nonterminals have procedures; terminals are matched directly by consuming the input token.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q34", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about FIRST and FOLLOW sets are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["If A ⇒* ε, then ε ∈ FIRST(A).", "If A is nullable, then FIRST(A B) = FIRST(A) ∪ FIRST(B) with ε removed from the result unless B is also nullable.", "If A is not nullable, FOLLOW(A) may still contain ε.", "For every nonterminal X of an LL(1) grammar, FIRST(X) ∩ FOLLOW(X) = ∅.", "For the start symbol S, $ ∈ FOLLOW(S)."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (E)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> FIRST is about what a string can begin with, FOLLOW about what can come immediately after a nonterminal. ε is a FIRST symbol only; FOLLOW holds terminals and the end marker $ and never ε.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: A derives the empty string exactly when ε is in <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(A)</code>.</li><li>(B) True: a nullable A lets the scan reach B, so B contributes its FIRST set, and the concatenation is nullable only if B is nullable too.</li><li>(C) False: ε is never a member of any FOLLOW set; FOLLOW answers \"which terminals may follow\", and the empty string is not a terminal.</li><li>(D) False: nothing forbids a terminal from both starting X and following X — the grammar <code class=\"mm\">S → a S | a</code> has <code class=\"mm\">a</code> in both FIRST(S) and FOLLOW(S) and is still LL(1).</li><li>(E) True: the end marker is placed in <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S)</code> as the initialisation step of every FOLLOW computation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (E).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Putting ε into a FOLLOW set when a nonterminal is nullable. FOLLOW is a set of terminals plus $; nullability affects which symbols are added, not whether ε is added.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q35", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In an LL(1) parsing table, the rows are indexed by ______ and the columns by ______.", "options": ["terminals; nonterminals", "nonterminals; terminals together with $", "productions; input symbols", "nonterminals; productions"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The table answers one question: given the nonterminal on top of the stack and the current input symbol, which production should be applied?</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The parser looks up <code class=\"mm\">M[A, a]</code> where A is the nonterminal on top of the stack.</li><li>So rows are nonterminals and columns are the lookahead symbols — the terminals of the grammar plus the end marker $.</li><li>Each entry is either empty (a syntax error) or a single production, which is what makes the parse deterministic.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Rows: nonterminals; columns: terminals plus $ — option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Swapping the two axes. The row is the symbol being expanded, the column is the token being looked at.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q36", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → a S | b, which one of the following is FOLLOW(S)?", "options": ["{a}", "{b}", "{$}", "{a, b}"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> FOLLOW(S) starts with $, and grows only when S appears somewhere other than at the end of a right side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Initialise <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S) = {$}</code> because S is the start symbol.</li><li>In <code class=\"mm\">S → a S</code> the recursive S is the last symbol, so nothing new is added beyond <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S)</code> itself.</li><li>In <code class=\"mm\">S → b</code> there is no S at all.</li><li>Hence <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S) = {$}</code>: nothing can follow a complete sentence.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">{$}</code> — option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding <code class=\"mm\">b</code> to FOLLOW because b ends a production. What matters is what follows S, and here S only ever appears at the very end of a right side.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q37", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → A B | c, A → a | ε, B → b, how many cells of the LL(1) parsing table are non-empty? Count a cell once however many entries it holds. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every production writes into one cell per symbol of its FIRST set, and an ε-production writes into one cell per symbol of FOLLOW. Rows that exist are only those of nonterminals that actually own productions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">FIRST(A) = {a, ε}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A) = FIRST(B) = {b}</code>, <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(B) = FOLLOW(S) = {$}</code>.</li><li>Row S: <code class=\"mm\">S → A B</code> covers <code class=\"mm\">a</code> (from A) and <code class=\"mm\">b</code> (A nullable, so B contributes); <code class=\"mm\">S → c</code> covers <code class=\"mm\">c</code> — three cells.</li><li>Row A: <code class=\"mm\">A → a</code> covers <code class=\"mm\">a</code>; <code class=\"mm\">A → ε</code> covers the column <code class=\"mm\">b</code> from FOLLOW(A) — two cells.</li><li>Row B: <code class=\"mm\">B → b</code> covers <code class=\"mm\">b</code> — one cell.</li><li>No cell receives two entries, so the grammar is LL(1) and the total is 3 + 2 + 1 = 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 5 by stopping the row of S after the terminal a, forgetting that a nullable A lets the same production also cover the column b.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q38", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a predictive parser the stack top is the nonterminal A, the input symbol is a, and M[A, a] is empty. The parser must", "options": ["pop A and continue with the next symbol on the stack.", "skip the input symbol a and try the next one.", "report a syntax error.", "apply the production A → ε."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An empty cell means no production of A can start with the lookahead <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and A cannot vanish here either; the input is not a sentence of the grammar.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The table holds a production in <code class=\"mm\">M[A, a]</code> exactly when A can legitimately produce something beginning with <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</li><li>The cell is empty, so no legal move exists at this point of the derivation.</li><li>A predictive parser has no backtracking to fall back on, so it stops and reports a syntax error.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Report a syntax error — option (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying <code class=\"mm\">A → ε</code>. That production is legal only when the lookahead lies in <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</code>, which is exactly the condition under which the cell would not be empty.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q39", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → A B C, A → a, B → b, C → ε | c, which one of the following is FOLLOW(B)?", "options": ["{c}", "{c, $}", "{b, c}", "{$}"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> When B is followed by C in a right side, FOLLOW(B) takes FIRST(C) without ε, and if C is nullable it also takes FOLLOW of the left-side nonterminal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">FIRST(C) = {c, ε}</code>, because <code class=\"mm\">C → ε</code> makes C nullable.</li><li>B is followed by C in <code class=\"mm\">S → A B C</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">c ∈ FOLLOW(B)</code>.</li><li>C is nullable, so the scan runs past C to the end of the production, and <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S) = {$}</code> is added as well.</li><li>Hence <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(B) = {c, $}</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">{c, $}</code> — option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">{c}</code> by treating C as non-nullable. Because C can vanish, whatever follows S can also follow B.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q40", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about the two preprocessing rewrites — eliminating left recursion and left factoring — are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["After immediate left recursion is removed from A → A α | β, no production of A begins with A.", "Left factoring never changes the language generated by the grammar.", "Removing indirect left recursion is enough to make any grammar LL(1).", "Left factoring always removes every LL(1) conflict a grammar has.", "A left-recursive grammar can be ambiguous."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (E)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Both transformations are language-preserving rewrites. Removing left recursion clears one obstacle to predictive parsing; left factoring clears another; neither is sufficient on its own.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: the result is <code class=\"mm\">A → β A′</code> with <code class=\"mm\">A′ → α A′ | ε</code>, and β does not begin with A by assumption, so no production of A starts with A.</li><li>(B) True: factoring only regroups alternatives under a new nonterminal, so the set of derived strings is unchanged.</li><li>(C) False: a grammar can be free of left recursion and still be non-LL(1), for instance when two alternatives share a common prefix or when FIRST/FOLLOW overlap at a nullable alternative.</li><li>(D) False: left factoring removes conflicts caused by a shared prefix only; conflicts from nullable alternatives, or from a grammar that is inherently ambiguous, survive it.</li><li>(E) True: <code class=\"mm\">E → E + E | E * E | id</code> is left recursive and ambiguous, since <code class=\"mm\">id + id * id</code> has two parse trees.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (E).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the two rewrites as a cure for every LL(1) conflict. They are necessary repairs for two specific causes of conflict, not a guarantee of LL(1)-ness.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q41", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → X Y Z, X → x | ε, Y → y | ε, Z → z | ε, how many symbols does FOLLOW(Y) contain? Count $ as a symbol. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> FOLLOW of a symbol in the middle of a right side takes the FIRST set of everything to its right, and continues past any nullable suffix into FOLLOW of the left-side nonterminal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Y is followed by Z, so <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(Z) = {z, ε}</code> contributes <code class=\"mm\">z</code>.</li><li>Z is nullable, so the scan reaches the end of <code class=\"mm\">S → X Y Z</code> and adds <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S) = {$}</code>.</li><li>ε is never a member of a FOLLOW set, so it is not counted.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(Y) = {z, $}</code>, two symbols.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting ε as a third symbol, or stopping at <code class=\"mm\">z</code> without noting that the nullable Z exposes the end of the sentence.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q42", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a recursive-descent parser, the recursion of the host language replaces which component of a table-driven predictive parser?", "options": ["the input buffer", "the explicit parse stack", "the parsing table", "the output stream"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Both parsers are predictive and consult the same FIRST/FOLLOW information; they differ only in how the pending sentential form is held.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A table-driven parser keeps the pending symbols on an explicit stack.</li><li>A recursive-descent parser keeps them implicitly: each pending nonterminal is an active procedure call, and the call stack records the symbols still to be matched.</li><li>The production to apply is still chosen by the same FIRST/FOLLOW test, so the table is not what the recursion replaces.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: The explicit parse stack — option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Saying the recursion replaces the parsing table. The choice of alternative is still driven by FIRST/FOLLOW; only the storage of the pending form moves to the call stack.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q43", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → A B | B a, A → a | ε, B → b | ε, which one of the following is FOLLOW(B)?", "options": ["{$}", "{a, $}", "{a, b, $}", "{b, $}"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A nonterminal that occurs twice in the grammar collects contributions from every occurrence, so each occurrence must be examined separately.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>First occurrence, <code class=\"mm\">S → A B</code>: B is last, so <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S) = {$}</code> is added.</li><li>Second occurrence, <code class=\"mm\">S → B a</code>: B is followed by the terminal <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is added.</li><li>A being nullable does not matter here: A appears only in the first alternative, where it precedes B rather than following it.</li><li>Hence <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(B) = {a, $}</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">{a, $}</code> — option (B).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering <code class=\"mm\">{$}</code> from the first occurrence only. Every occurrence of the nonterminal contributes to its FOLLOW set.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q44", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about recursive-descent and predictive parsing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A table-driven predictive parser keeps an explicit stack in place of the procedure calls of a recursive-descent parser.", "A recursive-descent parser for an LL(1) grammar needs no backtracking.", "The parse tree built by a top-down parser corresponds to a rightmost derivation.", "The procedure for a nonterminal returns after consuming exactly the input derived from that nonterminal.", "A hand-written recursive-descent parser for an LL(1) grammar may still need more than one token of lookahead."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A top-down parser builds a leftmost derivation: it expands the leftmost nonterminal first, and the frontier of the tree always matches the input read so far.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: the two parsers are the same algorithm with the pending sentential form stored differently — on an explicit stack or on the call stack.</li><li>(B) True: the FIRST/FOLLOW test picks a unique production for each lookahead, so no alternative ever has to be undone.</li><li>(C) False: top-down parsing expands the leftmost nonterminal first, giving a leftmost derivation; a rightmost derivation in reverse is what a bottom-up parser builds.</li><li>(D) True: that is the contract of the procedure — it consumes exactly the string derived from its nonterminal and leaves the rest of the input untouched.</li><li>(E) False: for an LL(1) grammar one token of lookahead always determines the choice, which is what the \"1\" in LL(1) asserts.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing a top-down parser produces a rightmost derivation. Leftmost expansion is the definition of top-down; the reverse of a rightmost derivation is the bottom-up (shift-reduce) order.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q45", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Immediate left recursion is removed from A → A a b | A c | d. Which one of the following is the result?", "options": ["A → d A′; A′ → a b A′ | c A′ | ε", "A → d A′; A′ → a A′ | b A′ | c A′ | ε", "A → A′ d; A′ → a b A′ | c A′ | ε", "A → d A′; A′ → b a A′ | c A′ | ε"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With <code class=\"mm\">A → A α₁ | … | A αₘ | β</code>, the rewrite is <code class=\"mm\">A → β A′</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A′ → α₁ A′ | … | αₘ A′ | ε</code>, keeping each α intact as a whole string.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The two recursive alternatives give α₁ = <code class=\"mm\">a b</code> and α₂ = <code class=\"mm\">c</code>; the non-recursive alternative gives β = <code class=\"mm\">d</code>.</li><li>So <code class=\"mm\">A → d A′</code>.</li><li>And <code class=\"mm\">A′ → a b A′ | c A′ | ε</code>, with the two symbols of α₁ kept together and in order.</li><li>A now generates <code class=\"mm\">d (ab | c)*</code>, the same language as before.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A) — <code class=\"mm\">A → d A′; A′ → a b A′ | c A′ | ε</code>.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Splitting the multi-symbol α into separate alternatives, as option (B) does. The α must be carried across as one string; splitting it changes the language.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q46", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The left-recursive grammar S → S a | b generates which language?", "options": ["b a*", "a* b", "(a b)*", "b* a"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Reading the grammar semantically: the only way to stop the recursion is the base alternative <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, and each recursive step appends one <code class=\"mm\">a</code> to the right.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every derivation must begin with the base case: <code class=\"mm\">S ⇒ b</code>.</li><li>Each application of <code class=\"mm\">S → S a</code> appends a single <code class=\"mm\">a</code> at the right end.</li><li>So the strings are <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b a a</code>, …, that is <code class=\"mm\">b a*</code>.</li><li>Left recursion changes how the strings are derived, not which strings exist.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: <code class=\"mm\">b a*</code> — option (A).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the recursion as prefixing, giving <code class=\"mm\">a* b</code>. The recursion <code class=\"mm\">S → S a</code> puts the new <code class=\"mm\">a</code> on the right of what S already produced.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q47", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "left-recursion-and-factoring", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Consider A → B a | c, B → A b | d. Indirect left recursion is removed by substituting the A-productions into B and then removing the immediate left recursion that results. Which one of the following describes the outcome?", "options": ["B → B a b | c b | d, with no further step needed", "B → c b B′ | d B′; B′ → a b B′ | ε, with A → B a | c unchanged", "B → a b B′ | d B′, B′ → c b B′ | ε, with A → B a | c unchanged", "B → c b | d; B′ → a b B′, with A → B a | c unchanged"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Indirect recursion is removed in two steps: substitute so the recursion becomes immediate, then apply the immediate-left-recursion rewrite.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Substitute <code class=\"mm\">A → B a | c</code> into <code class=\"mm\">B → A b | d</code>: <code class=\"mm\">B → B a b | c b | d</code>.</li><li>This is immediate left recursion with α = <code class=\"mm\">a b</code> and β ∈ {<code class=\"mm\">c b</code>, <code class=\"mm\">d</code>}.</li><li>Rewrite: <code class=\"mm\">B → c b B′ | d B′</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B′ → a b B′ | ε</code>.</li><li>A keeps its productions <code class=\"mm\">A → B a | c</code>, which no longer involve a cycle through B.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — <code class=\"mm\">B → c b B′ | d B′; B′ → a b B′ | ε</code>, A unchanged.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping after the substitution, as option (A) does. Substitution only exposes the left recursion; the rewrite still has to be applied to remove it.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q48", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "recursive-descent-parsers", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "A predictive parser uses E → T E′, E′ → + T E′ | ε, T → F T′, T′ → * F T′ | ε, F → ( E ) | id. For the input ( id ), how many productions are applied in total, counting each application separately? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every nonterminal expansion costs one production application, including each ε-production used to close off E′ or T′. Terminals are matched, not expanded, so they cost nothing.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">E → T E′</code> (1), <code class=\"mm\">T → F T′</code> (2), <code class=\"mm\">F → ( E )</code> (3) — the opening parenthesis is matched.</li><li>Inside the parentheses: <code class=\"mm\">E → T E′</code> (4), <code class=\"mm\">T → F T′</code> (5), <code class=\"mm\">F → id</code> (6).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T′</code> sees <code class=\"mm\">)</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">*</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">T′ → ε</code> (7); <code class=\"mm\">E′</code> sees <code class=\"mm\">)</code>, not <code class=\"mm\">+</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">E′ → ε</code> (8); the closing parenthesis is matched.</li><li>Back in the outer T: <code class=\"mm\">T′ → ε</code> (9) on the lookahead $; then <code class=\"mm\">E′ → ε</code> (10).</li><li>Total 10 productions; the two parentheses and the identifier are matched, not expanded.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 6 by counting only the productions that add terminals. Each ε-production is a real expansion and costs one application.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q49", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "ll1-parsing-tables", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following statements about LL(1) grammars is TRUE?", "options": ["LL(1)-ness is a property of the language, so every grammar for an LL(1) language is LL(1).", "LL(1)-ness is a property of the grammar, and a grammar that is not LL(1) may still generate a language that has an LL(1) grammar.", "Every unambiguous grammar is LL(1).", "Every deterministic context-free language has an LL(1) grammar."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> LL(1) is a property of a grammar — it is decided by inspecting FIRST and FOLLOW sets of productions — while the language is the set of strings the grammar generates.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The LL(1) test is applied to productions and their FIRST/FOLLOW sets, so it classifies grammars, not languages.</li><li>The same language can have several grammars: the left-recursive <code class=\"mm\">E → E + T | T</code> is not LL(1), yet the factored version <code class=\"mm\">E → T E′</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E′ → + T E′ | ε</code> generates the same language and is LL(1).</li><li>(C) is false: <code class=\"mm\">S → a S a | a</code> is unambiguous but not LL(1), because both alternatives put <code class=\"mm\">a</code> in FIRST.</li><li>(D) is false: deterministic context-free languages need not be LL(1); <code class=\"mm\">{aⁿ bⁿ} ∪ {aⁿ cⁿ}</code> is deterministic but has no LL(1) grammar.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B) — LL(1)-ness belongs to the grammar, not the language.</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that rewriting a language into a nicer grammar always rescues LL(1)-ness. Some deterministic languages have no LL(1) grammar at all, so the property is not a property of the language.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U2-Q50", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 2, "unit_slug": "top-down-parsing", "subtopic": "first-and-follow-sets", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about FIRST and FOLLOW sets are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["For a terminal a, FIRST(a) = {a}.", "The FOLLOW set of the start symbol always contains $.", "If a nonterminal other than the start symbol never occurs on the right side of any production, its FOLLOW set is empty.", "A FOLLOW set may contain a nonterminal as a member.", "FIRST of a string is decided by its leftmost symbol alone, even when that symbol is a nullable nonterminal."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> FIRST sets describe what a string can begin with; FOLLOW sets describe which terminals may appear immediately after a nonterminal, plus the end marker $ for the start symbol.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True: a terminal derives only itself, so its FIRST set is the singleton <code class=\"mm\">{a}</code>.</li><li>(B) True: every FOLLOW computation is initialised with <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S) = {$}</code>.</li><li>(C) True: FOLLOW of a nonterminal grows only from occurrences on right sides, so a nonterminal with no such occurrence keeps the empty set — only the start symbol is seeded with $.</li><li>(D) False: FOLLOW holds terminals and $ only; a nonterminal on the right side is followed by whatever its own FOLLOW is, not by the nonterminal itself.</li><li>(E) False: when the leftmost symbol is nullable, the scan continues to the next symbol, so the FIRST set of the whole string can include symbols that the leftmost symbol cannot start.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C).</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Stopping the FIRST computation at the leftmost symbol even when it is nullable, and imagining that nonterminals can appear inside FOLLOW sets.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q01", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In the LR(0) item A → X · Y Z, what does the position of the dot record?", "options": ["Nothing has been read yet; the parser still expects the whole right side X Y Z.", "A string derived from X has already been recognised, and a string derived from Y Z is still expected.", "All of X Y Z has been recognised, so the production is ready to be reduced.", "The dot separates the lookahead symbol from the handle and has no relation to the right side."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An LR(0) item is one production with a distinguished position in its right side. The dot is that position: everything to its left has been matched against the input already, everything to its right is still expected.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A → · X Y Z</code> — the dot is at the left end, so nothing of the right side has been read.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A → X · Y Z</code> — <code class=\"mm\">X</code> has been read; the parser now expects a string derived from <code class=\"mm\">Y Z</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A → X Y · Z</code> — two symbols read; one remains.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A → X Y Z ·</code> — the dot is at the right end, which makes this a <i>complete</i> (reduce) item.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — the dot records how much of the right side has been recognised, so <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is done and <code class=\"mm\">Y Z</code> is still to come.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the dot as a marker of the current input position or of the handle in the input string. The dot is always a position <i>inside one production</i>; the state the parser is in is what says where it stands in the input.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q02", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following is NOT an LR(0) item of the production A → X Y Z?", "options": ["A → · X Y Z", "A → X · Y Z", "A → X Y Z ·", "A → X · Z Y"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A production with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> symbols on its right side yields exactly <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> LR(0) items, one for each legal dot position. The order of the symbols on the right side is never changed: an item is not a permutation of a production.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">n = 3</code> symbols, so there are <code class=\"mm\">4</code> items.</li><li>They are <code class=\"mm\">A → · X Y Z</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A → X · Y Z</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A → X Y · Z</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A → X Y Z ·</code>.</li><li>The fourth option reads <code class=\"mm\">X · Z Y</code>: <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> have been exchanged, which is a different string, not a different dot position.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D) — <code class=\"mm\">A → X · Z Y</code> is not an item of <code class=\"mm\">A → X Y Z</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating an item as \"the production plus a marker anywhere\", which makes any rearrangement look legal. The dot moves through the right side in order; the symbol sequence is fixed.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q03", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → A A, A → a A | b, how many LR(0) items does the augmented grammar contain in total? Count every item of every production, including the added production S′ → S. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A production with <code class=\"mm\">n</code> symbols on its right side contributes <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> LR(0) items. The augmented grammar adds one production, <code class=\"mm\">S′ → S</code>, which contributes its own items.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">S′ → S</code>: one symbol on the right, so 2 items.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">S → A A</code>: two symbols, so 3 items.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A → a A</code>: two symbols, so 3 items.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A → b</code>: one symbol, so 2 items.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">2 + 3 + 3 + 2 = 10</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 10 items.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the four productions instead of the items, or forgetting the augmented production <code class=\"mm\">S′ → S</code>. The start symbol is always primed and its production always counted.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q04", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "An SLR(1) parser is about to reduce by A → ω in a state I. In the ACTION table the reduction is entered in column a only when which condition holds?", "options": ["a ∈ FIRST(A).", "a is the terminal that stands immediately to the right of the handle in the input seen so far.", "a ∈ FOLLOW(A).", "a is the terminal that was shifted last, whatever it happens to be."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> SLR(1) approximates the lookahead of a reduction by the FOLLOW set of the left side. Every reduction by <code class=\"mm\">A → ω</code> is entered in all columns <code class=\"mm\">a ∈ FOLLOW(A)</code>, and nowhere else.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>When the parser has <code class=\"mm\">A → ω ·</code> on top, <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is about to appear in the sentential form, so the next input symbol can only be one that may follow <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</li><li>SLR does not look at the individual state; it uses the grammar-wide set <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">FIRST(A)</code> governs shift decisions and the table entries of productions that begin with <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, not the reduction of <code class=\"mm\">A → ω</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C) — the reduction is entered on the terminals of <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing SLR(1) inspects the real context of the state. That is exactly what LR(1) does with per-item lookaheads; SLR substitutes the coarser <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW</code> set, which is why an SLR table can conflict where an LR(1) table does not.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q05", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In the LR(1) item [A → α · β, a], what does the second component a record?", "options": ["A terminal that may immediately follow A in some sentential form, so the reduction by this production is taken only on that lookahead.", "The terminal that must be shifted next, before any reduction of A can happen.", "The symbol that stands immediately to the left of the dot.", "The precedence level attached to the production A → αβ."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An LR(1) item is a pair: an LR(0) item and one lookahead terminal. The lookahead says which terminal may follow the completed left side, and therefore in which columns the reduction belongs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The dot part <code class=\"mm\">A → α · β</code> is exactly an LR(0) item and records progress through the right side.</li><li>The second component <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is the extra information LR(1) adds: a terminal that can come after <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</li><li>When the dot reaches the end, <code class=\"mm\">[A → αβ ·, a]</code> licenses the reduction by <code class=\"mm\">A → αβ</code> only in column <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A) — <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is the lookahead that may follow <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the lookahead with the next symbol to be shifted. The lookahead is a condition on a <i>reduction</i>, and the same state can carry different lookaheads for the same core.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q06", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar E → E + E | E * E | ( E ) | id, which precedence relation holds between the terminals ( and )?", "options": ["( <· )", "( ≌ )", "( ·> )", "No precedence relation holds between them."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The three relations are read off the productions: <code class=\"mm\">a ≌ b</code> when a right side contains <code class=\"mm\">a b</code> or <code class=\"mm\">a B b</code>; <code class=\"mm\">a &lt;· b</code> when <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is followed by a nonterminal whose FIRST set contains <code class=\"mm\">b</code>; <code class=\"mm\">a ·&gt; b</code> when <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is in the LAST set of a nonterminal that is followed by <code class=\"mm\">b</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The production <code class=\"mm\">E → ( E )</code> has the shape terminal, nonterminal, terminal: <code class=\"mm\">(</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">)</code>.</li><li>That is precisely the pattern <code class=\"mm\">a B b</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">( ≌ )</code>.</li><li>The relation is symmetric in form only: <code class=\"mm\">)</code> never satisfies <code class=\"mm\">) ≌ (</code>, because the production is written in one direction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — <code class=\"mm\">( ≌ )</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">≌</code> as \"the two operators have equal precedence\". It is a parsing relation meaning the parser is inside a bracketed construct and must keep shifting until the matching closing symbol appears.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q07", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about operator-precedence parsing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An operator grammar has no ε-production and no production whose right side contains two adjacent nonterminals.", "The relations are computed from the productions alone; no LR item set, closure or state machine is built.", "Every context-free grammar becomes an operator grammar once its ε-productions are deleted.", "The parser reduces when the relation between the topmost terminal on the stack and the next input symbol is ·>.", "The parser must compute the FOLLOW set of every nonterminal before it can run."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B)) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Operator-precedence parsing is a shift-reduce method that decides every move from a table of three relations between pairs of terminals, built directly from the productions of an operator grammar.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: those two restrictions are the definition of an operator grammar, and they guarantee that the handle of every right-sentential form is bordered by terminals.</li><li>(B) true: <code class=\"mm\">FIRST</code> and <code class=\"mm\">LAST</code> of the nonterminals, plus a scan of each right side, are all that is needed.</li><li>(C) false: deleting <code class=\"mm\">ε</code>-productions does not remove a right side such as <code class=\"mm\">A B</code>, and adjacent nonterminals are forbidden in an operator grammar.</li><li>(D) true: shift while <code class=\"mm\">&lt;·</code> or <code class=\"mm\">≌</code> holds, reduce while <code class=\"mm\">·&gt;</code> holds, and report an error when no relation holds.</li><li>(E) false: FOLLOW sets belong to SLR(1); the precedence relations replace them entirely.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that any CFG can be handled after a quick rewrite. The class of operator grammars is strictly smaller, and grammars with <code class=\"mm\">ε</code>-productions or adjacent nonterminals need a real transformation, not a deletion.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q08", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → A A, A → a A | b, which set is the closure of the single item {S → A · A}?", "options": ["{S → A · A, A → · a A, A → · b}", "{S → A · A, A → a · A, A → b ·}", "{S → A · A}", "{S → A · A, A → · a A, A → · b, S → · A A}"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The closure of an item set adds <code class=\"mm\">B → · γ</code> for every production of <code class=\"mm\">B</code> whenever some item in the set has the dot immediately before <code class=\"mm\">B</code>. Nothing else is added.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The item <code class=\"mm\">S → A · A</code> has the dot before the nonterminal <code class=\"mm\">A</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A</code> has the productions <code class=\"mm\">A → a A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A → b</code>, so the items <code class=\"mm\">A → · a A</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A → · b</code> are added.</li><li>Neither new item has a dot before a nonterminal, so the closure stops.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">S → · A A</code> is not added: the dot before <code class=\"mm\">A</code> adds the productions of <code class=\"mm\">A</code>, never productions of <code class=\"mm\">S</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Adding the productions of the item’s own left side (option D), or moving the dot forward while closing (option B). Closure only looks at the symbol <i>after</i> the dot, and only adds items with the dot at the left end.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q09", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → A A, A → a A | b, how many states does the canonical collection of LR(0) items contain? Count the initial state. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The states are the distinct closed item sets reached from the closure of <code class=\"mm\">S′ → · S</code> by repeated GOTO on every grammar symbol.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">I0 = {S′ → · S, S → · A A, A → · a A, A → · b}</code></li><li><code class=\"mm\">GOTO(I0, S) = I1 = {S′ → S ·}</code>; <code class=\"mm\">GOTO(I0, A) = I2 = {S → A · A, A → · a A, A → · b}</code></li><li><code class=\"mm\">GOTO(I0, a) = I3 = {A → a · A, A → · a A, A → · b}</code>; <code class=\"mm\">GOTO(I0, b) = I4 = {A → b ·}</code></li><li><code class=\"mm\">GOTO(I2, A) = I5 = {S → A A ·}</code>; <code class=\"mm\">GOTO(I3, A) = I6 = {A → a A ·}</code></li><li><code class=\"mm\">I2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">I3</code> reuse the states <code class=\"mm\">I3, I4</code> on <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, so no further state appears. The collection is <code class=\"mm\">I0 … I6</code>, seven states.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 7 states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting one state per item or per production. States are shared: the same core <code class=\"mm\">{A → · a A, A → · b}</code> appears inside <code class=\"mm\">I0</code>, <code class=\"mm\">I2</code> and <code class=\"mm\">I3</code>, and its GOTO on <code class=\"mm\">a</code> always lands in the same state.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q10", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "The grammar S → a S b | a is not LR(0). Why is it nevertheless SLR(1)?", "options": ["Because the grammar is unambiguous, and every unambiguous grammar is SLR(1).", "Because the conflict is a reduce/reduce conflict, which SLR(1) removes by production order.", "Because FOLLOW(S) is empty, so the reduction never competes with a shift.", "Because from the state that holds S → a · the only shift is on a, while FOLLOW(S) = {b, $} contains no a."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> LR(0) requires a state that holds a complete item to reduce in <i>every</i> column, so any state that also has a shift item is in conflict. SLR(1) narrows the reduce action to the columns of <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After reading <code class=\"mm\">a</code> from the initial state the parser is in <code class=\"mm\">{S → a · S b, S → · a S b, S → · a, S → a ·}</code>.</li><li>The complete item <code class=\"mm\">S → a ·</code> coexists with a shift on <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, so the LR(0) table has a shift/reduce conflict there.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(S) = {b, $}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">b</code> from <code class=\"mm\">S → a S b</code> and <code class=\"mm\">$</code> because <code class=\"mm\">S</code> is the start symbol.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a ∉ FOLLOW(S)</code>, so the reduce action and the shift action occupy disjoint columns and the SLR(1) table has no conflict.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the hierarchy is a chain in which failing LR(0) means failing SLR(1) too. The classes are nested the other way: every LR(0) grammar is SLR(1), and this grammar shows the inclusion is strict.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q11", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "LALR(1) is built by merging every pair of canonical LR(1) states that have the same core. Which conflicts can such a merge create that were not present in the canonical automaton?", "options": ["Shift/reduce conflicts only.", "Reduce/reduce conflicts only.", "Both shift/reduce and reduce/reduce conflicts.", "None — merging states with equal cores never creates a conflict."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two LR(1) states with the same core have the same LR(0) items, hence the same shift actions and the same GOTO transitions; they differ only in the lookahead terminals attached to complete items.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Shift actions depend only on the core, so a shift present in one state is present in the other, and after merging no new shift competes with anything.</li><li>Reduce actions depend on the lookaheads. Merging unions the lookahead sets.</li><li>If two complete items with different left sides are in the merged state, a terminal that is a lookahead of both now asks for two different reductions: a reduce/reduce conflict appears.</li><li>Since no new shift arises and no shift loses a column, no shift/reduce conflict can be created.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — only reduce/reduce conflicts can be introduced.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing LALR is simply \"LR(1) with fewer states\" and therefore equally powerful on every grammar. The grammar <code class=\"mm\">S → a A d | b B d | a B e | b A e</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A → c</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B → c</code> is LR(1) but the merge produces exactly such a reduce/reduce conflict.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q12", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → L = R | R, L → * R | id, R → L, how many states does the LALR(1) automaton have? Count the initial state; states are obtained by merging canonical LR(1) states that share a core. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The LALR(1) automaton has the states of the LR(0) collection with LR(1) lookaheads attached, so its state count equals the LR(0) state count and is at most the canonical LR(1) count.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Counting the LR(0) states of this grammar: the initial state, the state after <code class=\"mm\">S</code>, after <code class=\"mm\">L</code>, after <code class=\"mm\">R</code>, after <code class=\"mm\">*</code>, after <code class=\"mm\">id</code>, after <code class=\"mm\">=</code>, after <code class=\"mm\">* R</code>, after <code class=\"mm\">L =</code>, and the state after <code class=\"mm\">L = R</code>. That is 10 states.</li><li>The canonical LR(1) collection keeps two copies of the <code class=\"mm\">L</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R</code> states, one reached through <code class=\"mm\">S → · L = R</code> and one through <code class=\"mm\">S → · R</code>, giving 14 states.</li><li>Merging each pair with a common core reduces 14 to 10 states; the merged automaton has no conflict, which is why this grammar is LALR(1) although it is not SLR(1).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 10 states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 14 by counting canonical LR(1) states. LALR merges states with identical cores, so the count must equal the LR(0) count, not the LR(1) count.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q13", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar E → E + E | E * E | ( E ) | id, which precedence relation holds between the terminals id and +?", "options": ["id <· +", "id ≌ +", "id ·> +", "No precedence relation holds between them."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">a ·&gt; b</code> holds when some production has a nonterminal <code class=\"mm\">A</code> immediately followed by the terminal <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">a</code> belongs to <code class=\"mm\">LAST(A)</code>. <code class=\"mm\">LAST(E) = {id, )}</code> here, because <code class=\"mm\">E → E + E</code> and <code class=\"mm\">E → ( E )</code> end in <code class=\"mm\">id</code> and <code class=\"mm\">)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">E → E + E</code> has <code class=\"mm\">E</code> immediately before <code class=\"mm\">+</code>.</li><li>Every terminal in <code class=\"mm\">LAST(E)</code> therefore satisfies <code class=\"mm\">·&gt; +</code>: <code class=\"mm\">id ·&gt; +</code> and <code class=\"mm\">) ·&gt; +</code>.</li><li>Symmetrically <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(E) = {id, (}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">E → E + E</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">+ &lt;· id</code> and <code class=\"mm\">+ &lt;· (</code>.</li><li>The two relations go in opposite directions, so <code class=\"mm\">id &lt;· +</code> is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C) — <code class=\"mm\">id ·&gt; +</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the relations as operator precedence levels. They are local facts about where a handle can end: <code class=\"mm\">id ·&gt; +</code> says that a handle may end just before the <code class=\"mm\">+</code>, which is what lets the parser reduce <code class=\"mm\">id</code> to <code class=\"mm\">E</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q14", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In the dangling-else grammar S → i C t S | i C t S e S | a, C → b, the SLR(1) table has exactly one conflict. Which one is it, and how is it conventionally resolved?", "options": ["A shift/reduce conflict between shifting e and reducing S → i C t S; resolved by shifting, so the e binds to the nearest i.", "A reduce/reduce conflict between the two S-productions; resolved by taking the longer production.", "A shift/reduce conflict resolved by reducing, so the e binds to the outermost i.", "There is no conflict: the grammar is unambiguous, so SLR(1) accepts it as written."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The grammar is ambiguous: the sentence <code class=\"mm\">i b t i b t a e a</code> has two parse trees, one attaching the <code class=\"mm\">e</code> to each <code class=\"mm\">i</code>. SLR(1) exposes that ambiguity as a single shift/reduce conflict.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After <code class=\"mm\">i C t S</code> the parser is in a state holding the complete item <code class=\"mm\">S → i C t S ·</code> and the item <code class=\"mm\">S → i C t S · e S</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">e ∈ FOLLOW(S)</code>, so the reduce action sits in column <code class=\"mm\">e</code> — the same column that asks for a shift of <code class=\"mm\">e</code>.</li><li>Shifting is chosen, which postpones the reduction and attaches the <code class=\"mm\">e</code> to the innermost <code class=\"mm\">i</code>.</li><li>The two actions are exactly the two parse trees of the ambiguous sentence, which is why the conflict is unavoidable without changing the grammar or the rule.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting a reduce/reduce conflict, or thinking the resolution is part of the grammar. The conflict is shift/reduce and the \"shift wins\" rule is a parser-level convention that encodes the usual nearest-<code class=\"mm\">i</code> semantics.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q15", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following grammars is LR(1) but NOT LALR(1)?", "options": ["S → A A, A → a A | b", "S → a S b | a", "S → a A d | b B d | a B e | b A e, A → c, B → c", "S → L = R | R, L → * R | id, R → L"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> LALR(1) merges canonical LR(1) states that share a core, unioning their lookaheads. A grammar that is LR(1) fails LALR exactly when that union puts two different reductions in one cell.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A): <code class=\"mm\">S → A A</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A → a A | b</code> is LR(0), hence also SLR(1) and LALR(1).</li><li>Option (B): <code class=\"mm\">S → a S b | a</code> is not LR(0) but is SLR(1), so again LALR(1).</li><li>Option (D): <code class=\"mm\">S → L = R | R</code> is the standard grammar that is LALR(1) but not SLR(1).</li><li>Option (C): the canonical LR(1) collection has 14 states and no conflict. The states reached through <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and through <code class=\"mm\">b</code> carry <code class=\"mm\">A → c ·</code> with lookahead <code class=\"mm\">d</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B → c ·</code> with lookahead <code class=\"mm\">e</code>, and the mirror images carry the same two items with the lookaheads exchanged.</li><li>Merging each pair leaves 13 states in which both complete items carry <code class=\"mm\">{d, e}</code>, so column <code class=\"mm\">d</code> (and <code class=\"mm\">e</code>) asks for two different reductions: a reduce/reduce conflict.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming LALR(1) accepts every LR(1) grammar. It accepts every LR(1) <i>language</i>, but individual grammars can be lost in the merge.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q16", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → A A, A → a A | b, how many states of the canonical LR(0) collection contain at least one completed item, that is, an item whose dot stands at the right end? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A completed item is one of the form <code class=\"mm\">A → ω ·</code>. Such a state is where a reduction can happen, so the number of them measures how many places in the automaton a reduce action lives.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">I1 = {S′ → S ·}</code> — complete (accepting).</li><li><code class=\"mm\">I4 = {A → b ·}</code> — complete.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">I5 = {S → A A ·}</code> — complete.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">I6 = {A → a A ·}</code> — complete.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">I0, I2, I3</code> hold only items with the dot before a symbol, so they are not counted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 4 states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the accepting state separately from reduce states, or forgetting that <code class=\"mm\">I6</code> is complete. <code class=\"mm\">I1</code> holds a completed item too; it just happens to be the augmented production, whose \"reduction\" is acceptance.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q17", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about conflicts in an SLR(1) table are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A shift/reduce conflict arises in a state that holds a completed item and an item with the dot before a terminal, when that terminal belongs to the FOLLOW set of the completed item’s left side.", "A reduce/reduce conflict arises when two completed items in one state have left sides whose FOLLOW sets share a terminal.", "Every SLR(1) grammar is LR(0).", "Every LR(1) grammar is SLR(1).", "An SLR(1) table can never contain a reduce/reduce conflict."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The SLR(1) table is filled by shifting on every terminal transition and reducing by <code class=\"mm\">A → ω</code> in the columns of <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</code>. A conflict is simply a cell that receives two actions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: the shift action occupies column <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and the reduce action also occupies it, which is the definition of the conflict.</li><li>(B) true: both complete items write into every terminal of their own FOLLOW set, so a shared terminal receives two different reductions.</li><li>(C) false: <code class=\"mm\">S → a S b | a</code> is SLR(1) but not LR(0), so the inclusion is strict.</li><li>(D) false: <code class=\"mm\">S → L = R | R</code>, <code class=\"mm\">L → * R | id</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R → L</code> is LALR(1) but has a shift/reduce conflict under SLR(1).</li><li>(E) false: <code class=\"mm\">S → A a | B a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A → c</code>, <code class=\"mm\">B → c</code> produces a reduce/reduce conflict in column <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A) and (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating \"SLR(1) conflict\" as synonymous with shift/reduce. Reduce/reduce conflicts are equally possible, and they indicate two distinct handles that the grammar does not separate.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q18", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar S → A a | B a, A → c, B → c, which conflict does the SLR(1) construction produce?", "options": ["A shift/reduce conflict in column a.", "A reduce/reduce conflict in column a.", "A reduce/reduce conflict in column $.", "No conflict — the grammar is SLR(1)."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Both productions <code class=\"mm\">A → c</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B → c</code> are complete after the same terminal <code class=\"mm\">c</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A) = FOLLOW(B) = {a}</code>, so one cell receives two reductions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</code> comes from <code class=\"mm\">S → A a</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">{a}</code>; <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(B)</code> comes from <code class=\"mm\">S → B a</code>, also <code class=\"mm\">{a}</code>.</li><li>The state reached after shifting <code class=\"mm\">c</code> holds <code class=\"mm\">A → c ·</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B → c ·</code>.</li><li>Both write a reduce action into column <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, so the cell <code class=\"mm\">ACTION[that state, a]</code> holds two entries.</li><li>No shift action competes there, so the conflict is reduce/reduce, not shift/reduce.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — reduce/reduce in column <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting a shift/reduce conflict because the grammar \"looks\" like an operator grammar. Shift/reduce needs a shift item in the same state; here the state holds only the two complete items.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q19", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about LR(1) and LALR(1) are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["For the same grammar the canonical LR(1) collection can contain more states than the LALR(1) collection.", "LALR(1) grammars generate exactly the same class of languages as LR(1) grammars.", "Merging states with the same core can change the language the parser accepts.", "Every SLR(1) grammar is LALR(1).", "In the canonical LR(1) construction each item carries a set of lookahead terminals rather than one."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B)) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The four classes are nested: LR(0) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ LR(1) as classes of grammars, while LALR(1) and LR(1) accept the same class of languages.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: <code class=\"mm\">S → L = R | R</code>, <code class=\"mm\">L → * R | id</code>, <code class=\"mm\">R → L</code> has 14 canonical LR(1) states and only 10 LALR(1) states.</li><li>(B) true: for every LR(1) grammar there is an equivalent LALR(1) grammar, so the two classes of languages coincide.</li><li>(C) false: a merge only changes when a reduction is allowed, never which strings are derivable; if the merge produces a conflict the grammar is simply not LALR(1), and its language is unchanged.</li><li>(D) true: SLR(1) lookaheads are a special case of LALR(1) lookaheads, so no SLR(1) grammar is lost.</li><li>(E) false: in the canonical construction each item is a pair with a single lookahead terminal. Sets of lookaheads appear only after states are merged.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing \"same languages\" with \"same grammars\". LALR(1) accepts the same languages as LR(1) while rejecting some individual LR(1) grammars, exactly the ones whose cores conflict when merged.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q20", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the grammar E → E + E | E * E | ( E ) | id, using the standard definition of the precedence relations, how many ordered pairs (a, b) of terminals satisfy a ·> b? Count each ordered pair once. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">a ·&gt; b</code> holds when some production has a nonterminal <code class=\"mm\">A</code> immediately followed by the terminal <code class=\"mm\">b</code> and <code class=\"mm\">a ∈ LAST(A)</code>. Here <code class=\"mm\">LAST(E) = {id, )}</code>: every right side that ends in a terminal ends in <code class=\"mm\">id</code> or <code class=\"mm\">)</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">E → E + E</code> has <code class=\"mm\">E</code> followed by <code class=\"mm\">+</code>, so every <code class=\"mm\">a ∈ LAST(E)</code> gives <code class=\"mm\">a ·&gt; +</code>: <code class=\"mm\">id ·&gt; +</code> and <code class=\"mm\">) ·&gt; +</code> — two pairs.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">E → E * E</code> likewise gives <code class=\"mm\">id ·&gt; *</code> and <code class=\"mm\">) ·&gt; *</code> — two more.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">E → ( E )</code> has <code class=\"mm\">E</code> followed by <code class=\"mm\">)</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">id ·&gt; )</code> and <code class=\"mm\">) ·&gt; )</code> — two more.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">E → E + E</code> also has <code class=\"mm\">E</code> followed by <code class=\"mm\">E</code>, but the symbol after the nonterminal must be a terminal for the relation, so that occurrence adds nothing.</li><li>Total <code class=\"mm\">2 + 2 + 2 = 6</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 6 ordered pairs: <code class=\"mm\">(id, +), (), +), (id, *), (), *), (id, )), (), ))</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming <code class=\"mm\">·&gt;</code> only relates operator pairs, which would give the four pairs of <code class=\"mm\">{+, *, id}</code> alone and miss the two pairs involving <code class=\"mm\">)</code>. <code class=\"mm\">LAST(E)</code> decides the left member, and it contains <code class=\"mm\">id</code> and <code class=\"mm\">)</code>, not the operators.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q21", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "In a shift-reduce parser, the handle of a right-sentential form is which of the following?", "options": ["The leftmost terminal of the form.", "The longest prefix of the form that is also a prefix of some right-sentential form.", "The next input symbol that will be shifted.", "The substring that matches the right side of some production and whose reduction is one step of a rightmost derivation in reverse."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A bottom-up parse is a rightmost derivation written backwards. Each reduction undoes one step of that derivation, so the handle is the substring that the next reduction must replace.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A right-sentential form is what a rightmost derivation produces at some step.</li><li>Reversing one step means replacing a right side by its left side — that right side is the handle.</li><li>Option (B) describes a <i>viable prefix</i>, the other central notion of LR parsing; it is a stack prefix, not a handle.</li><li>Options (A) and (C) describe the input pointer, not a reducible substring.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the handle with the viable prefix. A viable prefix is any prefix of a right-sentential form that does not extend past the end of the handle; the handle itself is the part about to be reduced.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q22", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about LR(0) items and the canonical collection are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["If A → α · B β is in a set and B → γ is a production, then B → · γ belongs to the closure of that set.", "GOTO(I, X) is the closure of the set of items A → α X · β taken over all A → α · X β in I.", "Every state of the canonical collection is the closure of a set of kernel items.", "The number of states of the collection equals the number of productions of the augmented grammar.", "An item whose dot stands at the left end of a production is called a reduce item."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B)) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The collection is built by two operations: CLOSURE, which completes a set of items with the productions of the nonterminal after the dot, and GOTO, which advances the dot over one grammar symbol and then closes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: that is the definition of the closure operation.</li><li>(B) true: GOTO first moves the dot over <code class=\"mm\">X</code> in every item that allows it, then takes the closure.</li><li>(C) true: the initial state is the closure of <code class=\"mm\">{S′ → · S}</code>, and every other state is the closure of the items produced by a GOTO, which are exactly its kernel.</li><li>(D) false: the count depends on the grammar. <code class=\"mm\">S → A A</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A → a A | b</code> has four productions and seven states.</li><li>(E) false: an item with the dot at the left end is an initial item. A reduce item has the dot at the right end.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting a one-to-one correspondence between productions and states. States are sets of items, many states share items, and the same core can be reached by different paths.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q23", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "For the expression grammar E → E + T | T, T → T * F | F, F → ( E ) | id, how many states does the LALR(1) automaton have?", "options": ["12", "10", "22", "24"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This grammar has no pair of LR(1) states with a common core that carry different lookahead sets needing separate copies, so the LALR(1) automaton is the LR(0) automaton with lookaheads attached and has the same number of states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Start: <code class=\"mm\">I0 = {E′ → · E, E → · E + T, E → · T, T → · T * F, T → · F, F → · ( E ), F → · id}</code>.</li><li>The GOTO transitions follow the nesting <code class=\"mm\">E, T, F</code>: states for <code class=\"mm\">E′ → E ·</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E → E · + T</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E → T ·</code>/<code class=\"mm\">T → T · * F</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T → F ·</code>, <code class=\"mm\">F → id ·</code>, <code class=\"mm\">F → ( · E )</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E → E + · T</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T → T * · F</code>, <code class=\"mm\">F → ( E · )</code>, <code class=\"mm\">E → E + T ·</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T → T * F ·</code> and <code class=\"mm\">F → ( E ) ·</code>.</li><li>Counting them gives 12 states, which is also the LR(0) count and the LALR(1) count.</li><li>The canonical LR(1) automaton, by contrast, keeps separate copies of several of these states and has 22.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A) — 12 states.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Quoting the canonical LR(1) count for an LALR question. Whenever no two states share a core with different lookaheads, LALR and LR(0) have the same state count.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q24", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which one of the following is a genuine limitation of operator-precedence parsing?", "options": ["It cannot parse grammars that contain left recursion.", "It cannot parse expressions that use more than one operator.", "It applies only to operator grammars, so ε-productions and adjacent nonterminals must be removed first.", "It needs two tokens of lookahead where LR(1) needs one."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The method needs every handle to be bordered by terminals, which is exactly what an operator grammar guarantees: no <code class=\"mm\">ε</code>-production and no right side with two adjacent nonterminals.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Left recursion is harmless: the classic expression grammar <code class=\"mm\">E → E + E | E * E</code> is left recursive and is parsed by this method.</li><li>Several operators are the normal case; the relations handle them pair by pair.</li><li>One token of lookahead is enough: the decision uses only the topmost stack terminal and the next input symbol.</li><li>The real restriction is grammatical: a production such as <code class=\"mm\">A → B C</code> or <code class=\"mm\">A → ε</code> leaves a handle that is not bordered by terminals, so the relation table cannot decide.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the method is limited by recursion or by the number of operators. The limitation is the operator-grammar restriction, which excludes whole families of grammars regardless of how simple their operators are.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-Q25", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-27", "text": "Which of the following statements about the moves of a shift-reduce parser are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Shift pushes the next input symbol onto the stack and advances the input pointer.", "Reduce pops the handle from the top of the stack and pushes the left side of the production that matched it.", "Accept is announced as soon as the stack holds the start symbol, whether or not input remains.", "An error is detected as soon as the table has no action for the current state and the current lookahead.", "Every reduce pops exactly one symbol from the stack."], "answer_display": "Options ((A), (B)) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A shift-reduce parser performs four kinds of move: shift, reduce, accept and error. Accept needs the stack to hold exactly the start symbol <i>and</i> the input to be exhausted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) true: one input symbol moves onto the stack, and the input pointer advances by one.</li><li>(B) true: <code class=\"mm\">| ω |</code> symbols leave the stack and the left side <code class=\"mm\">A</code> takes their place, so the stack can also shrink by more than one symbol.</li><li>(C) false: with input still unread, holding the start symbol is not acceptance; the parser must also see <code class=\"mm\">$</code>.</li><li>(D) true: an empty ACTION entry is the parser’s only way to notice that no legal move exists, and it reports the error immediately.</li><li>(E) false: a reduce by <code class=\"mm\">A → X Y Z</code> pops three symbols.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking reduce always replaces one symbol by one symbol. It replaces a whole right side, of any length, by a single nonterminal.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q01", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a state of the canonical LR(0) collection the items are split into kernel items and items added by CLOSURE. Which one of the following is a non-kernel (closure) item by definition?", "options": ["S′ → · S, the initial item of the collection.", "Any item whose dot stands at the left end, other than S′ → · S.", "Any item whose dot does not stand at the left end.", "Any item whose dot stands at the right end."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The kernel of a state is <code class=\"mm\">S′ → · S</code> together with every item whose dot is not at the left end; everything else in the state was added by CLOSURE because the dot stands before a nonterminal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">S′ → · S</code> is the kernel of the initial state, by definition.</li><li>An item such as <code class=\"mm\">A → X · Y Z</code> was produced by advancing the dot, so it is a kernel item.</li><li>An item such as <code class=\"mm\">A → · X Y Z</code> enters a state only through the CLOSURE rule, so it is a non-kernel item — unless it is <code class=\"mm\">S′ → · S</code>.</li><li>An item with the dot at the right end, such as <code class=\"mm\">A → X Y Z ·</code>, is a complete (reduce) item, but it is still a kernel item because its dot did not start at the left end.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating complete items as closure items because they are the ones that trigger a reduction. Kernel versus closure is decided by how the dot got there, not by whether the item can be reduced.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q02", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Before the canonical LR(0) collection is built, the grammar is augmented with a new production S′ → S. Why is this augmentation done?", "options": ["It removes every ε-production from the grammar.", "It makes the start symbol appear on the right side of exactly one production, so that a single item S′ → S · marks acceptance and the parser stops in exactly one state.", "It makes the grammar unambiguous.", "It guarantees that the grammar is LR(0)."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The parser must know when to accept. Acceptance is a property of one distinguished item, and the augmented production supplies it without disturbing the rest of the grammar.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Without augmentation the start symbol may occur on the right side of other productions, so several states could contain a complete item with the start symbol on the left.</li><li>Adding <code class=\"mm\">S′ → S</code> guarantees that the start symbol occurs on the right side of exactly one production, so exactly one state contains <code class=\"mm\">S′ → S ·</code>.</li><li>That item alone is turned into the accept action; no other state can ever accept.</li><li>Augmentation changes neither ambiguity nor the class of the grammar, so options (C) and (D) are wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that augmentation simplifies or repairs the grammar. It adds one production and one item; it repairs nothing except the uniqueness of the accept action.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q03", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the expression grammar E → E + T | T, T → T * F | F, F → ( E ) | id, how many LR(0) items does the augmented grammar contain in total? Count every item of every production, including the items of the added production E′ → E. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "20", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A production whose right side has <code class=\"mm\">n</code> symbols has exactly <code class=\"mm\">n + 1</code> LR(0) items, one for each position of the dot.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">E′ → E</code> has 1 symbol, so 2 items.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">E → E + T</code> has 3 symbols, so 4 items; <code class=\"mm\">E → T</code> has 1 symbol, so 2 items.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T → T * F</code> gives 4 items; <code class=\"mm\">T → F</code> gives 2 items.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">F → ( E )</code> gives 4 items; <code class=\"mm\">F → id</code> gives 2 items.</li><li>Total: 2 + 4 + 2 + 4 + 2 + 4 + 2 = 20.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 20</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting one item per production (7), which forgets the dot positions, or forgetting the augmented production and answering 18.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q04", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the grammar S → C C, C → c C | d, the initial state is I₀ = CLOSURE({S′ → · S}). Which set of items is GOTO(I₀, C)?", "options": ["{S → C · C, C → · c C, C → · d}", "{S → C · C}", "{S′ → S ·}", "{C → c · C, C → · c C, C → · d}"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">GOTO(I, X)</code> advances the dot over <code class=\"mm\">X</code> in every item of I that has the dot before <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, and then closes the resulting set.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The only item of <code class=\"mm\">I₀</code> with the dot before <code class=\"mm\">C</code> is <code class=\"mm\">S → · C C</code>; advancing the dot gives the kernel <code class=\"mm\">S → C · C</code>.</li><li>The dot now precedes <code class=\"mm\">C</code>, so CLOSURE adds the productions of <code class=\"mm\">C</code> with the dot at the left end: <code class=\"mm\">C → · c C</code> and <code class=\"mm\">C → · d</code>.</li><li>Option (B) stops before CLOSURE; option (C) is <code class=\"mm\">GOTO(I₀, S)</code>; option (D) is <code class=\"mm\">GOTO(I₀, c)</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the CLOSURE step, which gives the bare kernel, or shifting the wrong symbol and returning the state reached on <code class=\"mm\">c</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q05", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the grammar S → C C, C → c C | d, how many states does the canonical collection of LR(0) items contain? Count the initial state. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The states are the distinct closed item sets reachable from the initial state by repeated GOTO.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">I₀ = {S′ → · S, S → · C C, C → · c C, C → · d}</code>.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">S</code>: <code class=\"mm\">{S′ → S ·}</code>.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">C</code>: <code class=\"mm\">{S → C · C, C → · c C, C → · d}</code>.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">c</code> from <code class=\"mm\">I₀</code> and from that state: <code class=\"mm\">{C → c · C, C → · c C, C → · d}</code>, a single shared state.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">d</code>: <code class=\"mm\">{C → d ·}</code>; on <code class=\"mm\">C</code> from <code class=\"mm\">{C → c · C, …}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">{C → c C ·}</code>; on <code class=\"mm\">C</code> from <code class=\"mm\">{S → C · C, …}</code>: <code class=\"mm\">{S → C C ·}</code>.</li><li>Counting the initial state: 7 states.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 7</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting that the two occurrences of <code class=\"mm\">c</code> lead to the same state, which gives 8, or dropping the accept state and answering 6.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q06", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about viable prefixes and the LR parser’s stack are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The stack of a shift-reduce LR parser always holds a viable prefix of the grammar.", "Every prefix of a right-sentential form is a viable prefix.", "A viable prefix is a prefix of a right-sentential form that does not continue past the right end of the rightmost handle of that sentential form.", "The set of viable prefixes of a grammar is a regular language, and the canonical LR(0) automaton is a DFA that recognises it.", "The number of states of the canonical LR(0) collection equals the number of distinct viable prefixes."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The correctness of LR parsing rests on one invariant: the symbols on the stack, read as a string, always form a viable prefix, and every viable prefix labels a path from the initial state.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the invariant of the parser: shift appends one symbol, reduce replaces a handle by its left side, and both moves preserve the viable-prefix property.</li><li>(B) is false. A prefix that runs past the right end of the handle is not a viable prefix, which is why the parser must not read past the handle before reducing.</li><li>(C) is the standard definition of a viable prefix.</li><li>(D) is true: the item sets form a DFA whose states are the equivalence classes of viable prefixes.</li><li>(E) is false: there are infinitely many viable prefixes but finitely many states, so several prefixes lead to the same state.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every prefix of a right-sentential form is viable, which would let the parser shift forever without reducing.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q07", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr0-items-parsing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about CLOSURE, GOTO and the states of the canonical LR(0) collection are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["CLOSURE(I) is the smallest set containing I and closed under the rule: if A → α · B β is in the set and B → γ is a production, then B → · γ is in the set.", "An item whose dot stands at the left end is a kernel item only when its left side is the start symbol.", "GOTO(I, X) is the CLOSURE of the set of items A → α X · β taken over all A → α · X β in I; when no item of I has the dot before X, GOTO(I, X) is empty.", "The initial state of the collection is CLOSURE({S′ → · S}).", "Every grammar has exactly one state in its canonical LR(0) collection that contains a completed item."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The collection is built from two operations only: CLOSURE, which adds the productions of a nonterminal that stands after the dot, and GOTO, which advances the dot and closes again.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the CLOSURE rule, stated exactly as it is used.</li><li>(B) is false: a left-end item is a non-kernel item whether or not its left side is the start symbol; <code class=\"mm\">S′ → · S</code> is simply the one left-end item that is also a kernel item.</li><li>(C) is the definition of GOTO, including the empty-set case.</li><li>(D) is the definition of the initial state.</li><li>(E) is false: a grammar with several productions that can complete will have several such states — for <code class=\"mm\">S → C C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C → c C | d</code> there are four.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading CLOSURE as \"add every production of every nonterminal in the set\". CLOSURE looks only at the symbol immediately after the dot.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q08", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In the parsing table of an SLR(1) parser, which transitions are recorded in the GOTO part rather than in ACTION?", "options": ["Shifts on terminals.", "Transitions on nonterminals.", "Reductions by completed items.", "The accept action."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The table has two halves: ACTION decides what the parser does when it sees a terminal, GOTO tells it which state to enter after a reduction has pushed a nonterminal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>ACTION is indexed by a state and a terminal, and its entries are shift, reduce, accept or error.</li><li>GOTO is indexed by a state and a nonterminal, and its entry is the state to move to.</li><li>Shifts, reductions and accept therefore all live in ACTION; only nonterminal transitions live in GOTO.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (B)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Putting nonterminal transitions in ACTION, which breaks the parser after every reduction.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q09", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "An LR(0) parser and an SLR(1) parser are built from the same canonical collection. Which statement describes the difference between their ACTION tables?", "options": ["The LR(0) table enters the reduction by A → ω in every column, whereas SLR(1) enters it only in the columns of the terminals in FOLLOW(A).", "The LR(0) table uses two symbols of lookahead, while SLR(1) uses one.", "The LR(0) table contains no reduce actions at all.", "The SLR(1) table reduces in every column, while the LR(0) table restricts reductions to FOLLOW sets."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> LR(0) has no lookahead, so a completed item forbids every shift in its state. SLR(1) attaches one token of lookahead and reduces only where that token can follow the left side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>In the LR(0) table, a state holding <code class=\"mm\">A → ω ·</code> reduces on any terminal whatsoever; the table is filled in every column.</li><li>That is why LR(0) fails on almost every practical grammar: the reduction collides with any shift in the same state.</li><li>SLR(1) narrows the reduction to the columns of <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A)</code>, which removes most of those collisions.</li><li>Options (B), (C) and (D) reverse the roles or invent lookahead that LR(0) does not have.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking that SLR(1) differs from LR(0) in the automaton it builds. The states are identical; only the placement of the reduce entries changes.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q10", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the grammar S → L = R | R, L → * R | id, R → L, the SLR(1) table contains exactly one conflict. Which one is it?", "options": ["A shift/reduce conflict on =, in the state that holds S → L · = R and R → L ·.", "A reduce/reduce conflict on =, between R → L and S → R.", "A shift/reduce conflict on *, in the initial state.", "None — the grammar is SLR(1)."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A shift/reduce conflict appears when one state holds both a completed item and an item with the dot before a terminal, and that terminal lies in the FOLLOW set of the completed item’s left side.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(R) = {$, =}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(L) = {$, =}</code>.</li><li>The state reached from the initial state on <code class=\"mm\">L</code> holds <code class=\"mm\">S → L · = R</code> and <code class=\"mm\">R → L ·</code>.</li><li>On <code class=\"mm\">=</code> the first item asks for a shift and the second, because <code class=\"mm\">=</code> ∈ FOLLOW(R), asks for a reduction by <code class=\"mm\">R → L</code>.</li><li>That is one shift/reduce conflict on <code class=\"mm\">=</code>; no other cell conflicts, so the grammar is not SLR(1).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that any conflict must be reduce/reduce because two items share a state. Here the two items are one shift item and one reduce item, so the conflict is shift/reduce.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q11", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following chains of strict inclusions between grammar classes is correct?", "options": ["LR(0) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ LR(1)", "LR(0) ⊂ LR(1) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1)", "SLR(1) ⊂ LR(0) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ LR(1)", "LALR(1) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LR(1) ⊂ LR(0)"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each class is obtained from the previous one by adding information, never by removing it, so the accepted class of grammars grows strictly at every step.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>LR(0) uses no lookahead, so it is the weakest of the four.</li><li>SLR(1) adds FOLLOW sets, which strictly enlarges the class.</li><li>LALR(1) adds exact lookaheads but keeps the LR(0) cores, which again strictly enlarges the class.</li><li>LR(1) keeps full canonical lookaheads and is the largest of the four; a grammar that is LR(1) but not LALR(1) exists, so the last inclusion is strict too.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that LALR(1) is stronger than LR(1) because its tables are smaller. Size and power go the other way: LR(1) accepts more grammars.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q12", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "slr1-parsing-conflicts", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the grammar S → A a | b A c | d c | b d a, A → d, how many cells of the SLR(1) ACTION table contain a conflict? Count each (state, terminal) cell once. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Count conflicts cell by cell: a conflict exists exactly when one cell receives two different actions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A) = {a, c}</code>, because <code class=\"mm\">A</code> is followed by <code class=\"mm\">a</code> in <code class=\"mm\">S → A a</code> and by <code class=\"mm\">c</code> in <code class=\"mm\">S → b A c</code>.</li><li>The state reached on <code class=\"mm\">d</code> from the initial state holds <code class=\"mm\">A → d ·</code> and <code class=\"mm\">S → d · c</code>. On <code class=\"mm\">c</code> it must shift (for <code class=\"mm\">S → d · c</code>) and reduce <code class=\"mm\">A → d</code>, since <code class=\"mm\">c</code> ∈ FOLLOW(A). That is one conflicting cell.</li><li>The state reached on <code class=\"mm\">b d</code> holds <code class=\"mm\">A → d ·</code> and <code class=\"mm\">S → b d · a</code>. On <code class=\"mm\">a</code> it must shift and reduce, again because <code class=\"mm\">a</code> ∈ FOLLOW(A). That is the second conflicting cell.</li><li>Both conflicts are shift/reduce; no other cell receives two actions, so the total is 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 2</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Finding only the state reached on <code class=\"mm\">d</code> and answering 1. The grammar has two such states, one after <code class=\"mm\">d</code> and one after <code class=\"mm\">b d</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q13", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the same grammar, how do the canonical LR(1) collection and the LALR(1) collection compare in size?", "options": ["LALR(1) never has more states than canonical LR(1); its states are obtained by merging LR(1) states that share the same kernel.", "LALR(1) always has strictly more states than canonical LR(1).", "The two collections always have exactly the same number of states.", "The LALR(1) collection has as many states as the grammar has productions."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> LALR(1) is defined as a quotient of the canonical LR(1) collection: states with equal kernels are merged, so the merged collection can only shrink.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Merging partitions the LR(1) states, so the number of LALR(1) states is at most the number of LR(1) states.</li><li>For the grammar <code class=\"mm\">S → C C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C → c C | d</code> the canonical LR(1) collection has 10 states while the LALR(1) collection has 7, so the inequality is often strict.</li><li>The LALR(1) states have exactly the LR(0) kernels, which is why LALR(1) and SLR(1) tables for the same grammar always have the same number of states.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting LALR(1) to be smaller but stronger. It is smaller and slightly weaker: the merge can introduce reduce/reduce conflicts that canonical LR(1) does not have.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q14", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In the closure of the LR(1) item [A → α · B β, a], which lookaheads are attached to the added items B → · γ?", "options": ["Every terminal in FIRST(β a).", "Only the single terminal a.", "Every terminal in FOLLOW(B).", "Only the end marker $."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The lookahead of a new item records what can follow <code class=\"mm\">B</code> at this point of the parse: anything derived from <code class=\"mm\">β</code>, or <code class=\"mm\">a</code> itself when <code class=\"mm\">β</code> can vanish.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After <code class=\"mm\">B</code> is reduced, the parser still has to match <code class=\"mm\">β</code> and then the lookahead <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</li><li>So the terminals that can legally follow <code class=\"mm\">B</code> here are exactly those in <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(β a)</code>.</li><li>When <code class=\"mm\">β</code> derives ε, the whole of <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(β a)</code> collapses to <code class=\"mm\">{a}</code>, which is why option (B) is the special case and not the rule.</li><li>FOLLOW(B) is the SLR(1) approximation: it unions the lookaheads over all contexts and is therefore weaker.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using FOLLOW(B) inside the LR(1) construction. That turns the canonical LR(1) automaton into an SLR(1) one and loses exactly the precision the extra states are paying for.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q15", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the grammar S → C C, C → c C | d, how many states does the canonical LR(1) collection contain? Count the initial state. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "10", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The canonical LR(1) collection separates states that have the same kernel but different lookaheads, so it can be larger than the LR(0) collection of the same grammar.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The LR(0) collection of this grammar has 7 states, and those 7 kernels are exactly the cores that appear in the LR(1) collection.</li><li>Three of them split, because the same kernel carries different lookaheads in different contexts: <code class=\"mm\">{C → c · C, C → · c C, C → · d}</code> splits into a copy with lookaheads <code class=\"mm\">{c, d}</code> and a copy with lookahead <code class=\"mm\">{$}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">{C → d ·}</code> splits the same way, into <code class=\"mm\">{c, d}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{$}</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">{C → c C ·}</code> splits likewise into <code class=\"mm\">{c, d}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">{$}</code>.</li><li>Total: 7 + 3 = 10 states. Merging the three pairs gives back the 7-state LALR(1) automaton, which is why this grammar is LALR(1).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 10</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 7 by assuming LR(1) and LR(0) collections always have the same number of states, or 13 by splitting every state.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q16", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Why is the canonical LR(1) collection usually much larger than the LALR(1) collection for the same grammar?", "options": ["Because each LR(1) item carries a specific lookahead, so two states with identical kernels but different lookaheads are kept apart.", "Because an LR(1) item carries a set of lookaheads while an LALR(1) item carries only one.", "Because the LR(1) construction adds an extra production to the grammar.", "Because an LR(1) parser uses two symbols of lookahead."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The identity of an LR(1) state is its set of items together with their lookaheads; the identity of an LALR(1) state is only its kernel.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Two contexts can reach the same kernel of items while permitting different lookaheads.</li><li>In the canonical construction those are two distinct states, so the collection grows.</li><li>LALR(1) deliberately merges them, keeping one state per kernel and unioning the lookaheads.</li><li>Option (B) reverses the two definitions — the LR(1) item holds a single lookahead, the merged LALR(1) item ends up with a set. Option (D) is wrong: LR(1) means one symbol of lookahead.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking that more states mean more lookahead. Both parsers read one symbol ahead; the difference is whether lookahead information is allowed to split a state.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q17", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about the construction of an LALR(1) table are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Merging two states that have the same kernel keeps every shift action, because shifts depend only on the dot positions and not on the lookaheads.", "In a merged state, a reduction is entered in exactly those columns given by the union of the lookaheads of the completed items that were merged.", "For any grammar, the LALR(1) collection has the same number of states as the SLR(1) collection of that grammar.", "An LALR(1) parser never performs a reduction earlier than the point at which a canonical LR(1) parser would have detected an error.", "Every grammar that is LALR(1) becomes SLR(1) once it has been left-factored."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> LALR(1) merges LR(1) states with equal kernels. Merging preserves the shift structure and only widens the lookaheads attached to reductions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: an item <code class=\"mm\">A → α · X β</code> asks for a shift on <code class=\"mm\">X</code> whatever its lookahead is, and merging cannot change the dot positions.</li><li>(B) is true and is the definition of the merged reduce entry.</li><li>(C) is true: both collections have exactly the LR(0) kernels as their states, so their state counts are equal — 7 for <code class=\"mm\">S → C C</code>, <code class=\"mm\">C → c C | d</code>, for example.</li><li>(D) is false: because a merged state may reduce on a lookahead that a canonical state would have used to detect an error, LALR(1) can delay error detection.</li><li>(E) is false: left-factoring changes the grammar, but <code class=\"mm\">S → A a | b A c | d c | b d a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">A → d</code> is LALR(1) and not SLR(1) and needs no factoring at all — the conflict comes from an imprecise FOLLOW set, not from a common prefix.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the merge is harmless. It never creates a shift/reduce conflict, but it can create a reduce/reduce conflict, and it is exactly that possibility that separates LALR(1) from LR(1).</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q18", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following grammars is LALR(1) but NOT SLR(1)?", "options": ["S → C C, C → c C | d", "S → a A d | b B d | a B e | b A e, A → c, B → c", "S → A a | b A c | d c | b d a, A → d", "S → a S | b S | a"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A grammar is LALR(1) but not SLR(1) when the FOLLOW sets are too coarse but the exact LALR lookaheads are not: the SLR table conflicts while the LALR table does not.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is SLR(1): its SLR table has no conflict at all.</li><li>(B) is LR(1) but not LALR(1): merging the states with the same kernel unions the lookaheads of <code class=\"mm\">A → c ·</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B → c ·</code> and produces reduce/reduce conflicts on <code class=\"mm\">d</code> and on <code class=\"mm\">e</code>. It fails at the LALR level, not the SLR level.</li><li>(C) has <code class=\"mm\">FOLLOW(A) = {a, c}</code>, so the SLR table reduces <code class=\"mm\">A → d</code> on both <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">c</code> and collides with a shift in two cells. In the LALR automaton the lookahead of <code class=\"mm\">A → d</code> is <code class=\"mm\">{a}</code> in one context and <code class=\"mm\">{c}</code> in the other, so both conflicts disappear. It is LALR(1) and not SLR(1).</li><li>(D) is SLR(1): its SLR table has no conflict.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Picking (B), which is the more famous example but fails one level too low: it is not LALR(1) at all.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q19", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "lr1-and-lalr1-parsers", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the grammar S → a A d | b B d | a B e | b A e, A → c, B → c, which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["It is LR(1).", "It is LALR(1).", "Merging the two states that share a kernel unions the lookaheads of A → c · and B → c ·, which creates reduce/reduce conflicts on d and on e.", "The canonical LR(1) collection has the same number of states as the LALR(1) collection.", "Every SLR(1) grammar is LR(1)."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (E)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> This is the standard witness that the inclusion LALR(1) ⊂ LR(1) is strict: two contexts reach the same kernel with disjoint lookaheads, and the merge destroys that distinction.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: the canonical LR(1) collection has 14 states and no conflict in any cell.</li><li>(B) is false — see (C).</li><li>(C) is true: after <code class=\"mm\">a c</code> the item <code class=\"mm\">A → c ·</code> has lookahead <code class=\"mm\">d</code>, and after <code class=\"mm\">b c</code> it has lookahead <code class=\"mm\">e</code>; the same holds for <code class=\"mm\">B → c ·</code> with the roles exchanged. Merging the two kernels unions the lookaheads, so both <code class=\"mm\">A → c</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B → c</code> become reducible on <code class=\"mm\">d</code> and on <code class=\"mm\">e</code>, giving two reduce/reduce conflicts.</li><li>(D) is false: the LR(1) collection has 14 states, the LALR(1) collection 13.</li><li>(E) is true: SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ LR(1), so every SLR(1) grammar is LR(1).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (C) and (E)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Concluding that the grammar is ambiguous because the LALR table conflicts. It is unambiguous and LR(1); only the merged table fails.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q20", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following grammars is an operator grammar?", "options": ["S → S S | a", "S → a S b | ε", "E → E + E | E * E | ( E ) | id", "S → A B, A → a, B → b"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An operator grammar has no ε-production and no production whose right side contains two adjacent nonterminals, so that any two adjacent terminals can be compared directly.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) has <code class=\"mm\">S S</code>, two adjacent nonterminals.</li><li>(B) has the ε-production <code class=\"mm\">S → ε</code>.</li><li>(C) satisfies both conditions: no production is empty and no right side has two adjacent nonterminals.</li><li>(D) has <code class=\"mm\">A B</code>, two adjacent nonterminals.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (C)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking that any expression grammar qualifies. The conditions are purely syntactic, on the right sides of the productions, and a grammar with <code class=\"mm\">S → S S</code> fails even though it has no ε.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q21", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "An operator-precedence parser keeps both terminals and nonterminals on its stack. Which comparison does it make at each step?", "options": ["It compares the topmost terminal on the stack with the next input symbol, ignoring the nonterminals above that terminal.", "It compares the symbol on top of the stack, whatever kind it is, with the next input symbol.", "It compares the top two symbols of the stack with each other.", "It compares the leftmost terminal on the stack with the last symbol of the input."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The precedence relations are defined between terminals only, so the parser must look past the nonterminals sitting above the topmost terminal.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>After a reduction the stack top is a nonterminal, and no relation is defined between a nonterminal and a terminal.</li><li>The parser therefore scans down to the first terminal, which is always the terminal that borders the handle it is about to recognise.</li><li>It then compares that terminal with the next input symbol to choose shift or reduce.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Comparing the raw stack top. That works only until the first reduction, after which the top is a nonterminal and the comparison is undefined.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q22", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about operator-precedence parsing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The precedence relations are computed from FIRST and LAST of the nonterminals of the grammar.", "When the relation between the topmost stack terminal and the next input symbol is ≌, the parser shifts.", "An operator grammar may contain a production whose right side is ε.", "If no precedence relation holds between the topmost stack terminal and the next input symbol, the parser reports an error, because the input is not a sentence of the grammar.", "The precedence relations form a partial order on the terminals: they are transitive and antisymmetric."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The relations come from FIRST and LAST alone, and each of the three relations maps to one parser action: shift on <code class=\"mm\">&lt;·</code>, shift on <code class=\"mm\">≌</code>, reduce on <code class=\"mm\">·&gt;</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the construction: <code class=\"mm\">a &lt;· b</code> when <code class=\"mm\">b</code> starts a string derivable from a nonterminal that follows <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">a ·&gt; b</code> when <code class=\"mm\">a</code> ends one that precedes <code class=\"mm\">b</code>.</li><li>(B) is true: <code class=\"mm\">≌</code> means the two terminals are adjacent inside one handle, so both are shifted before that handle is reduced.</li><li>(C) is false: an operator grammar has no ε-production by definition.</li><li>(D) is true: a missing relation means the pair can never occur in a sentential form, so the input is not derivable.</li><li>(E) is false: the relations are neither transitive nor antisymmetric in general; <code class=\"mm\">+ ·&gt; +</code> and <code class=\"mm\">+ &lt;· +</code> can both hold in different tables.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Options (A), (B) and (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">≌</code> as \"reduce\". It means the handle has been completed on both sides of the pair, so the parser shifts first and reduces afterwards.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q23", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the grammar E → E + E | E * E | ( E ) | id, which pair of terminals has no precedence relation in the table derived from FIRST and LAST?", "options": ["id and +", "+ and id", "( and id", ") and ("], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">FIRST(E) = {(, id}</code> and <code class=\"mm\">LAST(E) = {), id}</code>. A relation between <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">b</code> exists only when the grammar can put <code class=\"mm\">b</code> immediately after <code class=\"mm\">a</code> in a sentential form.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">id ·&gt; +</code>, because in <code class=\"mm\">E → E + E</code> the left <code class=\"mm\">E</code> can end with <code class=\"mm\">id</code> and is followed by <code class=\"mm\">+</code>. So option (A) is not the answer.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">+ &lt;· id</code>, because the right <code class=\"mm\">E</code> of the same production can begin with <code class=\"mm\">id</code>. So option (B) is not the answer.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">( &lt;· id</code>, from <code class=\"mm\">E → ( E )</code>. So option (C) is not the answer.</li><li>No production puts <code class=\"mm\">(</code> immediately after <code class=\"mm\">)</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">)</code> is in LAST, never in FIRST, so <code class=\"mm\">) </code> and <code class=\"mm\">(</code> have no relation at all.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (D)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that every pair of terminals must carry some relation. Of the 25 ordered pairs of these five terminals only 13 do: 6 satisfy <code class=\"mm\">&lt;·</code>, 6 satisfy <code class=\"mm\">·&gt;</code> and 1 satisfies <code class=\"mm\">≌</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q24", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the grammar E → E + E | E * E | ( E ) | id, using the relations derived from FIRST and LAST, how many ordered pairs (a, b) of the five terminals +, *, (, ), id satisfy a <· b? Count each ordered pair once. (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "6", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> <code class=\"mm\">a &lt;· b</code> holds when <code class=\"mm\">a</code> is a terminal and <code class=\"mm\">b</code> can begin the string that follows it, that is when <code class=\"mm\">b</code> ∈ FIRST of the symbol after <code class=\"mm\">a</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">FIRST(E) = {(, id}</code>.</li><li>From <code class=\"mm\">E → E + E</code>: <code class=\"mm\">+</code> is followed by <code class=\"mm\">E</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">+ &lt;· id</code> and <code class=\"mm\">+ &lt;· (</code> — 2 pairs.</li><li>From <code class=\"mm\">E → E * E</code>: <code class=\"mm\">* &lt;· id</code> and <code class=\"mm\">* &lt;· (</code> — 2 more.</li><li>From <code class=\"mm\">E → ( E )</code>: <code class=\"mm\">(</code> is followed by <code class=\"mm\">E</code>, giving <code class=\"mm\">( &lt;· id</code> and <code class=\"mm\">( &lt;· (</code> — 2 more.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">id</code> are followed by nothing inside any right side, so no pair starts with them. Total 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: 6</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the pairs of the form <code class=\"mm\">·&gt;</code> as well, which also number 6 and would give 12, or including the end marker <code class=\"mm\">$</code>, which adds two more.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U3-S2-Q25", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 3, "unit_slug": "bottom-up-parsing", "subtopic": "operator-precedence-parsing", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In the table derived from FIRST and LAST for E → E + E | E * E | ( E ) | id, no relation holds between + and +. What does this tell us?", "options": ["The grammar cannot be parsed by an operator-precedence parser built only from FIRST and LAST: reducing E + E before a second + needs an associativity convention that the relations do not supply.", "The grammar is not context-free.", "The string id + id + id is not in the language of the grammar.", "The table is complete: on the second + the parser shifts and the parse succeeds."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The FIRST/LAST relations record where terminals can stand relative to each other. They cannot decide associativity, because that is a property of how the grammar is used, not of its shape.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">+</code> is followed by <code class=\"mm\">E</code> in <code class=\"mm\">E → E + E</code>, so the only <code class=\"mm\">&lt;·</code> pairs for <code class=\"mm\">+</code> are <code class=\"mm\">+ &lt;· id</code> and <code class=\"mm\">+ &lt;· (</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">+</code> is preceded by <code class=\"mm\">E</code>, and <code class=\"mm\">LAST(E) = {), id}</code>, so the only <code class=\"mm\">·&gt;</code> pairs for <code class=\"mm\">+</code> are <code class=\"mm\">id ·&gt; +</code> and <code class=\"mm\">) ·&gt; +</code>.</li><li>Hence <code class=\"mm\">(+, +)</code> carries no relation, yet the string <code class=\"mm\">id + id + id</code> requires the parser to compare <code class=\"mm\">+</code> with <code class=\"mm\">+</code> at the second plus.</li><li>A practical parser therefore imposes precedence levels and left associativity, which adds <code class=\"mm\">+ ·&gt; +</code> and <code class=\"mm\">* ·&gt; +</code> by hand. The FIRST/LAST derivation alone is incomplete for an ambiguous grammar.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><span class=\"ansline\">Answer: Option (A)</span></p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that a complete FIRST/LAST derivation always yields a usable table. For an ambiguous grammar it leaves gaps, and those gaps are exactly where associativity has to be supplied.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q01", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A syntax-directed definition (SDD) is called S-attributed when:", "options": ["every attribute it uses is a synthesized attribute", "every attribute it uses is an inherited attribute", "inherited attributes are used only at the leaves of the parse tree", "its semantic rules are evaluated before the parse tree is built"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An SDD attaches a set of semantic rules to each production. If every attribute it defines is a synthesized attribute, the definition is S-attributed; such a definition can be evaluated by one bottom-up, postorder pass over the parse tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The definition of S-attributed turns on one question only: are all attributes synthesized? Option (A) states exactly that.</li><li>Option (B) describes the opposite extreme, where every attribute is inherited; no definition with an inherited attribute can be S-attributed.</li><li>Option (C) is a restriction on where inherited attributes may appear, not the definition of S-attributed; and a definition with an inherited attribute is not S-attributed wherever the attribute appears.</li><li>Option (D) confuses evaluation with construction: semantic rules are applied to nodes of a parse tree, which must therefore exist first. An S-attributed definition is evaluated during a bottom-up parse as each reduction completes, not before the tree exists.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the &ldquo;S&rdquo; as &ldquo;synthesized at the root&rdquo; or as a statement about where attributes are stored. The letter marks the attribute kind, and the test is that no inherited attribute occurs anywhere in the definition.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q02", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following statements about S-attributed and L-attributed definitions is TRUE?", "options": ["Every L-attributed definition is also S-attributed.", "Every S-attributed definition is L-attributed, but an L-attributed definition need not be S-attributed.", "The class of S-attributed definitions and the class of L-attributed definitions coincide.", "Neither class is contained in the other."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An L-attributed definition allows an inherited attribute of a node to depend only on attributes of its parent, of its left siblings and of itself, together with synthesized attributes of its own children. An S-attributed definition uses no inherited attributes at all, so every one of its dependencies is automatically of the permitted kind.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take any S-attributed definition. Its rules compute synthesized attributes from the attributes of children, which are themselves synthesized. No rule reads a right sibling, a parent or an inherited attribute, so every rule satisfies the L-attributed restriction.</li><li>Hence S-attributed &sube; L-attributed.</li><li>The inclusion is strict. The standard example is the definition for a list of identifiers, <code class=\"mm\">D &rarr; T L</code> with <code class=\"mm\">L.inh = T.type</code>: it carries an inherited attribute and is L-attributed, but it is not S-attributed.</li><li>Options (A) and (C) deny the strict inclusion, and option (D) denies the inclusion altogether.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the two classes are the same because both can be evaluated in a single pass. The passes differ: an S-attributed definition is evaluated bottom-up, an L-attributed one left-to-right depth-first, and only the latter can carry inherited information downwards.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q03", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following are permitted in an L-attributed definition? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["An inherited attribute of a node may be computed from an inherited attribute of its parent.", "An inherited attribute of a node may be computed from an attribute of a sibling to its left.", "An inherited attribute of a node may be computed from an attribute of a sibling to its right.", "A synthesized attribute of a node may be computed from an inherited attribute of the same node.", "An L-attributed definition can always be evaluated in one left-to-right, depth-first pass over the parse tree."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (D) and (E)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> L-attributed means &ldquo;left to right&rdquo;: every inherited attribute of a node depends only on information that is already available when the traversal reaches that node from the left. Concretely, an inherited attribute of <code class=\"mm\">X</code> may depend on inherited attributes of <code class=\"mm\">X</code>&rsquo;s parent, on attributes of <code class=\"mm\">X</code>&rsquo;s left siblings, and on inherited attributes of <code class=\"mm\">X</code> itself; a synthesized attribute of <code class=\"mm\">X</code> may depend on anything at <code class=\"mm\">X</code> or below it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is permitted: the parent&rsquo;s inherited attributes are computed before the traversal descends to the child.</li><li>(B) is permitted: a left sibling is fully visited before the traversal reaches <code class=\"mm\">X</code>.</li><li>(C) is not permitted: a right sibling has not been visited yet, so its attributes are unknown at the moment <code class=\"mm\">X</code> is visited. This single case is what separates L-attributed from the general class.</li><li>(D) is permitted: a synthesized attribute may read the node&rsquo;s own inherited attributes, which are known before the node is processed.</li><li>(E) follows from the definition: because every inherited attribute is available on entry and every synthesized attribute is available on return, one left-to-right depth-first pass computes all of them.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B), (D) and (E).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that an inherited attribute may read a right sibling because &ldquo;the whole tree is there anyway&rdquo;. The tree exists, but a single pass has not yet computed the right sibling&rsquo;s values, so such a definition is not L-attributed.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q04", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Consider the SDD: D → T L with the rule L.inh = T.type; T → int with the rule T.type = integer; L → L&#8321; , id with the rules L&#8321;.inh = L.inh and L.type = L&#8321;.type; L → id with the rule L.type = L.inh. This definition is:", "options": ["S-attributed.", "neither S-attributed nor L-attributed.", "L-attributed but not S-attributed.", "both S-attributed and L-attributed."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Decide the two questions separately. Is any attribute inherited? If yes, the definition is not S-attributed. Is every inherited attribute computed only from the parent&rsquo;s inherited attributes, from left siblings and from the node itself? If yes, it is L-attributed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">L.inh</code> is an inherited attribute &mdash; it is defined by rules attached to the <i>parent</i> production and flows downwards. So the definition is not S-attributed, which rules out (A) and (D).</li><li>Check each inherited rule. <code class=\"mm\">L.inh = T.type</code> reads a left sibling of <code class=\"mm\">L</code>, which is allowed.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">L&#8321;.inh = L.inh</code> reads the parent&rsquo;s inherited attribute, which is allowed.</li><li>The synthesized rules <code class=\"mm\">L.type = L&#8321;.type</code> and <code class=\"mm\">L.type = L.inh</code> read a child and the node&rsquo;s own inherited attribute, both allowed.</li><li>No rule reads a right sibling, so the definition is L-attributed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C) &mdash; L-attributed but not S-attributed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Classifying a definition as S-attributed because every attribute is eventually <i>passed upwards</i> or because the grammar is not left-recursive. The classification depends on the direction each attribute flows, not on the shape of the grammar.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q05", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following semantic rules, added to an otherwise L-attributed definition, would make it neither S-attributed nor L-attributed?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">X.syn = Y.syn + Z.syn</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> and <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> are the two children of <code class=\"mm\">X</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">Y.inh = X.inh</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">Y</code> is a child of <code class=\"mm\">X</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">X.inh = Z.syn</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">Z</code> is the sibling immediately to the right of <code class=\"mm\">X</code>.", "<code class=\"mm\">X.syn = X.inh</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">X.inh</code> is an inherited attribute of <code class=\"mm\">X</code>."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A rule breaks S-attributedness as soon as it mentions an inherited attribute, and it breaks L-attributedness when an inherited attribute depends on something not yet available in a left-to-right depth-first traversal &mdash; a right sibling is the standard case.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) uses only synthesized attributes of children: perfectly S-attributed, hence also L-attributed.</li><li>(B) is the archetypal L-attributed rule &mdash; information flowing from parent to child. It does break S-attributedness, but not L-attributedness, so it does not give &ldquo;neither&rdquo;.</li><li>(C) makes an inherited attribute of <code class=\"mm\">X</code> depend on a right sibling. When the traversal reaches <code class=\"mm\">X</code>, that sibling has not been visited and its synthesized attribute has no value, so the rule cannot be evaluated in one pass. It also uses an inherited attribute, so it is not S-attributed. The definition is neither.</li><li>(D) reads the node&rsquo;s own inherited attribute, which is available on entry: L-attributed, though not S-attributed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking that a rule involving an inherited attribute automatically destroys L-attributedness. Inherited attributes are the whole point of the class; only their <i>source</i> is restricted.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q06", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about the evaluation of attributes are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A parse tree for an S-attributed definition can be annotated by one bottom-up, postorder traversal.", "Every syntax-directed definition can be evaluated during a single pass over its parse tree.", "A parse tree for an L-attributed definition can be annotated by one left-to-right, depth-first traversal.", "Dependencies among inherited attributes can form a cycle, so that no evaluation order exists for that parse tree.", "If the dependency graph of a particular parse tree is acyclic, a topological order of that graph gives a valid evaluation order."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C), (D) and (E)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Attributes and semantic rules induce a directed graph on the annotated parse tree: an edge runs from every attribute used on the right of a rule to the attribute defined on the left. Any topological order of that graph is a legal evaluation order, and a single pass is possible only when a pass order happens to respect every edge.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the standard result: synthesized attributes of a node depend only on its children, so finishing the children first, in postorder, computes every value exactly once.</li><li>(B) is false. A definition whose rules need information from a right sibling, or whose dependency graph is circular, cannot be evaluated in one pass; it needs several passes or a separate dependency analysis.</li><li>(C) is the definition of L-attributed restated as an evaluation strategy: inherited attributes are available on the way down, synthesized ones on the way up.</li><li>(D) is true. With <code class=\"mm\">A.syn = B.syn</code> in one production and <code class=\"mm\">B.inh = A.inh</code> plus <code class=\"mm\">A.inh = B.syn</code> arranged around a cycle of nodes, the graph can close on itself; such a definition has no evaluation order at all.</li><li>(E) is true and is the general algorithm: build the graph for the given tree and sort it. A single pass is then a special case in which the pass order is already topological.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (C), (D) and (E).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that because a parse tree is finite, some evaluation order must exist. Finiteness does not prevent a cycle: the dependency graph can be circular even on a tree with three nodes.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q07", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the grammar E → E + T | T, T → T * F | F, F → ( E ) | id, let every E, T and F node carry one synthesized attribute val. For the input x + y * z, how many instances of val are there in the annotated parse tree? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "8", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In an annotated parse tree, each attribute of each node is one instance. Counting instances is therefore counting the nodes that carry the attribute &mdash; here every <code class=\"mm\">E</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T</code> and <code class=\"mm\">F</code> node, but not the <code class=\"mm\">id</code> leaves, which carry <code class=\"mm\">lexval</code> instead.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The grammar forces the shape of the tree. <code class=\"mm\">x + y * z</code> parses as <code class=\"mm\">E &rarr; E + T</code> at the root, because <code class=\"mm\">+</code> is the outermost operator; the right operand <code class=\"mm\">y * z</code> is a <code class=\"mm\">T</code>.</li><li>Root: <code class=\"mm\">E&#8320; &rarr; E&#8321; + T&#8320;</code>. That is two <code class=\"mm\">E</code> nodes and one <code class=\"mm\">T</code> node so far.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">E&#8321; &rarr; T&#8321; &rarr; F&#8321; &rarr; id x</code>: one <code class=\"mm\">T</code> and one <code class=\"mm\">F</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T&#8320; &rarr; T&#8322; * F&#8322;</code>: two <code class=\"mm\">T</code>-side nodes, one <code class=\"mm\">T&#8322;</code> and one <code class=\"mm\">F&#8322;</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">T&#8322; &rarr; F&#8323; &rarr; id y</code>: one more <code class=\"mm\">F</code>. <code class=\"mm\">F&#8322; &rarr; id z</code>.</li><li>Total: <code class=\"mm\">E</code> nodes 2, <code class=\"mm\">T</code> nodes 3, <code class=\"mm\">F</code> nodes 3 &rarr; 2 + 3 + 3 = 8.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 8 instances of <code class=\"mm\">val</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the three <code class=\"mm\">id</code> leaves as well and answering 11, or forgetting that <code class=\"mm\">E&#8321; &rarr; T</code> contributes a node of its own and answering 7.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q08", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following is NOT a valid three-address instruction?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">x = y + z</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">x[i] = y</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">if x &lt; y goto L</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">x = y * z * w</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A three-address instruction names at most three addresses: normally two operands and one result, plus the operator. A binary operator is therefore the most an instruction may contain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">x = y + z</code> has result <code class=\"mm\">x</code> and operands <code class=\"mm\">y</code>, <code class=\"mm\">z</code>: valid, the canonical form.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">x[i] = y</code> is the indexed copy instruction with result <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, index <code class=\"mm\">i</code> and source <code class=\"mm\">y</code>: valid.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">if x &lt; y goto L</code> is the conditional jump with operands <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, <code class=\"mm\">y</code> and target <code class=\"mm\">L</code>: valid.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">x = y * z * w</code> contains two operators and four addresses, so it must be broken into two instructions, for example <code class=\"mm\">t1 = y * z</code> followed by <code class=\"mm\">x = t1 * w</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading &ldquo;three-address&rdquo; as &ldquo;three variables may appear&rdquo; and therefore accepting a longer expression. The limit is on operators per instruction, which is what keeps each instruction cheap to translate and to reorder.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q09", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In the quadruple representation of three-address code, the four fields are, in order:", "options": ["operator, arg1, arg2, result", "operator, result, arg1, arg2", "result, arg1, arg2, operator", "arg1, arg2, operator, result"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A quadruple records one instruction as four slots: the operator and its two arguments, followed by the name of the result. A temporary appears explicitly in the result field.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Take <code class=\"mm\">t1 = b * c</code>. The operator is <code class=\"mm\">*</code>, arg1 is <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, arg2 is <code class=\"mm\">c</code> and the result is <code class=\"mm\">t1</code>.</li><li>So the fields read operator, arg1, arg2, result &mdash; option (A).</li><li>Instructions that do not need both arguments leave a slot empty: for <code class=\"mm\">t1 = -c</code> the operator is unary minus, arg1 is <code class=\"mm\">c</code>, arg2 is unused, and for a plain copy <code class=\"mm\">x = t1</code> the operator is <code class=\"mm\">=</code> with arg1 <code class=\"mm\">t1</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting the result first because it is written first in the source statement. In the quadruple table the destination sits in the fourth column, which is why a reader has to scan to the end of the row to see where a value lands.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q10", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Why are indirect triples often preferred to plain triples?", "options": ["They store the result of every instruction in an explicitly named temporary.", "They keep a separate array of pointers to the triples, so the instructions can be reordered without rewriting the positional references inside them.", "They need fewer instructions than quadruples for the same expression.", "They remove the need for temporaries altogether."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In a triple, an operand that holds the value of an earlier computation is written as that triple&rsquo;s index, for example <code class=\"mm\">(3)</code>. This makes the position of a triple part of the meaning of other triples.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Because positions are cited by number, any reordering of a plain triple list invalidates every reference into it. Optimisations such as moving code out of a loop do exactly that.</li><li>Indirect triples add one level of indirection: a separate array holds pointers (or indices) to the triples, and the program order is the order of that pointer array.</li><li>Reordering now means permuting the pointer array only; the triples keep their indices and their internal references stay correct.</li><li>Options (A) and (D) describe quadruples and temporaries wrongly, and (C) is false: all three representations describe the same instructions, only the bookkeeping differs.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing indirect triples save instructions or storage. They add a pointer array; what they buy is the freedom to reorder code cheaply, which is why optimising compilers prefer them.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q11", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the assignment a = b * -c + d, using one three-address instruction per operator and one for the final assignment, and never reusing a temporary, how many three-address instructions are needed? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Translating an expression to three-address code emits one instruction per operator, plus one copy instruction for the assignment to the left-hand-side variable. Temporaries carry the intermediate values.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Unary minus is an operator: <code class=\"mm\">t1 = -c</code> &nbsp;(1)</li><li>The multiplication: <code class=\"mm\">t2 = b * t1</code> &nbsp;(2)</li><li>The addition: <code class=\"mm\">t3 = t2 + d</code> &nbsp;(3)</li><li>The assignment to <code class=\"mm\">a</code>: <code class=\"mm\">a = t3</code> &nbsp;(4)</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 4 instructions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Dropping the unary minus as if <code class=\"mm\">b * -c</code> were a single operator and answering 3. In three-address code every operator, unary or binary, becomes its own instruction.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q12", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about the representations of three-address code are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["In a quadruple the result field names the temporary explicitly.", "In a triple a temporary is referred to by the index of the triple that computes it.", "In indirect triples the list of pointers can be reordered without touching the triples themselves.", "A three-address instruction has at most three addresses, one of which may be the result.", "In static single-assignment form the same variable may be defined more than once in a program."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> All three representations describe the same instructions and differ only in bookkeeping: quadruples name results, triples identify results by position, and indirect triples add a pointer array so that position and program order can differ.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition of the fourth field of a quadruple.</li><li>(B) is the definition of a triple reference such as <code class=\"mm\">(3)</code>.</li><li>(C) is the purpose of the extra array: program order becomes the order of the pointers, not the order of the triples.</li><li>(D) is the instruction format itself: two operands and a result at most.</li><li>(E) is false. Static single-assignment form renames each definition so that every variable is assigned exactly once; later definitions become new names such as <code class=\"mm\">x&#8321;</code> and <code class=\"mm\">x&#8322;</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B), (C) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing SSA with ordinary three-address code. Ordinary code may assign the same variable many times; SSA forbids it precisely so that each name has a single definition site, which is what makes data-flow facts easy to state.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q13", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "a and b are arrays of 4-byte integers with base addresses a0 and b0; i and j are integers and c is an integer. Which sequence correctly translates a[i] = b[j] + c?", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">t1 = j * 4; t2 = b[t1]; t3 = t2 + c; a[i] = t3</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">t1 = b[j]; t2 = t1 + c; a0[i] = t2</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">t1 = j * 4; t2 = b[t1]; t3 = t2 + c; t4 = i * 4; a[t4] = t3</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">t1 = i * 4; a[t1] = b[j] + c</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An array access with element size <code class=\"mm\">w</code> and base <code class=\"mm\">base</code> is translated by computing the byte offset <code class=\"mm\">index * w</code> and addressing <code class=\"mm\">base[offset]</code>. Every indexed access &mdash; on either side of the assignment &mdash; needs its own offset computation, and the right-hand side is evaluated before the left-hand side is stored into.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Right-hand side, inner access: <code class=\"mm\">t1 = j * 4</code> scales the subscript of <code class=\"mm\">b</code>; <code class=\"mm\">t2 = b[t1]</code> fetches the element.</li><li>Right-hand side, addition: <code class=\"mm\">t3 = t2 + c</code>.</li><li>Left-hand side: <code class=\"mm\">t4 = i * 4</code> scales the subscript of <code class=\"mm\">a</code>; then <code class=\"mm\">a[t4] = t3</code> stores the value.</li><li>(A) never scales <code class=\"mm\">i</code>, so it stores into the wrong element. (B) treats <code class=\"mm\">b[j]</code> as a single address and scales neither subscript. (D) folds the addition into one instruction, which is not three-address code, and leaves <code class=\"mm\">b[j]</code> unscaled as well.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that the left-hand side needs no offset computation because &ldquo;the destination is just a place&rdquo;. Both sides index an array, so both need <code class=\"mm\">index * 4</code>, and the store must come after the value has been computed.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q14", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For a = b * c + d * e + f * g, using one instruction per operator, no common subexpression elimination and no reuse of temporaries, how many temporaries does the three-address code need? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "5", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> With no reuse, each computed value occupies a fresh temporary; the final assignment writes into the named variable rather than into a temporary.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">t1 = b * c</code></li><li><code class=\"mm\">t2 = d * e</code></li><li><code class=\"mm\">t3 = t1 + t2</code> &mdash; the left addition, left to right</li><li><code class=\"mm\">t4 = f * g</code></li><li><code class=\"mm\">t5 = t3 + t4</code></li><li><code class=\"mm\">a = t5</code> &mdash; the assignment targets <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, not a temporary</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 5 temporaries (in 6 instructions).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 4 by counting only the four arithmetic results and forgetting that the two additions also need temporaries, or answering 6 by counting <code class=\"mm\">a</code> as a temporary.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q15", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "How does the DAG of an expression differ from its syntax tree?", "options": ["Common subexpressions are shared, so a node may have more than one parent.", "Leaves are shared but interior nodes never are.", "It always has more nodes than the syntax tree.", "It can represent only straight-line code without assignments."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A syntax tree gives every subexpression its own node. A DAG merges nodes that denote the same value, so a shared node has several parents &mdash; which is exactly why it is a directed acyclic graph rather than a tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For <code class=\"mm\">(a + b) * (a + b)</code> the syntax tree contains two separate <code class=\"mm\">+</code> nodes, one for each occurrence.</li><li>The DAG contains one <code class=\"mm\">+</code> node with two parents, the two operands of <code class=\"mm\">*</code>.</li><li>Sharing applies to interior nodes and leaves alike, so (B) is wrong.</li><li>Merging can only reduce the node count, so (C) is wrong, and DAGs represent whole basic blocks including assignments, so (D) is wrong.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the DAG is only a drawing convention. Sharing is the data structure that lets the compiler see that the second occurrence needs no new computation.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q16", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which optimisation is exposed directly by building a DAG for a basic block?", "options": ["loop unrolling", "register allocation", "strength reduction", "common subexpression elimination"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Two occurrences of the same expression map to the same node in the DAG. Every node with more than one parent is therefore a common subexpression, computed once and reused.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Build the DAG for the block, creating one node per distinct value.</li><li>Read the DAG back out: a node with several parents is emitted once, and its later uses refer to the earlier result.</li><li>Dead-code elimination is also available from the DAG &mdash; nodes with no live value are dropped &mdash; but the optimisation the construction exposes directly is common subexpression elimination.</li><li>Loop unrolling, register allocation and strength reduction are loop-level or machine-level transformations; a single basic block contains no loop to unroll.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting the DAG to reorder code. It only merges equal values; the instruction order still has to be recovered from the graph when the block is emitted.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q17", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "For the expression a + a * (b - c) + (b - c) * d, a DAG is built in which two nodes are merged exactly when they have the same operator and the same children, and a leaf is created once per distinct variable. How many nodes does the DAG contain in total, counting leaves and interior nodes once each? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "9", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Build the DAG bottom-up, creating a leaf once per distinct variable and merging any two interior nodes with the same operator and the same children. The two subtractions <code class=\"mm\">b - c</code> are identical, so they share one node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaves: <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, <code class=\"mm\">c</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, <code class=\"mm\">c</code>, <code class=\"mm\">d</code> collapse to the four distinct leaves <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b</code>, <code class=\"mm\">c</code>, <code class=\"mm\">d</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">n1 = b - c</code>, created once and reused for both occurrences.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">n2 = a * n1</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">n3 = a + n2</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">n4 = n1 * d</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">n5 = n3 + n4</code>, the root.</li><li>Interior nodes <code class=\"mm\">n1</code> to <code class=\"mm\">n5</code> = 5; leaves = 4; total = 9.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 9 nodes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Building a syntax tree instead and answering 12 (four leaves plus eight operator occurrences), or merging the two multiplications because they &ldquo;look alike&rdquo; &mdash; they have different children, so they stay distinct.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q18", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In a DAG built for a basic block, an interior node labelled + with two children represents:", "options": ["two variables that are added somewhere in the block.", "the value obtained by applying <code class=\"mm\">+</code> to the values of its children, a node shared by every use of that value.", "an instruction that must be executed twice.", "an operand whose value is not known at compile time."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each node of the DAG denotes a value. A leaf denotes the value of a variable; an interior node denotes the value produced by applying its operator to the values of its children.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The label names the operator, the children name the operands, and the node names the result.</li><li>Because nodes are merged on identical operator and identical children, a node with several parents is a value computed once and used several times.</li><li>Option (C) confuses the value with the instruction: a shared node means the opposite of executing twice.</li><li>Option (D) confuses a node with an unknown: every node in the DAG of a basic block is a value the block computes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating the DAG as a picture of the statements. It is a picture of the <i>values</i> the statements compute, which is why the statement count and the node count differ.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q19", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about DAGs for basic blocks are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A DAG exposes the common subexpressions of a basic block.", "A node whose value is never used can be removed from the DAG, which is dead-code elimination.", "Every syntax tree is a DAG.", "A DAG never has more nodes than the syntax tree of the same expression.", "A DAG for an expression can always be converted back into a unique syntax tree."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The DAG is obtained from the syntax tree by merging equal subexpressions. Merging never creates a node and never changes a value, so the DAG is a quotient of the tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the point of the construction: a node with several parents is a common subexpression.</li><li>(B) is true. A node with no parent outside the DAG and no live variable attached computes a value nobody reads; deleting it, and then its orphaned children, is dead-code elimination.</li><li>(C) is true: a tree is an acyclic directed graph in which every node has at most one parent.</li><li>(D) is true because the DAG is built by merging nodes of the tree; the node count can only fall.</li><li>(E) is false. After merging, the information about which occurrence came first is gone: <code class=\"mm\">(a + b) * (a + b)</code> and a block computing <code class=\"mm\">a + b</code> once and using it twice give the same DAG, and a shared node can be expanded in several different tree shapes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B), (C) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the DAG is a lossless replacement for the syntax tree. It preserves values, not occurrences, so a unique tree cannot be recovered from it.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q20", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Local value numbering finds common subexpressions in a basic block by:", "options": ["sorting the instructions of the block by operator.", "counting how many times each variable is assigned.", "giving the same number to two expressions that have the same operator and the same numbered operands.", "building the loop-nesting structure of the block."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Value numbering walks the block in order, assigning a number to each value. A leaf variable gets a number; an expression gets a number determined by its operator and the numbers of its operands. Two expressions with the same key are the same value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Read the block left to right, keeping a table from keys to numbers.</li><li>For <code class=\"mm\">t1 = b * c</code> the key is <code class=\"mm\">(*, #b, #c)</code>; it is new, so it receives a fresh number.</li><li>When a later instruction has the same key, it receives the same number, so it is a redundant computation and can be replaced by a copy of the earlier result.</li><li>Sorting instructions, counting assignments and analysing loop nesting are different analyses and none of them identifies equal expressions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Comparing expressions as text. Value numbering compares the <i>values</i> of operands, so it also catches <code class=\"mm\">t2 = x * y</code> when <code class=\"mm\">x</code> was previously copied from <code class=\"mm\">b</code> and <code class=\"mm\">y</code> from <code class=\"mm\">c</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q21", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which one of the following is a static semantic check that a compiler performs at compile time?", "options": ["that the operands of <code class=\"mm\">+</code> have compatible types", "that an array subscript is within bounds at run time", "that a division by zero never occurs", "that a pointer is non-null when it is dereferenced"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Static checks are decided from the program text alone, before execution; dynamic checks need the run-time values and are therefore inserted as code or left to the run-time system.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Type compatibility of <code class=\"mm\">+</code> depends only on the declared types of the operands, all of which the compiler knows. It is decidable statically.</li><li>Bounds of a subscript depend on the value of the index at run time; in general this cannot be decided by inspection.</li><li>Division by zero depends on the divisor&rsquo;s run-time value, and deciding it in general is undecidable.</li><li>Whether a pointer is null depends on the execution path, so it is a run-time property.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a compiler checks everything a language forbids. Many forbidden states &mdash; out-of-range subscripts, null dereference &mdash; are caught by run-time checks or not at all.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q22", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In type-expression notation, the type of a function that takes an integer and returns a pointer to a character is:", "options": ["<code class=\"mm\">integer &times; char</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">char &rarr; integer</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">pointer(integer) &rarr; char</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">integer &rarr; pointer(char)</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Type expressions are built with constructors: <code class=\"mm\">array</code>, <code class=\"mm\">pointer</code>, <code class=\"mm\">&times;</code> for a product and <code class=\"mm\">&rarr;</code> for a function. A function type is written <code class=\"mm\">s &rarr; t</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">s</code> is the domain and <code class=\"mm\">t</code> the range, so the arrow points from argument to result.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Domain: one argument of type <code class=\"mm\">integer</code>.</li><li>Range: <code class=\"mm\">pointer(char)</code>, a pointer to a character.</li><li>Compose: <code class=\"mm\">integer &rarr; pointer(char)</code>.</li><li>(A) is a product of two types, not a function. (B) reverses domain and range. (C) puts the pointer on the argument.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading <code class=\"mm\">&rarr;</code> as &ldquo;returns&rdquo; from the wrong end, or treating the arrow as symmetric. It is not: <code class=\"mm\">integer &rarr; char</code> and <code class=\"mm\">char &rarr; integer</code> are different types.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q23", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "Which of the following statements about type checking are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Type checking can be done statically, before the program runs, or dynamically, while it runs.", "A sound type system guarantees that a well-typed program cannot suffer a run-time type error.", "A complete type checker accepts every program that cannot suffer a run-time type error.", "A coercion is an implicit conversion inserted by the compiler to make the types of two operands agree.", "A static type checker rejects every program that could suffer a run-time type error."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Soundness and completeness are the two directions of correctness. Sound: nothing accepted is wrong. Complete: nothing right is rejected. They are independent properties, and a static checker that is both sound and complete cannot exist for a Turing-complete language.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true: a compiler checks types before execution, and a language may also check them at run time, as Lisp and Python do.</li><li>(B) is the definition of soundness.</li><li>(C) is the definition of completeness.</li><li>(D) is the standard meaning of coercion, as when an integer is widened to a real in <code class=\"mm\">i + f</code>.</li><li>(E) confuses soundness with completeness. A sound checker may reject a program that would in fact never fail, so it need not reject exactly the unsafe ones &mdash; and it cannot: deciding whether an arbitrary program raises a type error is undecidable.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B), (C) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using &ldquo;sound&rdquo; and &ldquo;complete&rdquo; interchangeably. A sound checker errs on the safe side by rejecting too much; a complete one errs by accepting everything that is actually safe. Real static checkers are sound but deliberately incomplete.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q24", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "In type-expression notation a two-dimensional array is written array(2, array(3, integer)). How many type constructors — occurrences of array, →, × or pointer — appear in the type expression array(2, array(3, integer → integer))? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A type expression is a tree whose leaves are basic types (<code class=\"mm\">integer</code>, <code class=\"mm\">char</code>, <code class=\"mm\">boolean</code>, <code class=\"mm\">real</code>) and whose interior nodes are type constructors. Counting constructors is counting interior nodes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Outermost: <code class=\"mm\">array(2, &hellip;)</code> &mdash; one constructor.</li><li>Inside it: <code class=\"mm\">array(3, &hellip;)</code> &mdash; second constructor.</li><li>Innermost: <code class=\"mm\">integer &rarr; integer</code> &mdash; third constructor.</li><li>The two occurrences of <code class=\"mm\">integer</code> are basic types, not constructors.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 3 type constructors.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the basic types as well and answering 5, or counting the array bound <code class=\"mm\">2</code> or <code class=\"mm\">3</code> as part of the type. The bounds are attributes of the constructor node, not constructors themselves.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q25", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-28", "text": "A sound static type checker:", "options": ["accepts every program that cannot raise a run-time type error.", "accepts exactly those programs that cannot raise a run-time type error.", "rejects exactly those programs that can raise a run-time type error.", "never accepts a program that can raise a run-time type error, but may reject some programs that cannot."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Soundness is a one-sided guarantee: whatever the checker accepts is safe. Completeness is the other side: whatever is safe is accepted. A sound checker may be conservative and reject safe programs; it may not accept unsafe ones.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) states completeness, not soundness.</li><li>(B) and (C) each state both directions at once, that is soundness <i>and</i> completeness. For a language with arbitrary computation such a checker cannot exist, since deciding whether a program raises a type error reduces to the halting problem.</li><li>(D) states exactly soundness, with the incompleteness made explicit. A typical witness: <code class=\"mm\">if (false) x = 1 / &quot;a&quot;;</code> is rejected by a sound checker although it never executes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting a good type checker to accept everything that would actually run safely. Real checkers are sound but incomplete on purpose: rejecting a safe program is a nuisance, accepting an unsafe one breaks the guarantee the type system exists to give.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q26", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Consider the class of L-attributed definitions. Which one of the following statements is TRUE?", "options": ["Every attribute of every symbol must be a synthesized attribute.", "An inherited attribute of a symbol on the right side may be computed from attributes of the symbols to its left and from the inherited attributes of the head of the production.", "An inherited attribute of a symbol on the right side may be computed from attributes of the symbols to its right.", "Such a definition can be evaluated only by a bottom-up parser."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A definition is L-attributed when every inherited attribute of a right-side symbol depends only on information already available when the parse tree is traversed left to right: the inherited attributes of the head, the attributes of the symbols to its left, and its own inherited attributes. Synthesized attributes may depend on anything at the node or below it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A) describes S-attributed, the special case in which there are no inherited attributes at all; it is not what L-attributed means.</li><li>Option (B) states the L-condition exactly: left siblings and the head are both visited before the current symbol, so their values are ready.</li><li>Option (C) is precisely what L-attributed forbids — a right sibling has not been visited yet when the current node is reached.</li><li>Option (D) is false: the standard use of an L-attributed definition is a single left-to-right depth-first pass, which is exactly what a top-down parser performs.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating L-attributed and S-attributed as opposites. S-attributed is a proper subset of L-attributed; the L-condition says nothing that an all-synthesized definition would violate.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q27", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "An SDD has one production, S → A B, with the rules A.i = S.i, B.i = A.s and S.s = B.s. Which of the following is a valid order for evaluating all six attributes of one node of the parse tree?", "options": ["S.i, A.i, A.s, B.i, B.s, S.s", "S.i, A.i, B.i, A.s, B.s, S.s", "A.s, A.i, S.i, B.i, B.s, S.s", "S.s, S.i, A.i, A.s, B.i, B.s"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A valid evaluation order is any topological order of the dependency graph induced by the semantic rules: a rule can be applied only after every attribute it reads has been computed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A.i</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">S.i</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">S.i</code> must come first.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B.i</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">A.s</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">A.s</code> must precede <code class=\"mm\">B.i</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">S.s</code> reads <code class=\"mm\">B.s</code>, so <code class=\"mm\">S.s</code> must come last.</li><li>Option (A) respects all three constraints: S.i, then A.i, then A.s, then B.i, then B.s, then S.s.</li><li>Option (B) evaluates <code class=\"mm\">B.i</code> before <code class=\"mm\">A.s</code>, which the rule forbids. Option (C) evaluates <code class=\"mm\">A.i</code> before <code class=\"mm\">S.i</code>. Option (D) evaluates <code class=\"mm\">S.s</code> first.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the rules as if they were assignment statements executed in the order written. Only the data dependencies matter, and any order consistent with them is valid.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q28", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following claims about the relationship between S-attributed and L-attributed definitions hold? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every S-attributed definition is also L-attributed.", "Every L-attributed definition is also S-attributed.", "An L-attributed definition can be evaluated in a single left-to-right, depth-first pass over the parse tree.", "An S-attributed definition needs a separate stack for inherited attributes during bottom-up evaluation.", "A definition in which one attribute of a node is computed from another attribute of the same node cannot be L-attributed."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The L-condition restricts only inherited attributes of right-side symbols. A definition with no inherited attributes satisfies it vacuously.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true. S-attributed means all attributes are synthesized; the L-condition is then never triggered, so the definition is L-attributed as well.</li><li>(B) is false. <code class=\"mm\">L.type = L.inh</code> in the standard list grammar uses an inherited attribute, so that definition is L-attributed but not S-attributed.</li><li>(C) is true. That single pass is the defining motivation for the class: values flow down the left spine and up on return.</li><li>(D) is false. An S-attributed definition defines no inherited attributes at all, so no stack for them is needed; values travel on the parser's own value stack.</li><li>(E) is false. Rules such as <code class=\"mm\">L.type = L.inh</code> and <code class=\"mm\">A.s = A.i + 1</code> combine two attributes of the same node and are perfectly legal in an L-attributed definition.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A) and (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the two classes coincide. The proper inclusion S-attributed ⊂ L-attributed is the single most examined fact about them.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q29", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "An SDD contains the productions S → A B with rules A.i = 1, B.i = A.s and S.s = B.s; A → id with rule A.s = A.i + 1; and B → id with rule B.s = B.i × 2. What is the value of S.s at the root? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Evaluate the attributes in dependency order: inherited attributes flow down from the root, synthesized attributes flow up from the leaves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">A.i = 1</code> is set by the rule on <code class=\"mm\">S → A B</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">A.s = A.i + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B.i = A.s = 2</code> — a left-sibling dependency, which is what makes this definition L-attributed rather than S-attributed.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">B.s = B.i × 2 = 4</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">S.s = B.s = 4</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 4.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Substituting <code class=\"mm\">A.s</code> as 1 by ignoring the <code class=\"mm\">+1</code> rule, which gives 2 at the root instead of 4.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q30", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "During bottom-up (LR) parsing of an S-attributed definition, where are synthesized attribute values kept?", "options": ["In a separate attribute stack indexed by parse-tree node, allocated when each node is created.", "On the parser's value stack beside each grammar symbol, so that a reduction finds the values of the right-side symbols at the top of the stack.", "In a hash table keyed by parser state, filled in when each terminal is shifted.", "Nowhere — a synthesized attribute cannot be computed during bottom-up parsing, so a separate pass over the parse tree is required."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Bottom-up parsing reduces a handle after all of its right-side symbols have been shifted or reduced, so their synthesized values are already on top of the stack when the reduction fires.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A reduction pops <code class=\"mm\">k</code> states and symbols; the corresponding <code class=\"mm\">k</code> attribute values are the top <code class=\"mm\">k</code> entries of the value stack.</li><li>The semantic action computes the head's attribute from those values and pushes the single result together with the new state.</li><li>No node-indexed structure is needed, because a node's value is consumed exactly once, at the reduction that creates it.</li><li>Option (D) is the misconception that semantic analysis must follow parsing; the standard construction is a parser with an added value stack.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing an S-attributed definition is evaluated after parsing. It is evaluated during parsing, in lock-step with the reductions.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q31", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "s-attributed-vs-l-attributed-definitions", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following attributes must be an inherited attribute?", "options": ["The value of an expression node, computed from the values of its children.", "The data type of an identifier, passed down from its declaration to a use of that identifier.", "The number of nodes in the subtree rooted at a node.", "The lexical value of an identifier, returned by the scanner."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A synthesized attribute is computed from the children of a node; an inherited attribute is computed from the parent, the siblings or the node itself.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Option (A) reads only the children, so it is synthesized.</li><li>Option (B) flows from the enclosing declaration downward to a use — information that is not available from the use itself, so it must be inherited.</li><li>Option (C) reads only the subtree, so it is synthesized.</li><li>Option (D) is supplied by the lexer at the leaf, so it is synthesized (a lexical attribute).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Deciding the direction from where the information \"lives\" in the program text rather than from the direction in which it travels in the tree.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q32", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "In the triple representation of three-address code, how does a later instruction refer to a value produced by an earlier instruction?", "options": ["By a named temporary variable.", "By the position number of the triple that produced it.", "By the address of the result operand.", "By a register number assigned during code generation."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A triple stores only three fields — operator, operand, operand — and there is no result field. The result of a triple is the triple itself, identified by its index.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The triples for <code class=\"mm\">a = b * c + d</code> are (0) <code class=\"mm\">* b c</code>, (1) <code class=\"mm\">+ (0) d</code>, (2) <code class=\"mm\">= a (1)</code>.</li><li>Triple 1 names triple 0 by the parenthesised index (0); no temporary name is introduced.</li><li>Option (A) describes quadruples, which do carry a named result. Options (C) and (D) belong to later phases.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting a result field in a triple. The absence of that field is exactly why triples are compact and why moving a triple renumbers its uses.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q33", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which sequence correctly translates the short-circuit test if (x < y && y < z) goto L?", "options": ["if x &lt; y goto L1; goto L2; L1: if y &lt; z goto L; L2:", "if x &lt; y goto L; if y &lt; z goto L", "if x &lt; y goto L; goto L", "if y &lt; z goto L; if x &lt; y goto L"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Short-circuit <code class=\"mm\">&amp;&amp;</code> evaluates the second operand only when the first is true. Control flow therefore falls through past the whole test as soon as one conjunct fails.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If <code class=\"mm\">x &lt; y</code> holds, the decision depends on <code class=\"mm\">y &lt; z</code>, so control goes to the second test at <code class=\"mm\">L1</code>.</li><li>If <code class=\"mm\">x &lt; y</code> fails, the conjunction is false and <code class=\"mm\">y &lt; z</code> must not be evaluated; control jumps past the test to <code class=\"mm\">L2</code>.</li><li>At <code class=\"mm\">L1</code> the truth of <code class=\"mm\">y &lt; z</code> sends control to <code class=\"mm\">L</code>; failure simply falls through to <code class=\"mm\">L2</code>.</li><li>Option (B) evaluates <code class=\"mm\">y &lt; z</code> even when <code class=\"mm\">x &lt; y</code> is false, which is the non-short-circuit semantics.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Writing the two tests back to back with the same target. That implements <code class=\"mm\">or</code>-style fall-through, not <code class=\"mm\">and</code>.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q34", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "In extended three-address code (the form that also allows indexed addresses), which of the following are valid instructions? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["x = y + z", "x = y * z + w", "x = y[z]", "goto L", "x = y = z"], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Three-address code allows at most one operator on the right of an assignment, together with an assignment or a jump. Each instruction therefore has at most three addresses.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the canonical form: one operator, three addresses, valid.</li><li>(B) has two operators and needs a temporary for the product, so it is not a three-address instruction.</li><li>(C) is an indexed copy, a standard extension used for arrays; it has three addresses and is valid.</li><li>(D) is a jump, which is one of the permitted instruction forms.</li><li>(E) is a double assignment, which requires two separate instructions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (C) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Rejecting the indexed copy because it \"looks like an array expression\". The restriction is on the number of operators, not on the shape of an address.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q35", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "In the quadruple form (op, arg1, arg2, result) a unary operation leaves arg2 empty: t1 = -b becomes (uminus, b, -, t1). For the code t1 = -b followed by t2 = a * t1, how many quadruples have an empty arg2 field? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A quadruple always has four fields; a field that an instruction does not use is filled with a placeholder. Unary operators use only <code class=\"mm\">arg1</code>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Quadruple 1 is <code class=\"mm\">(uminus, b, -, t1)</code>: <code class=\"mm\">arg2</code> is empty.</li><li>Quadruple 2 is <code class=\"mm\">(*, a, t1, t2)</code>: both operand fields are used.</li><li>Exactly one quadruple in the sequence has an empty <code class=\"mm\">arg2</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting a third operand for a unary minus, or counting the placeholder in the result field of a jump as an empty <code class=\"mm\">arg2</code> when no jump is present.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q36", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "For a = b * c + d * e, using one instruction per operator and one for the final assignment, what is the minimum number of distinct temporaries required if a temporary may be reused as soon as its value is no longer needed?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The number of temporaries needed at any point is the maximum number of values simultaneously live; temporaries can be recycled after their last use.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">t1 = b * c</code> — one temporary live.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">t2 = d * e</code> — two live, since <code class=\"mm\">t1</code> is still needed.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">t1 = t1 + t2</code> — <code class=\"mm\">t2</code> dies here and its slot is free, but only two were ever live at once.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">a = t1</code> — the final assignment consumes the last temporary.</li><li>The peak liveness is 2, so two temporaries suffice; one is impossible because both products must exist before they are added.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting temporaries by counting operators. Four operators do not imply four temporaries; only simultaneous liveness matters.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q37", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "An array is declared int a[1..10][1..20], each element occupying 4 bytes, stored in row-major order, with base address 1000. In the three-address code for a[i][j], what byte offset is added to the base address when i = 4 and j = 7? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "264", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> In row-major order the offset of <code class=\"mm\">a[i][j]</code> in an array declared <code class=\"mm\">a[lo₁..hi₁][lo₂..hi₂]</code> is <code class=\"mm\">((i − lo₁)·n₂ + (j − lo₂))·w</code>, where <code class=\"mm\">n₂ = hi₂ − lo₂ + 1</code> is the number of columns and <code class=\"mm\">w</code> is the element width.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Columns per row: <code class=\"mm\">n₂ = 20 − 1 + 1 = 20</code>.</li><li>Rows skipped: <code class=\"mm\">i − lo₁ = 4 − 1 = 3</code>, contributing <code class=\"mm\">3 × 20 = 60</code> elements.</li><li>Columns skipped within the row: <code class=\"mm\">j − lo₂ = 7 − 1 = 6</code>.</li><li>Total elements before <code class=\"mm\">a[4][7]</code>: <code class=\"mm\">60 + 6 = 66</code>.</li><li>Byte offset: <code class=\"mm\">66 × 4 = 264</code>, so the address is <code class=\"mm\">1000 + 264 = 1264</code>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 264 (the offset; the full address is 1264).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using <code class=\"mm\">i·n₂ + j</code> and answering <code class=\"mm\">(80 + 7) × 4 = 348</code>. The lower bounds are 1, not 0, and the question asks for the offset from the base, not the absolute address.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q38", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "three-address-code-quadruples-triples", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Consider while (a < b) do S, translated with the loop test at the top, a jump that leaves the loop when the test fails, and a jump at the end of the body back to the test. How many jump instructions appear in the generated three-address code?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A while loop has one conditional exit jump at the test and one unconditional jump back from the end of the body; the exit path needs a jump over the body only when the body is placed before the test.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">L1: if a &lt; b goto L2</code> — the conditional jump that enters the body.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">goto L3</code> — leaves the loop when the test fails.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">L2:</code> code for <code class=\"mm\">S</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">goto L1</code> — the unconditional jump back to the test.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">L3:</code> the statement after the loop. That is three jumps in total.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C) — 3.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2 by counting only the test and the back edge. The exit jump over the body is a separate instruction in this scheme, and a compiler that inverts the test (<code class=\"mm\">if a ≥ b goto L3</code>) would emit only two — which is why the stem fixes the scheme.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q39", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "When a parse tree is condensed into a syntax tree, which one of the following is the result?", "options": ["Every grammar symbol is kept as an interior node, including those introduced by single-production chains.", "Nodes introduced by single-production chains and the parentheses are dropped, so operators become interior nodes and operands become leaves.", "Every operator node is duplicated, so the syntax tree has strictly more nodes than the parse tree.", "Only the leaves survive, and operator precedence is recovered from the order of the tokens."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A syntax tree is a condensed parse tree: nodes whose productions have a single symbol on the right, and punctuation such as parentheses, are removed because they carry no value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>For <code class=\"mm\">id + id * id</code> the parse tree contains chains such as <code class=\"mm\">E → T → F → id</code>.</li><li>The syntax tree collapses each such chain to one node per operator, with the operands as leaves.</li><li>Precedence is preserved because the tree shape is unchanged: the multiplication subtree is still a child of the addition.</li><li>Hence the syntax tree never has more nodes than the parse tree, and it does represent precedence.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the syntax tree loses information. It loses only nodes that carry no operator and no operand.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q40", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A DAG is built for the expression ((a + b) × (a + b)) − (a + b), merging two nodes exactly when they carry the same operator and the same children, and creating one leaf per distinct variable. How many nodes does the DAG contain in total?", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "7"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DAG shares every subexpression that denotes the same value, so a repeated subexpression is stored once and reached by several edges.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaves: <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">b</code> — 2 nodes.</li><li>Interior node <code class=\"mm\">n1 = +(a, b)</code>, built once and shared by all three occurrences.</li><li>Interior node <code class=\"mm\">n2 = ×(n1, n1)</code>.</li><li>Interior node <code class=\"mm\">n3 = −(n2, n1)</code>.</li><li>Total: 2 leaves + 3 interior nodes = 5.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — 5.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting three separate <code class=\"mm\">+</code> nodes and answering 7. Sharing is the whole point of the DAG.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q41", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "For the expression a × b + a × b, identical operator nodes are shared and each distinct variable contributes one leaf. How many nodes does the resulting DAG contain in total? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Identical subexpressions collapse to one node in a DAG, which is how common subexpressions become visible.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaves <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">b</code>: 2 nodes.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">n1 = ×(a, b)</code>: the two products are the same node.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">n2 = +(n1, n1)</code>: 1 node.</li><li>Total 2 + 1 + 1 = 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 4.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 6 by keeping both product nodes, which is the syntax tree, not the DAG.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q42", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A DAG for a basic block is built statement by statement. Which of the following statements about that construction are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Two interior nodes are merged exactly when they carry the same operator and the same ordered list of children.", "A leaf labelled with a variable may have more than one parent.", "Every interior node of the DAG has exactly one parent.", "The DAG can be built incrementally, one statement at a time, reusing an existing node whenever an identical one is already present.", "Building the DAG requires the whole basic block to be parsed into a syntax tree before any node is created."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The DAG is built by a value-numbering style pass: each variable and each operator application is given a number, and a repeated number means the node already exists.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is true and is the merge rule; the order of children matters, since subtraction and division are not commutative.</li><li>(B) is true — a variable used in several statements has one leaf with several parents, which is exactly the sharing the DAG records.</li><li>(C) is false. An interior node may also have several parents, for example the shared <code class=\"mm\">+(a, b)</code> in <code class=\"mm\">((a + b) × (a + b))</code>.</li><li>(D) is true; that single incremental pass is the standard construction and is equivalent to local value numbering.</li><li>(E) is false. No separate syntax tree is needed; nodes are created as each statement is processed.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a DAG is still a tree apart from its leaves. Any node, leaf or interior, may have several parents.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q43", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which statement relating the syntax tree and the DAG of the same expression is TRUE?", "options": ["The DAG always has strictly fewer nodes than the syntax tree.", "The DAG never has more nodes than the syntax tree, and has fewer exactly when some subexpression occurs more than once.", "The DAG and the syntax tree always have the same number of nodes.", "The DAG may have more nodes, because it records sharing with extra nodes."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A DAG is obtained from a syntax tree by merging nodes that denote the same value, so it is at most as large as the tree.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Every node of the DAG corresponds to at least one node of the syntax tree, so the DAG has no more nodes.</li><li>If no subexpression repeats, nothing can be merged and the two have the same size — which rules out option (A).</li><li>Sharing is recorded by extra <i>edges</i>, never by extra nodes, which rules out option (D).</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking a DAG needs a new node to point at a shared value. A second edge from the existing node is enough.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q44", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "syntax-trees-dags", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A DAG is built for the basic block d = a + b; e = a + b; f = d + e. How many interior nodes does the DAG contain?", "options": ["1", "2", "3", "4"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each statement adds at most one interior node, and an identical node is reused rather than created.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">d = a + b</code> creates <code class=\"mm\">n1 = +(a, b)</code>; <code class=\"mm\">d</code> is attached to <code class=\"mm\">n1</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">e = a + b</code> finds the identical node <code class=\"mm\">n1</code> already present, so no node is created; <code class=\"mm\">e</code> is attached to the same <code class=\"mm\">n1</code>.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">f = d + e</code> becomes <code class=\"mm\">+(n1, n1)</code>, a new node <code class=\"mm\">n2</code>, because its children are node numbers, not the operands <code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">b</code>.</li><li>Interior nodes: <code class=\"mm\">n1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">n2</code> — two. Adding the three leaves <code class=\"mm\">a</code>, <code class=\"mm\">b</code> and the variable labels gives the full node count, but only two are interior.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — 2.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Merging <code class=\"mm\">f</code>'s node with <code class=\"mm\">n1</code> because both are additions over the same values. Merging compares the child <i>nodes</i>; <code class=\"mm\">+(a, b)</code> and <code class=\"mm\">+(n1, n1)</code> are different nodes even though they compute the same value.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q45", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following checks must be performed at run time rather than at compile time?", "options": ["That both operands of + have compatible types.", "That every variable is declared before it is used.", "That an array subscript lies within the declared bounds.", "That a function is called with the number of arguments given in its declaration."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A static check is decidable from the program text alone; a dynamic check depends on values that exist only while the program runs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Operand types are known from the declarations, so compatibility is a static check.</li><li>Declaration before use is a scope rule enforced on the symbol table, again static.</li><li>A subscript is usually a computed value; whether it lies within bounds is not known until execution, so the check is dynamic — unless the language forces static bounds.</li><li>Arity is fixed by the declaration and is static.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling every semantic check static. Type checking is static only when types are declared or inferable before execution.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q46", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "In type-expression notation, the type of an array of ten pointers to integers is:", "options": ["array(10, pointer(integer))", "pointer(array(10, integer))", "array(10, integer) → pointer(integer)", "pointer(integer) × 10"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A type expression is built with constructors: <code class=\"mm\">array(n, T)</code> for <code class=\"mm\">n</code> consecutive objects of type <code class=\"mm\">T</code>, <code class=\"mm\">pointer(T)</code> for a pointer to <code class=\"mm\">T</code>, <code class=\"mm\">T₁ × T₂</code> for a product and <code class=\"mm\">T₁ → T₂</code> for a function.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The outermost object is the array of ten elements, so <code class=\"mm\">array(10, ·)</code> is the outer constructor.</li><li>Each element is a pointer to an integer, so the inner type is <code class=\"mm\">pointer(integer)</code>.</li><li>Composing gives <code class=\"mm\">array(10, pointer(integer))</code>.</li><li>Option (B) is the type of a single pointer to an array of ten integers — the constructors are swapped.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading \"array of pointers\" and \"pointer to array\" as the same type. Their type expressions differ in the order of the constructors, and so does their size.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q47", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Soundness, completeness and coercion decide what a type checker can promise. Which of the following statements are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["A sound type checker rejects every ill-typed program.", "A complete type checker accepts every well-typed program.", "A type checker that is sound is necessarily also complete.", "A coercion is an implicit conversion inserted so that an operation which would otherwise be ill-typed becomes well-typed.", "A language with no static types must still have its type errors detected at compile time."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Soundness means \"accepts only well-typed programs\"; completeness means \"accepts all well-typed programs\". They are independent properties, and real checkers usually trade completeness for decidability.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is the definition of soundness.</li><li>(B) is the definition of completeness.</li><li>(C) is false. A checker that rejects every program is sound but hopelessly incomplete.</li><li>(D) is true; a coercion such as widening an integer to a real keeps the program well-typed without an explicit cast.</li><li>(E) is false. Without static types, type errors can only be detected dynamically, at run time.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using \"sound\" and \"complete\" interchangeably. A compiler that accepts everything is complete but unsound; one that rejects everything is sound but incomplete.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q48", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "In type-expression notation, how many type constructors — occurrences of array, pointer, × or → — appear in the type of a function that takes an integer and returns a pointer to a function from an integer to an integer, written integer → pointer(integer → integer)? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A type constructor is a symbol that builds a type from other types; a basic type such as <code class=\"mm\">integer</code> is not a constructor.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The outermost <code class=\"mm\">→</code> makes the whole thing a function type: 1.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">pointer(·)</code> applied to the result type: 2.</li><li>The inner <code class=\"mm\">→</code> inside the pointer, for the function from integer to integer: 3.</li><li>The three occurrences of <code class=\"mm\">integer</code> are basic types, not constructors, so they are not counted.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 3.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the basic types as well and answering 6, or missing the inner arrow because it sits inside a pointer.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q49", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Resolving an overloaded operator or function name to a particular definition is an example of:", "options": ["lexical analysis.", "syntax analysis.", "static semantic analysis.", "machine-independent code optimization."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Overload resolution needs the types of the operands, which are recorded in the symbol table during semantic analysis; it is therefore not a syntactic decision.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The scanner returns the same token for <code class=\"mm\">+</code> whatever its operand types, so this is not lexical.</li><li>The parser accepts the same phrase structure for both interpretations, so this is not syntactic either.</li><li>Semantic analysis looks up the declared types and picks the definition, then annotates the tree with it.</li><li>Optimization works on the already-typed intermediate code.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating overloading as a parsing problem because it looks like an ambiguity. It is resolved by type information, not by grammar.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U4-Q50", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 4, "unit_slug": "sdt-intermediate-code", "subtopic": "type-checking", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following are checks a compiler can make at compile time? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Every variable is declared before it is used.", "No division by zero is ever performed.", "The type of the left side of an assignment is compatible with the type of the right side.", "A function is called with the number of arguments its declaration specifies.", "An array subscript is always within the declared bounds."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (C) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A static check must be decidable from the source text; anything that depends on run-time values is a dynamic check.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) is a scope rule, decided from the symbol table.</li><li>(B) depends on values of divisors, so it is undecidable in general and, at best, approximated by data-flow analysis.</li><li>(C) is ordinary type checking over declared types.</li><li>(D) is fixed by the declaration, so arity is checked statically.</li><li>(E) depends on the computed subscript; it is a run-time check unless the language restricts subscripts to constants.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (C) and (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming every semantic error can be caught statically. Division by zero and out-of-bounds subscripts are the standard examples that cannot.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q01", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following is not normally stored in the activation record of a procedure?", "options": ["The actual parameters passed to the procedure.", "The procedure's local variables and compiler-generated temporaries.", "The return address and the saved machine state of the caller.", "The symbol table of the entire program."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An activation record holds exactly the information needed for one activation of one procedure: parameters, links, saved state, locals and temporaries.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The activation record is created when the procedure is entered and destroyed when it returns.</li><li>It must hold the incoming parameters, the links to other records, the saved registers and return address, the locals and the temporaries.</li><li>The symbol table belongs to the compilation, not to a run-time activation; it is consulted by the compiler and is not part of any single frame.</li><li>Hence the whole-program symbol table is the item that is not stored per activation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing the compile-time symbol table with the run-time activation record. Only the entries the compiler decides are needed at run time end up in the frame.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q02", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "In a language that allows nested procedures, the access link (static link) of the activation record of procedure P points to", "options": ["the activation record of whoever called P", "the activation record of the most recent activation of the procedure that lexically encloses P", "the activation record of the main program, always", "the previous activation record of P itself"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The access link implements lexical (static) scoping: it chains a record to the record of its enclosing procedure, not to its caller.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A non-local name inside <code class=\"mm\">P</code> refers to a declaration in the procedure that textually encloses <code class=\"mm\">P</code>.</li><li>That declaration lives in the most recent activation of the enclosing procedure, which is still on the stack.</li><li>The access link therefore points at that record, and a chain of access links reaches every lexically enclosing scope.</li><li>The caller's record is reached by the control link, which is a different link with a different purpose.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading the access link as the caller link. The caller link is the control (dynamic) link; the two coincide only when the call is to a procedure nested directly in the caller.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q03", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about activation records and the run-time stack are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["The control link (dynamic link) of an activation record points to the activation record of the caller.", "The access link and the control link always point to the same record.", "When a procedure returns, its activation record is popped and the caller's record becomes current again.", "A procedure that is never called recursively does not need an activation record at all."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The control link implements the dynamic call chain; the access link implements lexical nesting; the stack discipline follows the LIFO order of calls.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The control link is defined as the pointer to the caller's record, so option (A) is true.</li><li>The access link points to the lexically enclosing procedure's record, which is generally not the caller, so option (B) is false.</li><li>Calls nest, so records are pushed and popped in LIFO order; on return the caller's record is current again. Option (C) is true.</li><li>Every call — recursive or not — needs a frame to hold parameters, saved state and locals. Option (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A) and (C)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a non-recursive procedure can keep its locals in static storage. Only when the compiler can prove a single live activation, and the language forbids recursion or re-entrancy, is that possible.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q04", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A procedure's activation record holds 8 bytes for the return address, 16 bytes of incoming parameters, 40 bytes of local scalars, 24 bytes of saved registers and 8 bytes of compiler temporaries. How many bytes does one activation record occupy? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "96", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The frame size is the sum of every field the compiler places in the activation record.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Return address: 8 bytes.</li><li>Incoming parameters: 16 bytes.</li><li>Local scalars: 40 bytes.</li><li>Saved registers: 24 bytes.</li><li>Temporaries: 8 bytes.</li><li>8 + 16 + 40 + 24 + 8 = 96 bytes.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 96 bytes</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the saved-register area, or leaving out the temporaries because they do not appear in the source. Both occupy real frame space.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q05", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Each activation record of a recursive procedure is 96 bytes and the run-time system reserves a further 64 bytes of fixed overhead at the base of the stack. If the deepest recursion reached during a run is 12, what is the total stack space used?", "options": ["1152 bytes", "1216 bytes", "1280 bytes", "1088 bytes"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Stack usage for a recursion depth d is d × frame size plus any fixed base overhead.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Twelve simultaneous activations each occupy 96 bytes: 12 × 96 = 1152 bytes.</li><li>The fixed 64-byte base overhead is charged once, not per activation.</li><li>Total = 1152 + 64 = 1216 bytes.</li><li>1216 is the only option that adds the overhead exactly once.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) — 1216 bytes</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Charging the 64-byte overhead on every frame (giving 12 × 160 = 1920) or forgetting it entirely (1152).</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q06", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Why can two simultaneous activations of the same recursive procedure each keep a private copy of a local variable?", "options": ["Because each activation has its own activation record on the run-time stack.", "Because local variables are always allocated in static memory.", "Because the compiler gives each call site a different name for the variable.", "Because local variables are always kept in registers."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Recursion works because each activation gets a fresh frame, so a local name maps to a different storage location in each activation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A local variable is an offset within the current activation record.</li><li>Each call pushes a new record, so the same offset denotes different storage in different activations.</li><li>Static allocation would give one location for all activations and destroy the older value on each recursive call.</li><li>Registers cannot hold every local of every activation, and the compiler cannot rename a variable at run time.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking the compiler 'renames' variables per call. Renaming is a compile-time idea; what actually separates the copies is the frame pointer.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q07", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A basic block is", "options": ["a maximal sequence of consecutive three-address instructions in which control enters only at the first instruction and leaves only at the last", "any sequence of instructions that contains no jump instruction", "the body of a loop, together with its test", "a single three-address instruction"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A basic block is a maximal straight-line run: one entry point at the top, one exit at the bottom.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Control must not be able to enter the middle of the sequence, so no instruction except the first may be a jump target.</li><li>Control must not be able to leave early, so no instruction except the last may be a jump.</li><li>The sequence must be maximal: extending it in either direction would break one of those two conditions.</li><li>A block may therefore contain many instructions, and it may well contain a jump as its final instruction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Defining a basic block as 'a sequence with no jump'. A block may end with a jump; what it may not have is a jump anywhere but at the end, or a label anywhere but at the start.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q08", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "When three-address code is partitioned into basic blocks, which of the following is not a leader?", "options": ["The first instruction of the code.", "Any instruction that is the target of a conditional or unconditional jump.", "Any instruction that immediately follows a conditional or unconditional jump.", "Any instruction that uses a variable defined earlier in the same block."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Leaders are found by three structural rules only: first instruction, jump targets, and instructions following a jump.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The first instruction is a leader by definition, since control enters the procedure there.</li><li>A jump target may receive control from elsewhere, so it must start a block.</li><li>The instruction after a jump may be reached by fall-through only, and the jump ends the previous block, so it starts a new one.</li><li>Using a variable defined earlier in the same block is ordinary data dependence; it does not let control enter from outside, so it does not create a leader.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing data dependences with control-flow boundaries. Only jumps and jump targets split blocks.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q09", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Consider the three-address code below, where L1, L2 and L3 are labels. i = 1 if i > n goto L3 L1: t = 4 * i a[t] = 0 i = i + 1 if i <= n goto L1 L3: x = 0 goto L5 L5: y = x + 1 How many basic blocks does this code contain? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "4", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Apply the three leader rules, then each block runs from one leader up to the instruction before the next.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaders: the first instruction <code class=\"mm\">i = 1</code>; the target <code class=\"mm\">L3</code>; the target <code class=\"mm\">L1</code>; the instruction after <code class=\"mm\">goto L5</code>, namely <code class=\"mm\">y = x + 1</code> at <code class=\"mm\">L5</code>.</li><li>Block 1: <code class=\"mm\">i = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">if i &gt; n goto L3</code>.</li><li>Block 2 (L1): <code class=\"mm\">t = 4 * i</code>, <code class=\"mm\">a[t] = 0</code>, <code class=\"mm\">i = i + 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">if i &lt;= n goto L1</code>.</li><li>Block 3 (L3): <code class=\"mm\">x = 0</code>, <code class=\"mm\">goto L5</code>.</li><li>Block 4 (L5): <code class=\"mm\">y = x + 1</code>. Total 4.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 4 basic blocks</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Missing the block that begins after <code class=\"mm\">goto L5</code>. Any instruction following a jump is a leader even when nothing jumps to it.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q10", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about the flow graph of a procedure are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Each node of the flow graph is a basic block.", "There is an edge from block B1 to block B2 if control can pass from the last instruction of B1 to the first instruction of B2.", "The initial node is the block that contains the first instruction of the procedure.", "Every block in a flow graph has exactly one predecessor."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The flow graph records the possible transfers of control between basic blocks.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Nodes are basic blocks, by construction. Option (A) is true.</li><li>An edge exists exactly when a jump or a fall-through can carry control from one block to the other. Option (B) is true.</li><li>Execution begins at the procedure's first instruction, which lies in the initial node. Option (C) is true.</li><li>A block that is the target of several jumps has several predecessors, and the initial node has none. Option (D) is false.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (C)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a single predecessor because the source reads top to bottom. Any block with two incoming jumps breaks that assumption.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q11", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "An edge (B2 → B1) in a flow graph whose head B1 dominates its tail B2 is called a back edge. What does a back edge identify?", "options": ["A basic block that is never executed.", "A natural loop, with B1 as its header.", "A procedure call that has not been inlined.", "A block that has two or more predecessors."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Back edges are the structural signature of loops: a back edge plus the nodes that can reach its tail without passing through its head form a natural loop.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>B1 dominates B2 means every path from the initial node to B2 passes through B1.</li><li>The edge B2 → B1 therefore closes a cycle, and the set of nodes that can reach B2 without going through B1, together with B1, is the natural loop.</li><li>B1 is called the header: it dominates every node of the loop and is the only entry point.</li><li>Irreducible flow can contain cycles without a back edge in this sense, which is why the definition is stated in terms of dominance.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating every cycle as a natural loop. Only cycles with a single entry — equivalently, closed by a back edge — are natural loops.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q12", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Consider the three-address code below, where L1, L3 and L4 are labels. x = 1 y = 2 if x < y goto L1 x = x + 1 goto L3 L1: y = y * 2 L3: if y > 10 goto L4 z = y L4: w = z + 1 How many edges does the flow graph of this code contain? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Partition into basic blocks with the leader rules, then count one edge per possible transfer of control.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leaders: <code class=\"mm\">x = 1</code>; <code class=\"mm\">L1</code>; <code class=\"mm\">x = x + 1</code> (follows the conditional jump); <code class=\"mm\">L3</code>; <code class=\"mm\">z = y</code> (follows the conditional jump at L3); <code class=\"mm\">L4</code>.</li><li>Blocks: B1 = [<code class=\"mm\">x = 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">y = 2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">if x &lt; y goto L1</code>]; B2 = [<code class=\"mm\">x = x + 1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">goto L3</code>]; B3 = [<code class=\"mm\">y = y * 2</code>] (L1); B4 = [<code class=\"mm\">if y &gt; 10 goto L4</code>] (L3); B5 = [<code class=\"mm\">z = y</code>]; B6 = [<code class=\"mm\">w = z + 1</code>] (L4).</li><li>Edges: B1→B3 (jump taken), B1→B2 (fall-through), B2→B4 (goto L3), B3→B4 (fall-through), B4→B6 (jump taken), B4→B5 (fall-through), B5→B6 (fall-through).</li><li>B6 has no successor. Total 7 edges.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 7 edges</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Forgetting the fall-through edge out of a conditional jump, or merging <code class=\"mm\">z = y</code> into B4. An instruction after a jump always starts a new block.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q13", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "In the flow graph of a while loop, which node dominates every node of the loop body?", "options": ["The node that contains the loop test — the loop header.", "The node that contains the exit from the loop.", "The node with the largest number of successors.", "Every node of the body dominates the header, not the other way round."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The header of a natural loop dominates every node in the loop, because control can reach the body only through the test.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Control reaches the loop body only after passing the loop test, so every path to a body node goes through the header.</li><li>That is exactly the definition of domination, so the header dominates every body node.</li><li>The exit node is reached from inside the loop, so it cannot dominate the body.</li><li>The reverse claim fails: a body node can be bypassed by leaving the loop early.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing the exit block because it is 'after' the loop. Domination is about all paths from entry, and the exit is not on the path into the body.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q14", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Detecting that t1 = b + c and t2 = b + c inside one basic block compute the same value, and rewriting the second to use t1, is called", "options": ["common subexpression elimination", "dead code elimination", "loop unrolling", "constant folding"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Local common subexpression elimination reuses a value already computed earlier in the same block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The two right-hand sides are identical and neither operand has been redefined in between.</li><li>The second computation is therefore redundant and can be replaced by a copy from the first result.</li><li>This is common subexpression elimination, performed locally because both occurrences lie in one block.</li><li>The other three transformations remove unused code, replicate a loop body, or evaluate constants — none applies here.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling it copy propagation. Copy propagation moves an assignment <code class=\"mm\">x = y</code> into later uses; here a whole recomputation is removed.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q15", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "An instruction whose result is never used and which has no side effect is removed by", "options": ["dead code elimination", "strength reduction", "code motion", "induction variable elimination"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Dead code elimination deletes computations whose results cannot affect the program's output.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A definition is dead when the variable it defines is not live after it.</li><li>With no side effect, removing it cannot change any observable behaviour.</li><li>That is precisely dead code elimination.</li><li>Strength reduction, code motion and induction variable elimination all keep the computation but change its form or position.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Deleting a call or a store because 'the result is unused'. Calls and stores have side effects, so they are not dead merely because a value is discarded.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q16", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "After constant propagation and constant folding, what integer value is assigned by x = 3 * (4 + 5) - 2 * 6? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "15", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Constant folding evaluates at compile time any expression whose operands are all constants.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Fold the parenthesised sum: 4 + 5 = 9.</li><li>Fold the first product: 3 × 9 = 27.</li><li>Fold the second product: 2 × 6 = 12.</li><li>Subtract: 27 − 12 = 15.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 15</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Applying the multiplication before the parentheses (giving 3×4+5 = 17). Parentheses bind first; only then do × and ÷.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q17", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which one of the following transformations replaces an expensive operation inside a loop by a cheaper equivalent one?", "options": ["constant folding", "strength reduction", "unreachable code elimination", "common subexpression elimination"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Strength reduction substitutes a cheaper operation — typically addition for multiplication, or a shift for a power-of-two multiply — while preserving the value.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Constant folding evaluates expressions whose operands are known at compile time; nothing expensive is being replaced at run time.</li><li>Strength reduction keeps the same value but computes it with a cheaper operation, for example maintaining <code class=\"mm\">t = t + 8</code> instead of recomputing <code class=\"mm\">8 * i</code> each iteration.</li><li>Unreachable code elimination removes code that can never execute; it is not a replacement.</li><li>Common subexpression elimination removes a redundant recomputation rather than making an operation cheaper.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing strength reduction with constant folding. Folding needs compile-time constants; strength reduction works on run-time values.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q18", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following are loop optimizations? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Loop-invariant code motion.", "Induction variable elimination.", "Loop unrolling.", "Register allocation by graph colouring."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Loop optimizations exploit the repeated execution of a loop body: hoisting invariants, simplifying induction variables, or trading code size for fewer branch and index operations.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Loop-invariant code motion moves a computation that does not change inside the loop to just before it. Option (A) is a loop optimization.</li><li>Induction variable elimination replaces a variable that steps by a constant with a simpler recurrence or removes it entirely. Option (B) is a loop optimization.</li><li>Loop unrolling replicates the body to cut the loop-control overhead. Option (C) is a loop optimization.</li><li>Graph colouring is a global register-allocation technique; it applies to the whole procedure and is not specific to loops. Option (D) is not.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (C)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating every optimization that happens to help a loop as a loop optimization. Graph colouring and dead code elimination are global or local, not loop-specific.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q19", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "In the loop while (i < n) { s = s + a * b; i = i + 1; }, where a and b are not modified anywhere in the loop, which statement is TRUE?", "options": ["a * b is loop-invariant and may be computed once before the loop, provided the body is known to execute at least once or the computation has no side effect.", "a * b cannot be moved out of the loop, because it appears inside a loop.", "Hoisting a * b changes the number of times s is updated.", "i = i + 1 is loop-invariant and can be hoisted out of the loop."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Loop-invariant code motion hoists a computation whose operands do not change in the loop, subject to the usual safety conditions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">a</code> and <code class=\"mm\">b</code> are not assigned in the loop, so <code class=\"mm\">a * b</code> has the same value on every iteration — it is loop-invariant.</li><li>Computing it once before the loop removes repeated work and cannot change the value of <code class=\"mm\">s</code>.</li><li>The one caveat is that hoisting executes the computation even when the loop body runs zero times; that is safe for a pure multiplication but not for an operation that may trap or has side effects.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">i = i + 1</code> changes every iteration, so it is the opposite of invariant.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Hoisting an invariant division or a dereference without checking. Hoisting can introduce a fault on a path that never executed the body.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q20", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "A variable is live at a point in a program if", "options": ["it appears somewhere in the program text", "its value may be used before being redefined along some path starting at that point", "it is declared at the top of the procedure", "it is currently held in a register"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Liveness is about future use: a variable is live where its current value may still be read before being overwritten.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The definition quantifies over paths that start at the point in question.</li><li>Along at least one such path the current value reaches a use before any redefinition, so the value must be preserved.</li><li>If every path redefines the variable before any use, the current value is dead there.</li><li>Mentioning a name in the source, declaring it, or holding it in a register says nothing about whether the current value will be read.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Defining liveness as 'appears in the program'. A variable can appear many times and still be dead at a given point because it is overwritten first.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q21", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Live variable analysis is a ____ data-flow problem.", "options": ["forward, must", "forward, may", "backward, may", "backward, must"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Liveness flows information from later points to earlier ones, and a variable counts as live if it is live along any one path.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Whether a variable is live at a point depends on what happens after that point, so the equations are solved backwards from the exit.</li><li>The transfer is <code class=\"mm\">live_in = use ∪ (live_out − def)</code>, and the meet over predecessors' successors is set union.</li><li>Union is a may-operator: live on some path is enough.</li><li>Hence backward, may.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling it must because it is backward. Reaching definitions is forward/may and available expressions is forward/must; liveness is backward/may.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q22", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Consider the block below. Assume that z is live on exit from the block and that no other variable is. 1: x = 1 2: y = x + 2 3: x = 3 4: z = y How many variables are live immediately before instruction 3? (Enter an integer.)", "options": [], "answer_display": "1", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Work backwards: a variable is live before a statement if it is used by that statement, or live after it and not defined by it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">z</code> is live on exit and is defined at instruction 4, so before instruction 4 the live set is {y} — <code class=\"mm\">y</code> is used there.</li><li>Instruction 3 defines <code class=\"mm\">x</code>. The value of <code class=\"mm\">x</code> from instruction 1 is not used at instruction 3 or 4, so <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is not live after instruction 2 either.</li><li>Before instruction 3 the live set is therefore {y}: <code class=\"mm\">y</code> will be read at instruction 4, and nothing kills it at instruction 3.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">x</code> is not in the set, because instruction 3 overwrites it before any use. Count = 1.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 1</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Answering 2 by keeping <code class=\"mm\">x</code> live. A variable whose next action on every path is a redefinition is dead, whatever its earlier uses were.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q23", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Which of the following statements about classical data-flow analyses are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Reaching definitions is a forward, may analysis.", "Live variable analysis is a backward, may analysis.", "Available expressions is a forward, must analysis.", "Very busy expressions is a forward, must analysis."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Each classical analysis is fixed by two choices: the direction information flows, and whether the meet is union (may) or intersection (must).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Reaching definitions asks which definitions may reach a point, propagating forwards and taking union. Option (A) is true.</li><li>Liveness propagates backwards and takes union. Option (B) is true.</li><li>Available expressions asks which expressions are computed on every path, propagating forwards and intersecting. Option (C) is true.</li><li>Very busy expressions asks which expressions will be computed on every future path — it propagates backwards, and the meet is intersection. Option (D) has the wrong direction.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (C)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming 'must' always pairs with 'forward'. Very busy expressions is backward and must; the two choices are independent.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q24", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "For which of the following is liveness information used? (Select all that apply.)", "options": ["Register allocation, since variables that are simultaneously live cannot share a register.", "Dead code elimination, since an assignment to a variable that is not live afterwards can be removed.", "Directly identifying the loop-invariant computations of a loop.", "Propagating information backwards through the flow graph, since liveness on exit from a block is needed to compute liveness on entry."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Liveness answers 'is this value still needed?', which drives both register pressure and the removal of useless definitions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Two variables live at the same point interfere, so they need different registers; this is the basis of the interference graph. Option (A) is a use.</li><li>An assignment whose target is not live afterwards computes a value nobody reads, so it is dead. Option (B) is a use.</li><li>Loop-invariance is a property of the operands' definitions relative to the loop, not of liveness. Option (C) is not a use.</li><li>Liveness is itself defined by a backward system of equations in which a block's live-out feeds its live-in. Option (D) is a use.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (D)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Expecting liveness to find loop invariants. Invariance is decided by asking whether a definition lies outside the loop; liveness says nothing about where a definition is.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q25", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-29", "text": "Assume that only t is live on exit from the block below. 1: p = q * r 2: p = p + 1 3: t = p 4: p = 0 Which of the following is TRUE?", "options": ["Line 4 is dead and can be deleted; lines 1, 2 and 3 must all be kept.", "Lines 2 and 4 are dead and can be deleted.", "Line 1 is dead, because p is redefined at line 2.", "No statement is dead, because every line assigns something."], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A definition is dead when the variable it defines is not live afterwards; here only <code class=\"mm\">t</code> is live on exit.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li><code class=\"mm\">p</code> is not live on exit and nothing after line 4 reads it, so line 4 defines a value nobody uses. It is dead.</li><li>Line 1 defines <code class=\"mm\">p</code>, which line 2 reads on its right-hand side, so line 1 is live.</li><li>Line 2 produces the <code class=\"mm\">p</code> that line 3 reads, so line 2 is live; line 3 produces <code class=\"mm\">t</code>, which is live on exit.</li><li>Deleting line 1 would change the value of <code class=\"mm\">t</code>, so option (C) is wrong despite the redefinition at line 2.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A)</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Deleting line 1 because <code class=\"mm\">p</code> is reassigned at line 2. The reassignment *reads* <code class=\"mm\">p</code>, so the earlier value is used.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q26", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In the run-time environment of a language that allows nested procedures, the <b>display</b> is</p>", "options": ["the set of registers reserved for passing actual parameters", "the table of free blocks maintained by the heap manager", "a vector whose <i>d</i>-th entry points to the most recent activation of the procedure at nesting depth <i>d</i>", "the stack of return addresses saved by the call instruction"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The display is an array indexed by lexical nesting depth. Entry <i>d</i> holds a pointer to the activation record of the most recent activation of the procedure declared at depth <i>d</i>, so a reference to a nonlocal variable costs one array access instead of a walk along the access-link chain.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A display is indexed by nesting depth, not by call order and not by parameter position.</li><li>Looking up depth <i>d</i> gives the frame of the current activation of the procedure at that depth, which is exactly the frame that lexically encloses the reference.</li><li>Options (A), (B) and (D) describe parameter registers, the heap free list and the return-address stack; none of them is indexed by nesting depth.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C) &mdash; a depth-indexed vector of frame pointers.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading &ldquo;display&rdquo; as the hardware frame buffer or as the parameter area. In compiler terminology it is a run-time data structure for nonlocal access.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Access links are per frame; a display is per depth. Both reach the same frames, and the display buys O(1) access at the cost of save and restore on every call.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q27", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Procedure <code class=\"mm\">P</code> sits at lexical nesting depth 3 and refers to a variable declared in the procedure at lexical nesting depth 1. With access (static) links and no display, how many links must the code follow from the activation record of <code class=\"mm\">P</code> to reach the record that holds that variable?</p>", "options": ["one link", "two links", "three links", "none: the compiler turns it into a static address"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An access link points to the activation record of the procedure that lexically encloses the current one. Following one access link moves outward by exactly one lexical level, so the number of links to follow is the difference in nesting depth.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Nesting depth of <code class=\"mm\">P</code> = 3; depth of the declaring procedure = 1.</li><li>Difference = 3 &minus; 1 = 2, so two links are followed: depth 3 &rarr; depth 2 &rarr; depth 1.</li><li>One link would land on the depth-2 frame, and three links would leave the lexical chain altogether.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) &mdash; two links.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Following the <i>control</i> link instead, which walks the call chain and may arrive at a frame that does not lexically enclose <code class=\"mm\">P</code> at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Access link = one lexical level. Nesting-depth difference = number of hops.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q28", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the calling sequence of a procedure are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["The caller evaluates the actual parameters and places them where the callee can find them.", "The return address that is saved is the address of the instruction that follows the call in the caller.", "The callee saves the registers it is about to overwrite and restores them before it returns.", "The caller must decrement the stack pointer by exactly the size of the callee&rsquo;s activation record before transferring control."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The calling sequence is a division of labour: the caller makes the arguments available and records where to resume, and the callee saves whatever it will clobber, allocates its own frame and initialises its own data.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Arguments are evaluated in the caller&rsquo;s context, because only the caller can see them.</li><li>(B) True. Execution must resume at the instruction after the call, so that address is the one saved.</li><li>(C) True. The callee knows which registers it will overwrite; saving and restoring them is its job.</li><li>(D) False. The caller may only save its own state and push arguments; the callee allocates the rest of its frame, which is why the frame size need not be fixed before the call.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming the caller must know the full frame size. That is why the frame layout is fixed by the compiler, not computed by the caller at run time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Sort every step of a call by who performs it: caller supplies arguments and return point, callee saves registers, allocates and initialises.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q29", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A recursive procedure uses 48 bytes per activation record. During a run the deepest recursion reaches 20 simultaneous activations, and the run-time system reserves 512 bytes of fixed overhead at the base of the stack. The run-time stack is 16384 bytes. How many bytes of the stack remain unused at the deepest point? (Enter an integer.)</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "14912", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Stack usage is the fixed base overhead plus one frame per live activation; the unused space is the stack size minus that total.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Frames in use at the deepest point = 20, so frame space = 20 &times; 48 = 960 bytes.</li><li>Total in use = 512 + 960 = 1472 bytes.</li><li>Unused = 16384 &minus; 1472 = <b>14912</b> bytes.</li><li>Check: 14912 + 1472 = 16384, which is the whole stack.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 14912 bytes.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting 19 frames by assuming the outermost call does not need one, or forgetting the 512-byte base overhead. Both errors inflate the answer.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Stack cost = fixed overhead + depth &times; frame size. Compute the frames in use first, then subtract from the stack size.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q30", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Compilers usually keep a separate frame pointer (FP) as well as a stack pointer (SP). The reason is that</p>", "options": ["SP moves whenever temporaries are pushed or popped, whereas FP stays fixed for the whole activation, so parameters and locals keep stable offsets", "FP can be read faster than SP by the processor", "FP holds the base address of the heap segment", "the call instruction saves the frame pointer but not the stack pointer"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Locals and parameters are addressed as fixed offsets from a base register. If that base were SP, every push and pop of a temporary would shift every offset, so the compiler would have to track the depth of the expression stack.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A frame pointer is set once, on entry, to a fixed point in the frame and restored on return.</li><li>SP is free to move as temporaries are evaluated, and the offsets from FP never change.</li><li>That stability is what lets a single offset serve a variable for the whole activation, including inside expressions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A) &mdash; FP gives stable offsets while SP moves.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Thinking FP exists for speed. It exists for addressing stability, and an optimising compiler often omits it when no variable-length frame is needed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> FP = fixed base for offsets; SP = moving top of the expression stack.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q31", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "activation-records-call-stack", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A nested procedure creates a closure that captures a local variable of the enclosing procedure <b>by reference</b>, and the closure is called after the enclosing procedure has returned. What must the compiler do?</p>", "options": ["leave the variable in its stack frame and simply never reuse that part of the stack", "copy the variable into the closure&rsquo;s own activation record when the closure is created", "keep the variable in a callee-saved register that the closure also uses", "allocate the captured variable in the heap, or heap-allocate the whole frame, so that it outlives the call"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A stack frame dies when its procedure returns, so any object that must outlive the call has to live somewhere else. Capturing by reference means the closure and the enclosing code must share one storage location, so the location itself must survive the return.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The lifetime required of the captured variable exceeds the lifetime of the frame, which is the defining reason for heap allocation.</li><li>Copying it into the closure (option B) gives the closure a private copy: later writes by the enclosing code, or by a second closure, no longer reach it, so by-reference semantics break.</li><li>A register (option C) cannot be shared safely either; the register is reused by later calls.</li><li>Leaving the frame unreclaimed (option A) leaks stack space without bound and is not what an implementation can guarantee.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D) &mdash; the captured variable must live in the heap (or the frame must be heap-allocated).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Treating a closure capture as a copy. Copying is correct for capture by value and wrong for capture by reference; the two semantics are not interchangeable.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Lifetime, not scope, decides storage. Anything that outlives its activation escapes the stack.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q32", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In a flow graph with entry node <code class=\"mm\">s</code>, node <code class=\"mm\">B1</code> <b>dominates</b> node <code class=\"mm\">B2</code> if</p>", "options": ["every path from <code class=\"mm\">s</code> to <code class=\"mm\">B2</code> passes through <code class=\"mm\">B1</code>", "at least one path from <code class=\"mm\">s</code> to <code class=\"mm\">B2</code> passes through <code class=\"mm\">B1</code>", "<code class=\"mm\">B1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">B2</code> lie on a common cycle", "<code class=\"mm\">B1</code> appears before <code class=\"mm\">B2</code> in the source text"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Dominance is a universal statement about paths: a node is unavoidable on the way to another node.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Dominance asks whether control can reach <code class=\"mm\">B2</code> while avoiding <code class=\"mm\">B1</code>. If it cannot, <code class=\"mm\">B1</code> dominates.</li><li>&ldquo;Every path&rdquo; is therefore the definition; &ldquo;some path&rdquo; (option B) is far too weak.</li><li>Sharing a cycle (option C) or appearing earlier in the text (option D) says nothing about unavoidability; a branch can bypass either one.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A) &mdash; every path from the entry to B2 passes through B1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing dominance with reachability or with textual order. Dominance is about all paths, not one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Dominance is the foundation of loop detection, of the SSA &phi;-placement rule and of every &ldquo;this must have executed&rdquo; argument.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q33", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about basic blocks and flow graphs are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["Two instructions in the same basic block may be interchanged only if neither uses a value that the other defines.", "Control can enter a basic block only at its first instruction.", "Every basic block must end with a conditional or unconditional jump.", "If a block has two predecessors, then the last instruction of each predecessor must be a jump."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A basic block is a maximal straight-line sequence: one entry, one exit. That structure makes local optimisation possible, but it does not make the instructions freely reorderable, and it does not force every block to end in a jump.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. Reordering two instructions that carry a dependence changes the values computed; independence is the precondition for any reordering.</li><li>(B) True. That is the definition of a block: a leader is the only entry point.</li><li>(C) False. A block may end with an ordinary instruction and fall through to the next block, which is a leader because it follows a jump elsewhere.</li><li>(D) False. One of the two predecessors may reach the block by falling through, with no jump at all.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A) and (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing that every edge of a flow graph is an explicit jump. A fall-through edge is an edge with no jump instruction behind it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Straight-line entry and exit, not universal jumps, is what defines a basic block.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q34", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about <b>reducible</b> flow graphs is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["Every flow graph produced by a structured program is irreducible.", "A flow graph is reducible exactly when it can be reduced to a single node by the T1 and T2 transformations, and then every retreating edge in a depth-first ordering is a back edge.", "A flow graph that contains a loop cannot be reducible.", "Reducibility guarantees that every block has at most one predecessor."], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Reducibility is the property that all loops in the graph are entered through a single header. It has three equivalent characterisations: reducibility by the T1 and T2 transformations, every retreating edge being a back edge, and every cycle having a node that dominates the rest of the cycle.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>T1 removes a self-loop and T2 collapses a node with a single predecessor; a graph is reducible when repeated application reduces it to one node.</li><li>Under that condition every retreating edge in a depth-first ordering turns out to be a back edge, which is the second equivalent form.</li><li>Structured code (if, while, for) always produces reducible graphs, so option (A) reverses the truth, and having a loop (option C) is exactly what reducibility talks about.</li><li>Reducibility says nothing about the in-degree of a block, so option (D) is unrelated.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading &ldquo;reducible&rdquo; as &ldquo;can be simplified at all&rdquo;. Irreducible graphs exist &mdash; two entries into one loop body &mdash; and they are what makes loop analysis hard.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> One header per loop is the practical content of reducibility; goto-based spaghetti is what breaks it.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q35", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the three-address code below, where <code class=\"mm\">L1</code>, <code class=\"mm\">L2</code>, <code class=\"mm\">L3</code> and <code class=\"mm\">L4</code> are labels.<br/><code class=\"mm\">i = 0</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">L1: if i >= 10 goto L4</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">t = a[i]</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">if t == 0 goto L3</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">s = s + t</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">goto L2</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">L2: i = i + 1</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">goto L1</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">L3: c = c + 1</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">L4: d = s + c</code><br/>How many basic blocks does this code contain? (Enter an integer.)</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "7", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A basic block starts at a leader. The leaders are the first instruction, every label that is the target of a jump, and every instruction that immediately follows a jump. Everything between one leader and the next belongs to the same block.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Leader 1: <code class=\"mm\">i = 0</code>, the first instruction.</li><li>Leader 2: <code class=\"mm\">L1: if i &gt;= 10 goto L4</code>, a jump target.</li><li>Leader 3: <code class=\"mm\">t = a[i]</code>, which immediately follows the conditional jump at <code class=\"mm\">L1</code>.</li><li>The line <code class=\"mm\">if t == 0 goto L3</code> follows an ordinary assignment, not a jump, so it is <i>not</i> a leader: it stays inside the block that began at <code class=\"mm\">t = a[i]</code>.</li><li>Leader 4: <code class=\"mm\">s = s + t</code>, which immediately follows the conditional jump.</li><li>Leader 5: <code class=\"mm\">L2: i = i + 1</code>, a jump target and also the line after <code class=\"mm\">goto L2</code>.</li><li>Leader 6: <code class=\"mm\">L3: c = c + 1</code>, a jump target.</li><li>Leader 7: <code class=\"mm\">L4: d = s + c</code>, a jump target.</li><li>The blocks are therefore <code class=\"mm\">[i = 0]</code>, <code class=\"mm\">[L1: if i &gt;= 10 goto L4]</code>, <code class=\"mm\">[t = a[i]; if t == 0 goto L3]</code>, <code class=\"mm\">[s = s + t; goto L2]</code>, <code class=\"mm\">[L2: i = i + 1; goto L1]</code>, <code class=\"mm\">[L3: c = c + 1]</code> and <code class=\"mm\">[L4: d = s + c]</code> &mdash; <b>7</b> blocks.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 7 basic blocks.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting the conditional test <code class=\"mm\">if t == 0 goto L3</code> as the start of a block, which gives 8. A conditional jump <i>ends</i> a block; only the instruction after it begins one.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Collect leaders first, then split. Every leader starts a block, and a jump ends a block.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q36", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">The <b>immediate dominator</b> of a node <code class=\"mm\">n</code> (other than the entry node) is</p>", "options": ["the predecessor of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> that has the largest number of successors", "the node that <code class=\"mm\">n</code> dominates directly", "the dominator of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> that every other dominator of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> dominates", "the header of the outermost loop that contains <code class=\"mm\">n</code>"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The dominators of a node form a chain ordered by dominance, so exactly one member of that chain is the closest to the node. That one is the immediate dominator.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>If <code class=\"mm\">d</code> dominates <code class=\"mm\">n</code>, every dominator of <code class=\"mm\">d</code> also dominates <code class=\"mm\">n</code>, so the dominators of <code class=\"mm\">n</code> are totally ordered by dominance.</li><li>The last element of that chain, dominated by all the others, is the immediate dominator.</li><li>Option (B) reverses the relation: it is <code class=\"mm\">n</code> that dominates its own successors, not its dominator.</li><li>Options (A) and (D) describe a predecessor and a loop header, neither of which is defined by the dominance chain.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing &ldquo;dominates&rdquo; with &ldquo;is dominated by&rdquo;. The immediate dominator is above <code class=\"mm\">n</code> in the chain, not below it.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Dominators form a tree; the immediate dominator is the parent. Every control-flow question about &ldquo;the nearest unavoidable block&rdquo; is a question about that tree.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q37", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "basic-blocks-and-flow-graphs", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A <code class=\"mm\">while</code> loop whose body contains a <code class=\"mm\">break</code> statement is compiled to a flow graph. Compared with the flow graph of the same loop without the break,</p>", "options": ["one extra edge is added from the block containing the break to the block that follows the loop, and that edge is not a back edge", "the loop header acquires a second back edge", "the flow graph becomes irreducible", "the block containing the break becomes the new loop header"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A break is an additional exit from the middle of the loop body. In the flow graph it is one more edge from the break block to the loop&rsquo;s successor; the loop still has exactly one entry, so the graph stays reducible and the header is unchanged.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The loop header remains the block with the test, and the only back edge is still the one from the end of the body to the header.</li><li>The break block gains an edge straight to the block after the loop, which is a forward edge, not a back edge.</li><li>One entry and two exits is still reducible; irreducibility needs two entries into one loop, which a break does not create.</li><li>Nothing promotes the break block to header: the test block still dominates the whole body.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming any second exit from a loop body makes the graph irreducible. Irreducibility is about entries, not exits.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> When you add a construct, ask which node gains an edge and in which direction; that answer settles most flow-graph questions.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q38", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">After an assignment <code class=\"mm\">x = y</code>, replacing later uses of <code class=\"mm\">y</code> by <code class=\"mm\">x</code> inside the same basic block is called</p>", "options": ["constant folding", "dead code elimination", "loop unrolling", "copy propagation"], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Copy propagation takes an assignment of the form <code class=\"mm\">x = y</code> and rewrites the uses of <code class=\"mm\">y</code> that follow it into uses of <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, so the copy itself can often be deleted.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The assignment establishes that <code class=\"mm\">x</code> and <code class=\"mm\">y</code> hold the same value from that point on.</li><li>Every later use of <code class=\"mm\">y</code> in the block can therefore read <code class=\"mm\">x</code> instead, provided neither variable is redefined in between.</li><li>Once no use of <code class=\"mm\">y</code> remains that needs the copy, the assignment itself becomes dead and is removed.</li><li>Constant folding, dead code elimination and loop unrolling are different transformations with different preconditions.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D) &mdash; copy propagation.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Calling it common subexpression elimination. CSE removes a repeated <i>computation</i>; copy propagation removes a repeated <i>value transfer</i>.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Copy propagation usually works with dead code elimination: propagate first, then delete the copy that nothing reads.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q39", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Replicating the body of a loop <i>k</i> times inside one iteration, with the loop control adjusted to run <i>n</i>/<i>k</i> iterations, is called</p>", "options": ["loop fusion", "loop unrolling", "loop distribution", "loop inversion"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Loop unrolling replicates the body so that one iteration performs <i>k</i> copies of the work and the branch and induction-variable update are amortised over those copies.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The body is copied <i>k</i> times and the loop bound is divided by <i>k</i>.</li><li>Branch overhead per unit of work falls, and the scheduler gets a larger straight-line window.</li><li>Fusion merges two loops, distribution splits one loop into several, and inversion turns a while-loop into a do-while with a guard &mdash; none of them replicates a body.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) &mdash; loop unrolling.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Confusing unrolling with fusion. Unrolling copies the body of one loop; fusion joins two separate loops.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Unrolling trades code size for fewer branches and a bigger scheduling window.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q40", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In the loop<br/><code class=\"mm\">for (i = 0; i &lt; 100; i = i + 1) a[4 * i] = 0;</code><br/>strength reduction replaces the multiplication by an induction variable <code class=\"mm\">j</code> that starts at 0 and is increased by 4 on every iteration. What is the value of <code class=\"mm\">j</code> in the last iteration in which the loop body executes? (Enter an integer.)</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "396", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Strength reduction turns the multiplication into an addition by carrying the value 4&middot;<i>i</i> in its own induction variable, which starts at the value for <i>i</i> = 0 and is incremented by the same amount the multiplication would have produced each time.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The body executes for <i>i</i> = 0, 1, 2, &hellip;, 99, so there are 100 iterations.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">j</code> takes the values 0, 4, 8, &hellip;, one per iteration, so on the <i>k</i>-th iteration (counting from 0) it holds 4<i>k</i>.</li><li>The last iteration is <i>k</i> = 99, giving 4 &times; 99 = <b>396</b>.</li><li>Check: 4 &times; 99 = 396 and 396 + 4 = 400 = 4 &times; 100, which is the value the loop would have computed if it had run one more time.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 396.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reporting 400 by using the loop bound 100 instead of the last index 99. The body never executes for <i>i</i> = 100.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> An induction variable after strength reduction holds exactly what the original expression held; read its final value off the last iteration, not off the bound.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q41", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about local and loop optimizations are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["Constant folding replaces an expression whose operands are all known at compile time by its value.", "Two identical expressions in different basic blocks can always be merged into one computation.", "An assignment <code class=\"mm\">x = e</code> can be deleted when <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is not live after the assignment and evaluating <code class=\"mm\">e</code> has no side effect.", "A loop-invariant expression that may raise an exception can be moved out of a loop whose body may execute zero times."], "answer_display": "Options (A) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Every transformation needs a precondition. Constant folding needs compile-time constants; dead-code elimination needs the target not to be live and the right-hand side to be free of side effects; code motion needs the moved computation to be safe wherever it lands.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. If all operands are constants, the value is fixed and can be computed once by the compiler.</li><li>(B) False. A redefinition of an operand between the two blocks destroys the equality; only available expressions justify the merge.</li><li>(C) True. If the value is never used again and evaluating the right-hand side cannot trap or change state, the assignment has no observable effect.</li><li>(D) False. Hoisting a potentially trapping computation out of a loop that might execute zero times can introduce an exception the original program never raised, which changes behaviour.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A) and (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming that an unused assignment is always removable. The right-hand side can still divide by zero, call a function or write to memory.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> State the precondition with the transformation. Unconditional versions of these rules are the classic source of &ldquo;optimisations&rdquo; that change program behaviour.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q42", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">To eliminate, in block <code class=\"mm\">B3</code>, a computation of <code class=\"mm\">b + c</code> that was already performed on every path reaching <code class=\"mm\">B3</code> from block <code class=\"mm\">B1</code>, the compiler needs the data-flow information called</p>", "options": ["live variables", "very busy expressions", "available expressions", "reaching definitions"], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Global common subexpression elimination asks whether an expression is <i>available</i> at a point: whether it has been computed on every path to that point and no operand has been redefined since.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The key words are &ldquo;already performed on every path&rdquo;, which is a <i>must</i> condition along all paths &mdash; exactly the meet of an available-expressions analysis.</li><li>Available expressions is a forward, must analysis whose universe is the set of expressions in the procedure.</li><li>Live variables is backward and about values still needed; very busy expressions is backward and about computations that will be needed; reaching definitions tracks definitions, which is a may analysis and too weak for this &ldquo;every path&rdquo; guarantee.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C) &mdash; available expressions.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Choosing reaching definitions because it also mentions &ldquo;definitions&rdquo;. Reaching definitions is a may analysis, so a single path is enough to satisfy it, and that is not what CSE needs.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Every &ldquo;on every path&rdquo; phrase is a must analysis; every &ldquo;on some path&rdquo; phrase is a may analysis.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q43", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A loop with a large body is unrolled by a factor of 8. Which of the following is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["It always reduces the running time, because fewer branches are executed.", "It removes the loop-carried dependences in the body.", "It is legal only when the trip count is an exact multiple of 8.", "It reduces loop overhead but increases code size and register pressure, so it can make the program slower through instruction-cache misses and spills."], "answer_display": "Option (D)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Unrolling is a space-for-time transformation. It removes branches and induction-variable updates per unit of work, but the code grows by the unroll factor and the scheduler needs more registers to keep the widened body busy.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Per-iteration overhead (branch, compare, increment) is divided by 8, which is the gain.</li><li>The code footprint is multiplied by 8 in that loop, which competes for instruction cache and may exceed it, so the gain can be more than cancelled.</li><li>A larger straight-line body also raises register pressure; the compiler may spill, adding memory traffic.</li><li>Dependences are a property of the computation, not of how many copies of it are written out, and a trip count that is not a multiple of 8 is handled by a residual loop, not by rejecting the transformation.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (D).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming fewer branches must mean a faster loop. Branch count is one cost among several, and code size is one of the others.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Every loop transformation is a trade: name the resource it spends and the resource it saves.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q44", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "local-and-loop-optimizations", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about <b>constant propagation</b> are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["It replaces a use of a variable by a known constant when no intervening definition changes the variable.", "Finding those constants across basic blocks needs a forward, must data-flow analysis.", "It can turn a conditional branch whose test has become constant into an unconditional jump.", "It may be applied across a procedure call even when the call might assign to the variable."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Constant propagation carries the fact &ldquo;this variable has this constant value here&rdquo; through the flow graph. The fact is true on every path or it is not usable, which is why the analysis is forward and must.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. That is the transformation itself: substitute the known value at the use.</li><li>(B) True. The constant must hold on every path reaching the point, so the meet is intersection and the direction is forward along the flow graph.</li><li>(C) True. Once the test is constant, one arm is unreachable and the branch becomes a jump; this is where constant propagation feeds unreachable-code elimination.</li><li>(D) False. A call may assign to a global or to a variable it can reach through a pointer, so the constant is no longer guaranteed after the call.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Assuming a variable keeps its constant across a call. Calls are the standard kill point for propagated facts.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Constant propagation and constant folding are partners: propagation exposes constants at the use, folding computes with them.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q45", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">In live-variable analysis, the set of variables live on <b>exit</b> from a basic block is</p>", "options": ["the intersection of the variables live on entry to its successors", "the union of the variables live on entry to its successors", "the variables both used and defined in the block", "the variables that the block defines and never uses again"], "answer_display": "Option (B)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Liveness is a may property: a variable is live if some continuation of the execution uses it before redefining it. Because a block can be followed by any one of its successors, the exit set is the union over those successors.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The block can transfer control to any successor, so a variable that is live on entry to at least one successor is live on exit from this block.</li><li>&ldquo;At least one&rdquo; is union, and it is what makes liveness a may analysis.</li><li>An intersection would demand the variable be live on every successor, which is the must version and is not what liveness asks.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (B) &mdash; the union over successors.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Using intersection because liveness feels like a &ldquo;must&rdquo; property. It is existential: one path that uses the value is enough.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Backward + may = union over successors. Write the direction and the meet operator down before touching the equations.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q46", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Assume that only <code class=\"mm\">z</code> is live on exit from the block below.<br/><code class=\"mm\">1: x = a + b</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">2: y = c * d</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">3: x = y + 1</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">4: z = x * 2</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">5: w = y</code><br/>How many of the five assignments are dead, that is, their result is never used? (Enter an integer.)</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "2", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> An assignment is dead when the variable it defines is not live immediately after it. Liveness is computed backwards from the exit set, so the block is scanned from line 5 up to line 1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>Live after line 5 = {<code class=\"mm\">z</code>}. Line 5 defines <code class=\"mm\">w</code>, which is not live after it, so line 5 is dead.</li><li>Live after line 4 = {<code class=\"mm\">z</code>}; line 4 defines <code class=\"mm\">z</code>, which is live, so line 4 stays.</li><li>Live before line 4 = {<code class=\"mm\">x</code>, <code class=\"mm\">z</code>}; line 3 defines <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, which is live, so line 3 stays.</li><li>Live before line 3 = {<code class=\"mm\">y</code>}; line 2 defines <code class=\"mm\">y</code>, which is live, so line 2 stays.</li><li>Live before line 2 = {<code class=\"mm\">c</code>, <code class=\"mm\">d</code>}; line 1 defines <code class=\"mm\">x</code>, which is <i>not</i> live after line 1 (line 3 redefines <code class=\"mm\">x</code> before any use), so line 1 is dead.</li><li>Dead assignments: lines 1 and 5, that is <b>2</b>.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 2 dead assignments (lines 1 and 5).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Keeping line 1 because <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is used at line 3. The use at line 3 reads the value defined at line 3&rsquo;s own right-hand side, not the value from line 1.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Walk backwards and keep the live set in hand. An assignment dies the moment its target is not in the live set just after it.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q47", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "MSQ", "difficulty": "medium", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Which of the following statements about the iterative data-flow framework are <b>TRUE</b>? (Select all that apply.)</p>", "options": ["The meet operator of a must analysis is set intersection.", "Monotone transfer functions are what guarantee that the iterative algorithm reaches a fixed point.", "A forward analysis converges fastest when the blocks are visited in reverse postorder.", "A taller lattice cannot change the number of iterations the algorithm needs."], "answer_display": "Options (A), (B) and (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The iterative framework rests on three facts: a lattice with a meet operator, monotone transfer functions, and an iteration order. Monotonicity gives termination; the order only affects speed.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>(A) True. A must property holds only if it holds on every path, and every path means intersection.</li><li>(B) True. Monotonicity ensures the values only move one way along the lattice, so the process cannot oscillate and must stop at a fixed point.</li><li>(C) True. Reverse postorder visits a block after most of its predecessors, so information propagates far in one pass and fewer passes are needed.</li><li>(D) False. The height of the lattice bounds the number of times a value can change, so a taller lattice can require more iterations.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Options (A), (B) and (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Believing the iteration order affects the answer. It affects only the number of passes; the fixed point reached is the same.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Monotonicity gives correctness and termination; the visiting order gives speed. Keep the two arguments separate.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q48", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "easy", "source": "STANDARD_TEXTBOOK", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">For live-variable analysis, the <b>gen</b> and <b>kill</b> sets of a basic block are</p>", "options": ["gen = the variables used in the block before any redefinition in it; kill = the variables defined in the block", "gen = the variables defined in the block; kill = the variables used in the block", "gen = every variable that appears in the block; kill = the empty set", "gen = the empty set; kill = every variable that appears in the block"], "answer_display": "Option (A)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> The transfer function of a block for liveness is <code class=\"mm\">live_in = use &cup; (live_out &minus; def)</code>. The <code class=\"mm\">use</code> set &mdash; uses reached before any definition in the block &mdash; is what the block generates, and the definitions are what it kills.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>A variable used before it is redefined in the block is live on entry whatever the exit set says, so it is generated.</li><li>A variable defined in the block is killed: an earlier value of it cannot be needed beyond the block, because the definition overwrites it.</li><li>Options (B), (C) and (D) get the two roles backwards or throw the distinction away.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (A).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Generating every variable that appears in the block. A use that comes <i>after</i> a definition in the same block reads the new value, so it does not make the old value live.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> gen = used before defined; kill = defined. That pair is the whole transfer function for liveness.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q49", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "NAT", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">Consider the code below, where <code class=\"mm\">L1</code> and <code class=\"mm\">L4</code> are labels.<br/><code class=\"mm\">1: i = 0</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">2: s = 0</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">L1: 3: if i >= n goto L4</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">4: s = s + i</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">5: u = s * 2</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">6: i = i + 1</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">7: u = 0</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">8: goto L1</code><br/><code class=\"mm\">L4: 9: r = s</code><br/>How many variables are live on entry to the block that begins at label <code class=\"mm\">L1</code>? (Enter an integer.)</p>", "options": [], "answer_display": "3", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> A variable is live on entry to the loop header if some execution starting there uses its current value before redefining it. Redefinitions inside the loop remove variables from the set; uses keep them in.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>The block at <code class=\"mm\">L1</code> tests <code class=\"mm\">i</code> and <code class=\"mm\">n</code>, so both are live on entry.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">s</code> is read at line 4 (<code class=\"mm\">s = s + i</code>) and again at line 9 (<code class=\"mm\">r = s</code>), so its incoming value is used and it is live.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">u</code> is written at line 5 but overwritten at line 7 before any use, so the value written at line 5 is never read; <code class=\"mm\">u</code> is not live on entry to the loop.</li><li><code class=\"mm\">r</code> is defined at line 9 and never read, so it is not live either.</li><li>The live set is {<code class=\"mm\">i</code>, <code class=\"mm\">n</code>, <code class=\"mm\">s</code>}, which has <b>3</b> variables.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> 3 variables (i, n and s).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Counting <code class=\"mm\">u</code> because it appears twice in the loop. A definition that is overwritten before any use generates no liveness at the loop header.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> Ask of each variable: is the value it holds on entry to the loop actually read before being overwritten? Only those count.</p>"}, {"id": "CD-U5-Q50", "subject": "compiler-design", "unit": 5, "unit_slug": "runtime-code-optimization", "subtopic": "data-flow-analysis-liveness", "type": "MCQ", "difficulty": "hard", "source": "ORIGINAL_SYNTHESIS", "date": "2026-10-30", "text": "<p class=\"qtext\">A variable <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is <b>live</b> at a point <code class=\"mm\">p</code>. Which of the following statements is TRUE?</p>", "options": ["On every path from <code class=\"mm\">p</code> the variable <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is used before it is redefined.", "On every path from <code class=\"mm\">p</code> the variable <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is redefined.", "There is at least one path from <code class=\"mm\">p</code> to a use of <code class=\"mm\">x</code> on which <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is not redefined first.", "The variable <code class=\"mm\">x</code> is used at the point <code class=\"mm\">p</code> itself."], "answer_display": "Option (C)", "solution_html": "<h4>Detailed solution</h4><p class=\"row\"><b>Concept.</b> Liveness is existential over paths. A variable is live at a point when some continuation uses its current value before overwriting it; it does not have to be used on every continuation, and it need not be used at that point at all.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Step by step.</b></p><ol><li>&ldquo;Live&rdquo; is defined as: there exists a path from the point to a use, with no definition of the variable in between. That is option (C) verbatim.</li><li>Option (A) strengthens &ldquo;some path&rdquo; to &ldquo;every path&rdquo;, which would make liveness a must property and would discard most of the information register allocation relies on.</li><li>Option (B) is not implied at all; a variable can be live forever and never redefined.</li><li>Option (D) is too strong: liveness is about a future use, not a use at the point itself.</li></ol><p class=\"row\"><b>Final answer.</b> Option (C).</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Common misconception.</b> Reading liveness as &ldquo;used on all paths&rdquo;. That universal reading is a different and much rarer property, and it would make almost no variable live after a branch.</p><p class=\"row\"><b>Takeaway.</b> For every data-flow fact, write down its quantifier: some path (may, union) or every path (must, intersection).</p>"}];
